{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789817810689,"sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_edf5cc0cec174ab983390475","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt","limit":400},"output":"<path>C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ===== PAGE 1 =====\n2: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n3: Page 1 of 43\n4: SAI 1 — SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE AND INTEGRATION 1\n5: (ITNET312-T)\n6: COMPLETE EXAM REVIEWER — LESSONS L2 to L8\n7: Source: SAI Review Reference · Prof. Pops Madriaga · 3rd Year 1st Sem · BSIT-S-3A\n8: Lessons covered: L2 – Enterprise Architecture Components · L3 – Developing an Enterprise Architecture · L4 – Using an\n9: Enterprise Architecture · L5 – All About SOA · L6 – Integrating Technology · L7 – Integrating Applications · L8 – Enterprise\n10: Integration\n11: \n12: ===== PAGE 2 =====\n13: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n14: Page 2 of 43\n15: L2: ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE COMPONENTS\n16: MUST MEMORIZE — Key Terms & Definitions\n17: EA Implementation Methodology — a detailed, step-by-step approach that describes how an Enterprise Architecture program\n18: is established, developed, managed, maintained, and used within an organization. It provides a clear approach for building and\n19: managing an effective EA program.\n20: Importance of a Structured Implementation Methodology — helps an organization establish and manage its EA program in a\n21: clear, consistent, and coordinated way.\n22: Key Benefits (6)\n23: 1. Clear Direction — defines the EA's scope, goals, and overall approach.\n24: 2. Better Governance — establishes how EA decisions are managed and coordinated.\n25: 3. Organized Documentation — identifies what EA information and artifacts need to be documented.\n26: 4. Stakeholder Alignment — helps stakeholders understand the EA's purpose, value, and direction.\n27: 5. Better Decision-Making — provides organized EA information that supports planning and decisions.\n28: 6. Continuous Improvement — supports regular updates and maintenance as the organization changes.\n29: EA Framework — a structured set of tools, models, and guidelines used to organize and design an enterprise's architecture. It\n30: provides a common language and structure for planning, building, and managing IT and business systems, and helps ensure\n31: consistency across all levels of the organization.\n32: Why organizations need a framework\n33: • Reduces complexity and miscommunication\n34: • Aligns business goals with IT strategy\n35: • Improves decision-making and resource planning\n36: • Provides consistency, especially in large or growing organizations\n37: The 4 Phases of EA Implementation (LIST TO MEMORIZE)\n38: 1. PHASE I — EA Program Establishment — establish the EA program and leadership; define the implementation\n39: methodology; establish governance; communicate the EA vision and gain stakeholder support.\n40: 2. PHASE II — Framework and Tool Selection — select the EA documentation framework; identify Lines of Business and\n41: Crosscuts; identify EA components to document; select documentation methods and tools; establish an EA repository.\n42: 3. PHASE III — Documentation of the EA — evaluate existing documentation; document the current state; develop future\n43: operating scenarios; define future planning assumptions; document the future state; develop the EA Management Plan.\n44: 4. PHASE IV — Use and Maintain the EA — use EA documentation for planning and decision-making; regularly update\n45: current and future views; maintain the EA repository and tools; update the EA Management Plan.\n46: Application of EA Implementation Methodology (Example)\n47: Situation: a planning team needs to identify improvements in information exchange within a Line of Business.\n48: Using the EA Repository: access current and future views of the information flow; review related business services, applications,\n49: and networks; display the EA diagrams and information during the meeting.\n50: Result: faster communication of ideas, better understanding among stakeholders, fewer interpretation errors, more productive\n51: discussions, better-informed decisions.\n52: Benefits and Challenges (MEMORIZE)\n53: Benefits: Clearer Direction, Better Governance, Improved Understanding, Better Communication, More Coordinated Planning,\n54: Continuous Improvement.\n55: Challenges: Complexity, Governance Difficulties, Requirements Gaps, Implementation Problems, Maintenance Issues.\n56: METHODOLOGY vs FRAMEWORK (COMPARISON)\n57: \n58: ===== PAGE 3 =====\n59: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n60: Page 3 of 43\n61: Aspect\n62: EA Implementation Methodology\n63: EA Framework\n64: Question it answers\n65: HOW the EA program is established and\n66: managed\n67: WHAT STRUCTURE is used to organize\n68: the EA\n69: Focus\n70: Defines processes, steps, and activities\n71: Organizes architecture information\n72: Role\n73: Guides development, documentation, and\n74: maintenance\n75: Defines the scope and relationships of\n76: architecture areas\n77: Includes\n78: Framework and tool selection\n79: Provides the structure used for\n80: documenting the EA\n81: ZACHMAN FRAMEWORK\n82: Zachman Framework — developed by John Zachman in 1987; one of the earliest and most influential EA frameworks. It is not\n83: a methodology — it is a classification schema (a way of organizing and categorizing architectural artifacts). Often compared\n84: to blueprints used in other engineering disciplines.\n85: Structure (6x6 matrix, 36 cells total)\n86: • 6 Columns (Focus / Interrogatives): What, How, Where, Who, When, Why\n87: • 6 Rows (Perspectives): Planner, Owner, Designer, Builder, Sub-Contractor, Enterprise (Actual System)\n88: • Each cell represents a specific artifact or model relevant to that intersection.\n89: How it is used / Key takeaways\n90: • Helps organizations ensure completeness — no perspective or aspect is overlooked.\n91: • Used as a reference/checklist, not a step-by-step process.\n92: • Encourages clear communication between different stakeholders (executives, designers, technical teams).\n93: • Still influences modern frameworks like TOGAF today.\n94: TOGAF (THE OPEN GROUP ARCHITECTURE FRAMEWORK)\n95: TOGAF — an Enterprise Architecture framework used to help organizations design, plan, implement, and manage their\n96: enterprise architecture. It provides a structured approach for developing architectures and is widely used to align business goals\n97: with IT systems and technology.\n98: Purpose of TOGAF (LIST)\n99: • Align IT with business objectives\n100: • Plan changes to systems and technology\n101: • Identify gaps between the current and desired architecture\n102: • Reduce duplication and unnecessary technology\n103: • Improve communication between business and IT teams\n104: • Manage architecture changes in a structured way\n105: Example: a company wants to move from manual processes to an online system — TOGAF helps analyze the current situation,\n106: design the target system, and plan how to transition.\n107: ADM — Architecture Development Method\n108: The ADM helps organizations (6 steps):\n109: 1. Identify business requirements\n110: 2. Analyze the current architecture\n111: 3. Design the target architecture\n112: 4. Identify the changes needed\n113: 5. Plan implementation\n114: 6. Manage and review the architecture\n115: TOGAF ADM CYCLE (MEMORIZE)\n116: \n117: ===== PAGE 4 =====\n118: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n119: Page 4 of 43\n120: Phase\n121: Key Focus\n122: Preliminary Phase — Preparation\n123: Establish EA principles and governance; define roles,\n124: responsibilities, and processes\n125: Phase A — Architecture Vision\n126: Define goals and scope; identify stakeholders; create the overall\n127: architecture vision\n128: Phase B — Business Architecture\n129: Define business goals, processes, and organization\n130: Phase C — Information Systems Architecture\n131: Data: identify and organize required data; Applications: define\n132: systems and how they interact\n133: Phase D — Technology Architecture\n134: Define infrastructure, networks, platforms, and technologies\n135: Phase E — Opportunities & Solutions\n136: Identify possible solutions; group changes into projects; define\n137: implementation approach\n138: Phase F — Migration Planning\n139: Prioritize projects; create migration roadmap; consider resources,\n140: costs, risks, and timelines\n141: Phase G — Implementation Governance\n142: Monitor implementation; check architecture compliance; manage\n143: implementation issues\n144: Phase H — Architecture Change Management\n145: Monitor business and technology changes; update the architecture\n146: when needed; support continuous improvement\n147: Requirements Management\n148: Operates throughout all ADM phases; tracks, evaluates, and\n149: manages changing requirements\n150: FEAF (FEDERAL ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE FRAMEWORK)\n151: FEAF — a framework developed by the U.S. Federal Government to organize and manage enterprise architecture. Provides a\n152: common structure for analyzing the current organization and designing the desired future state. Helps government agencies align\n153: business goals, processes, information, and technology. Supports standardization, integration, and better decision-making across\n154: agencies.\n155: FEAF Reference Models (MEMORIZE the 6 abbreviations + purpose)\n156: Model\n157: Purpose\n158: PRM — Performance Reference Model\n159: Measures how well an organization and its IT systems achieve\n160: strategic goals and desired outcomes\n161: DRM — Data Reference Model\n162: Defines how data is categorized, described, shared, and managed\n163: across the enterprise\n164: SRM — Security Reference Model\n165: Defines security-related capabilities, controls, and requirements for\n166: protecting enterprise systems and information\n167: BRM — Business Reference Model\n168: Describes the organization's business functions, services, and\n169: activities\n170: ARM — Application Reference Model\n171: Organizes IT services and application components that support\n172: business functions\n173: TRM — Technical Reference Model\n174: Provides a common structure for technologies, platforms, and\n175: technical standards used by the enterprise\n176: GARTNER EA METHODOLOGY\n177: Gartner EA Methodology — developed by Gartner, a research and advisory organization. Focuses on business strategy,\n178: stakeholders, and business outcomes rather than technology alone. Helps organizations understand their current state and\n179: define their future state. Emphasizes continuous communication between business and IT.\n180: KEY IDEA: Gartner views Enterprise Architecture as a continuous process of change and improvement, not a one-time\n181: project.\n182: Gartner EA Process (6 steps)\n183: \n184: ===== PAGE 5 =====\n185: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n186: Page 5 of 43\n187: 1. Understand the Business — identify business goals, strategies, and stakeholder needs.\n188: 2. Assess the Current State — examine existing processes, systems, data, and technology.\n189: 3. Define the Future State — design the desired architecture based on business objectives.\n190: 4. Identify Gaps — compare the current and future states to determine what needs to change.\n191: 5. Develop & Implement Roadmap — prioritize projects and create a practical plan for achieving the future state.\n192: 6. Continuous Improvement — monitor results and update the architecture as business needs change.\n193: CHOOSING THE RIGHT FRAMEWORK (COMPARISON TABLE)\n194: Situation\n195: Suitable Framework\n196: Need to organize and classify EA information\n197: Zachman\n198: Need a detailed EA development process\n199: TOGAF\n200: Government agency or public-sector environment\n201: FEAF\n202: Need flexibility and strong business alignment\n203: Gartner\n204: ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE COMPONENT\n205: EA is rarely managed as a single monolithic block. To make complex organizations understandable, governable, and\n206: transformable, EA divides the enterprise into distinct, interconnected domains or components. Dividing the enterprise into\n207: layers follows the principle of separation of concerns — letting architects, business leaders, and engineers focus on specific\n208: dimensions (business strategy, data flows, application software, hardware infrastructure) while keeping a clear view of how the\n209: layers interact and depend on one another.\n210: Most standard EA frameworks (such as TOGAF) categorize the enterprise into four primary architectural domains: Business,\n211: Data, Application, and Technology.\n212: The Four Core EA Components (Layers) — MEMORIZE\n213: Layer\n214: Definition\n215: Question It Answers\n216: Key Focus Areas\n217: Business Architecture\n218: The foundation of the EA\n219: framework; defines the\n220: organization's business\n221: strategy, governance,\n222: organization structure, and key\n223: business processes\n224: \"What does the business do,\n225: and how is it organized to\n226: achieve its strategic goals?\"\n227: Business strategy,\n228: organizational charts, business\n229: capabilities, value chains,\n230: standard operating procedures\n231: Data (Information)\n232: Architecture\n233: The structure and management\n234: of an organization's logical and\n235: physical data assets and data\n236: management resources\n237: \"What information does the\n238: enterprise need to operate,\n239: how is it stored, governed, and\n240: how does it flow across\n241: systems?\"\n242: Data entities, data models\n243: (conceptual, logical, physical),\n244: master data management\n245: (MDM), data governance,\n246: information lifecycles\n247: Application Architecture\n248: A blueprint for the individual\n249: applications deployment, their\n250: interactions, and their\n251: relationships to core business\n252: processes\n253: \"What software applications are\n254: needed to automate business\n255: processes and manage data?\"\n256: Software portfolio\n257: management, ERP systems,\n258: custom applications, software\n259: integration patterns,\n260: microservices architecture\n261: Technology Architecture\n262: The hardware, software, and\n263: network infrastructure that\n264: provides the hosting and\n265: runtime environment for\n266: applications\n267: \"What physical and virtual\n268: infrastructure is required to run\n269: the application architecture\n270: securely and scalably?\"\n271: Servers, cloud infrastructure\n272: (IaaS/PaaS), operating\n273: systems, network topologies,\n274: data centers, security\n275: infrastructure, end-user\n276: hardware\n277: ARCHITECTURE ARTIFACTS\n278: \n279: ===== PAGE 6 =====\n280: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n281: Page 6 of 43\n282: Architecture Artifact — an architectural work product that describes an aspect of the architecture. In simple terms, artifacts are\n283: the deliverables, documents, diagrams, models, matrices, and catalogs produced throughout the EA lifecycle to capture and\n284: communicate architectural designs.\n285: Classification of Artifacts (3 major categories) — MEMORIZE\n286: Category\n287: What It Is\n288: Answers\n289: Examples\n290: Catalogs\n291: A hierarchical list or inventory\n292: of things\n293: \"What inventory do we have?\"\n294: Application Portfolio Catalog,\n295: Data Entity Catalog\n296: Matrices\n297: A grid that shows the\n298: relationship between two or\n299: more architectural elements\n300: \"How do elements relate to\n301: each other?\"\n302: Application-to-Function Matrix,\n303: Actor-to-Role Matrix\n304: Diagrams / Models\n305: Visual representations of\n306: architecture components and\n307: their topology or dynamics\n308: \"What does the structure or\n309: flow look like?\"\n310: Network Diagram, Business\n311: Process Flow\n312: Examples of Artifacts Used at Each Layer\n313: Layer\n314: Type\n315: Specific Example\n316: Description & Purpose\n317: Business\n318: Diagram / Model\n319: Business Process Model\n320: (BPMN)\n321: Visual flowchart of step-by-step\n322: business workflows, decision\n323: points, and actors (e.g.,\n324: Order-to-Cash)\n325: Business\n326: Catalog\n327: Business Capability Map\n328: Hierarchical decomposition of\n329: what the business does to fulfill\n330: its mission (e.g., Marketing ->\n331: Lead Generation -> Campaign\n332: Management)\n333: Data\n334: Diagram\n335: Entity-Relationship Diagram\n336: (ERD)\n337: Visual model of data entities\n338: (e.g., Customer, Order,\n339: Product) and\n340: relationships/cardinality\n341: Data\n342: Matrix\n343: Information Flow Matrix\n344: Grid mapping which\n345: processes/applications create,\n346: read, update, or delete (CRUD)\n347: specific data entities\n348: Application\n349: Catalog\n350: Application Portfolio Catalog\n351: Comprehensive inventory of all\n352: software systems, including\n353: version, owner, lifecycle status,\n354: and cost\n355: Application\n356: Diagram\n357: Application Communication\n358: Diagram\n359: Visual map of how applications\n360: integrate, interface, and\n361: exchange data (APIs, message\n362: queues, batch files)\n363: Technology\n364: Diagram\n365: Network Topology Diagram\n366: Architectural blueprint of\n367: routers, firewalls, subnets,\n368: zones (DMZ, internal), and\n369: cloud connection points\n370: Technology\n371: Catalog\n372: Technology Standards Catalog\n373: Approved hardware, software,\n374: and cloud services\n375: specifications permitted in the\n376: organization\n377: INTEROPERABILITY AND ARTIFACT TRACEABILITY\n378: \n379: ===== PAGE 7 =====\n380: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n381: Page 7 of 43\n382: Key concept — a frequent and critical pitfall in EA is the creation of siloed or disconnected artifacts — e.g., polished business\n383: process models with no structural or logical connection to the underlying IT software and hardware. When architectural\n384: components are isolated, organizations lose alignment between strategic goals and technical execution.\n385: Effective EA requires rigorous traceability across layers: every technical asset maps directly back to a business need, and\n386: every business objective has explicit digital support.\n387: The Anatomy of Cross-Layer Traceability (4 layers) — MEMORIZE\n388: 1. Business Layer (Strategy & Capabilities) — the chain begins with enterprise strategy; a defined Business Capability\n389: (e.g., Customer Support) dictates operational responsibilities. Artifact: business capability maps and high-level organizational\n390: models.\n391: 2. Data Layer (Information & Entities) — business capabilities cannot function without information (e.g., customer support\n392: requires handling, creating, and modifying data elements). Artifact: Conceptual and Logical Entity-Relationship Diagrams\n393: (ERDs) identifying critical data entities such as the Customer Ticket and its metadata (timestamps, priority levels, resolution\n394: status).\n395: 3. Application Layer (Software & Services) — data must be processed and manipulated by software systems that\n396: automate human workflows. Artifact: Application Portfolio catalogs and Application Communication diagrams (e.g., enterprise\n397: CRM or Zendesk ticketing platform).\n398: 4. Technology Layer (Infrastructure & Hardware) — software applications must reside on physical or virtual hardware,\n399: operating systems, and network topologies to execute. Artifact: Network Topology and Deployment diagrams (e.g., CRM\n400: hosted on AWS EC2 behind secure virtual private clouds / VPCs).\n\n(Showing lines 1-400 of 2262. Use offset=401 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"===== PAGE 1 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 1 of 43\nSAI 1 — SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE AND INTEGRATION 1\n(ITNET312-T)\nCOMPLETE EXAM REVIEWER — LESSONS L2 to L8\nSource: SAI Review Reference · Prof. Pops Madriaga · 3rd Year 1st Sem · BSIT-S-3A\nLessons covered: L2 – Enterprise Architecture Components · L3 – Developing an Enterprise Architecture · L4 – Using an\nEnterprise Architecture · L5 – All About SOA · L6 – Integrating Technology · L7 – Integrating Applications · L8 – Enterprise\nIntegration\n\n===== PAGE 2 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 2 of 43\nL2: ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE COMPONENTS\nMUST MEMORIZE — Key Terms & Definitions\nEA Implementation Methodology — a detailed, step-by-step approach that describes how an Enterprise Architecture program\nis established, developed, managed, maintained, and used within an organization. It provides a clear approach for building and\nmanaging an effective EA program.\nImportance of a Structured Implementation Methodology — helps an organization establish and manage its EA program in a","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt","text":"===== PAGE 1 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 1 of 43\nSAI 1 — SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE AND INTEGRATION 1\n(ITNET312-T)\nCOMPLETE EXAM REVIEWER — LESSONS L2 to L8\nSource: SAI Review Reference · Prof. Pops Madriaga · 3rd Year 1st Sem · BSIT-S-3A\nLessons covered: L2 – Enterprise Architecture Components · L3 – Developing an Enterprise Architecture · L4 – Using an\nEnterprise Architecture · L5 – All About SOA · L6 – Integrating Technology · L7 – Integrating Applications · L8 – Enterprise\nIntegration\n\n===== PAGE 2 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 2 of 43\nL2: ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE COMPONENTS\nMUST MEMORIZE — Key Terms & Definitions\nEA Implementation Methodology — a detailed, step-by-step approach that describes how an Enterprise Architecture program\nis established, developed, managed, maintained, and used within an organization. It provides a clear approach for building and\nmanaging an effective EA program.\nImportance of a Structured Implementation Methodology — helps an organization establish and manage its EA program in a\nclear, consistent, and coordinated way.\nKey Benefits (6)\n1. Clear Direction — defines the EA's scope, goals, and overall approach.\n2. Better Governance — establishes how EA decisions are managed and coordinated.\n3. Organized Documentation — identifies what EA information and artifacts need to be documented.\n4. Stakeholder Alignment — helps stakeholders understand the EA's purpose, value, and direction.\n5. Better Decision-Making — provides organized EA information that supports planning and decisions.\n6. Continuous Improvement — supports regular updates and maintenance as the organization changes.\nEA Framework — a structured set of tools, models, and guidelines used to organize and design an enterprise's architecture. It\nprovides a common language and structure for planning, building, and managing IT and business systems, and helps ensure\nconsistency across all levels of the organization.\nWhy organizations need a framework\n• Reduces complexity and miscommunication\n• Aligns business goals with IT strategy\n• Improves decision-making and resource planning\n• Provides consistency, especially in large or growing organizations\nThe 4 Phases of EA Implementation (LIST TO MEMORIZE)\n1. PHASE I — EA Program Establishment — establish the EA program and leadership; define the implementation\nmethodology; establish governance; communicate the EA vision and gain stakeholder support.\n2. PHASE II — Framework and Tool Selection — select the EA documentation framework; identify Lines of Business and\nCrosscuts; identify EA components to document; select documentation methods and tools; establish an EA repository.\n3. PHASE III — Documentation of the EA — evaluate existing documentation; document the current state; develop future\noperating scenarios; define future planning assumptions; document the future state; develop the EA Management Plan.\n4. PHASE IV — Use and Maintain the EA — use EA documentation for planning and decision-making; regularly update\ncurrent and future views; maintain the EA repository and tools; update the EA Management Plan.\nApplication of EA Implementation Methodology (Example)\nSituation: a planning team needs to identify improvements in information exchange within a Line of Business.\nUsing the EA Repository: access current and future views of the information flow; review related business services, applications,\nand networks; display the EA diagrams and information during the meeting.\nResult: faster communication of ideas, better understanding among stakeholders, fewer interpretation errors, more productive\ndiscussions, better-informed decisions.\nBenefits and Challenges (MEMORIZE)\nBenefits: Clearer Direction, Better Governance, Improved Understanding, Better Communication, More Coordinated Planning,\nContinuous Improvement.\nChallenges: Complexity, Governance Difficulties, Requirements Gaps, Implementation Problems, Maintenance Issues.\nMETHODOLOGY vs FRAMEWORK (COMPARISON)\n\n===== PAGE 3 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 3 of 43\nAspect\nEA Implementation Methodology\nEA Framework\nQuestion it answers\nHOW the EA program is established and\nmanaged\nWHAT STRUCTURE is used to organize\nthe EA\nFocus\nDefines processes, steps, and activities\nOrganizes architecture information\nRole\nGuides development, documentation, and\nmaintenance\nDefines the scope and relationships of\narchitecture areas\nIncludes\nFramework and tool selection\nProvides the structure used for\ndocumenting the EA\nZACHMAN FRAMEWORK\nZachman Framework — developed by John Zachman in 1987; one of the earliest and most influential EA frameworks. It is not\na methodology — it is a classification schema (a way of organizing and categorizing architectural artifacts). Often compared\nto blueprints used in other engineering disciplines.\nStructure (6x6 matrix, 36 cells total)\n• 6 Columns (Focus / Interrogatives): What, How, Where, Who, When, Why\n• 6 Rows (Perspectives): Planner, Owner, Designer, Builder, Sub-Contractor, Enterprise (Actual System)\n• Each cell represents a specific artifact or model relevant to that intersection.\nHow it is used / Key takeaways\n• Helps organizations ensure completeness — no perspective or aspect is overlooked.\n• Used as a reference/checklist, not a step-by-step process.\n• Encourages clear communication between different stakeholders (executives, designers, technical teams).\n• Still influences modern frameworks like TOGAF today.\nTOGAF (THE OPEN GROUP ARCHITECTURE FRAMEWORK)\nTOGAF — an Enterprise Architecture framework used to help organizations design, plan, implement, and manage their\nenterprise architecture. It provides a structured approach for developing architectures and is widely used to align business goals\nwith IT systems and technology.\nPurpose of TOGAF (LIST)\n• Align IT with business objectives\n• Plan changes to systems and technology\n• Identify gaps between the current and desired architecture\n• Reduce duplication and unnecessary technology\n• Improve communication between business and IT teams\n• Manage architecture changes in a structured way\nExample: a company wants to move from manual processes to an online system — TOGAF helps analyze the current situation,\ndesign the target system, and plan how to transition.\nADM — Architecture Development Method\nThe ADM helps organizations (6 steps):\n1. Identify business requirements\n2. Analyze the current architecture\n3. Design the target architecture\n4. Identify the changes needed\n5. Plan implementation\n6. Manage and review the architecture\nTOGAF ADM CYCLE (MEMORIZE)\n\n===== PAGE 4 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 4 of 43\nPhase\nKey Focus\nPreliminary Phase — Preparation\nEstablish EA principles and governance; define roles,\nresponsibilities, and processes\nPhase A — Architecture Vision\nDefine goals and scope; identify stakeholders; create the overall\narchitecture vision\nPhase B — Business Architecture\nDefine business goals, processes, and organization\nPhase C — Information Systems Architecture\nData: identify and organize required data; Applications: define\nsystems and how they interact\nPhase D — Technology Architecture\nDefine infrastructure, networks, platforms, and technologies\nPhase E — Opportunities & Solutions\nIdentify possible solutions; group changes into projects; define\nimplementation approach\nPhase F — Migration Planning\nPrioritize projects; create migration roadmap; consider resources,\ncosts, risks, and timelines\nPhase G — Implementation Governance\nMonitor implementation; check architecture compliance; manage\nimplementation issues\nPhase H — Architecture Change Management\nMonitor business and technology changes; update the architecture\nwhen needed; support continuous improvement\nRequirements Management\nOperates throughout all ADM phases; tracks, evaluates, and\nmanages changing requirements\nFEAF (FEDERAL ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE FRAMEWORK)\nFEAF — a framework developed by the U.S. Federal Government to organize and manage enterprise architecture. Provides a\ncommon structure for analyzing the current organization and designing the desired future state. Helps government agencies align\nbusiness goals, processes, information, and technology. Supports standardization, integration, and better decision-making across\nagencies.\nFEAF Reference Models (MEMORIZE the 6 abbreviations + purpose)\nModel\nPurpose\nPRM — Performance Reference Model\nMeasures how well an organization and its IT systems achieve\nstrategic goals and desired outcomes\nDRM — Data Reference Model\nDefines how data is categorized, described, shared, and managed\nacross the enterprise\nSRM — Security Reference Model\nDefines security-related capabilities, controls, and requirements for\nprotecting enterprise systems and information\nBRM — Business Reference Model\nDescribes the organization's business functions, services, and\nactivities\nARM — Application Reference Model\nOrganizes IT services and application components that support\nbusiness functions\nTRM — Technical Reference Model\nProvides a common structure for technologies, platforms, and\ntechnical standards used by the enterprise\nGARTNER EA METHODOLOGY\nGartner EA Methodology — developed by Gartner, a research and advisory organization. Focuses on business strategy,\nstakeholders, and business outcomes rather than technology alone. Helps organizations understand their current state and\ndefine their future state. Emphasizes continuous communication between business and IT.\nKEY IDEA: Gartner views Enterprise Architecture as a continuous process of change and improvement, not a one-time\nproject.\nGartner EA Process (6 steps)\n\n===== PAGE 5 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 5 of 43\n1. Understand the Business — identify business goals, strategies, and stakeholder needs.\n2. Assess the Current State — examine existing processes, systems, data, and technology.\n3. Define the Future State — design the desired architecture based on business objectives.\n4. Identify Gaps — compare the current and future states to determine what needs to change.\n5. Develop & Implement Roadmap — prioritize projects and create a practical plan for achieving the future state.\n6. Continuous Improvement — monitor results and update the architecture as business needs change.\nCHOOSING THE RIGHT FRAMEWORK (COMPARISON TABLE)\nSituation\nSuitable Framework\nNeed to organize and classify EA information\nZachman\nNeed a detailed EA development process\nTOGAF\nGovernment agency or public-sector environment\nFEAF\nNeed flexibility and strong business alignment\nGartner\nENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE COMPONENT\nEA is rarely managed as a single monolithic block. To make complex organizations understandable, governable, and\ntransformable, EA divides the enterprise into distinct, interconnected domains or components. Dividing the enterprise into\nlayers follows the principle of separation of concerns — letting architects, business leaders, and engineers focus on specific\ndimensions (business strategy, data flows, application software, hardware infrastructure) while keeping a clear view of how the\nlayers interact and depend on one another.\nMost standard EA frameworks (such as TOGAF) categorize the enterprise into four primary architectural domains: Business,\nData, Application, and Technology.\nThe Four Core EA Components (Layers) — MEMORIZE\nLayer\nDefinition\nQuestion It Answers\nKey Focus Areas\nBusiness Architecture\nThe foundation of the EA\nframework; defines the\norganization's business\nstrategy, governance,\norganization structure, and key\nbusiness processes\n\"What does the business do,\nand how is it organized to\nachieve its strategic goals?\"\nBusiness strategy,\norganizational charts, business\ncapabilities, value chains,\nstandard operating procedures\nData (Information)\nArchitecture\nThe structure and management\nof an organization's logical and\nphysical data assets and data\nmanagement resources\n\"What information does the\nenterprise need to operate,\nhow is it stored, governed, and\nhow does it flow across\nsystems?\"\nData entities, data models\n(conceptual, logical, physical),\nmaster data management\n(MDM), data governance,\ninformation lifecycles\nApplication Architecture\nA blueprint for the individual\napplications deployment, their\ninteractions, and their\nrelationships to core business\nprocesses\n\"What software applications are\nneeded to automate business\nprocesses and manage data?\"\nSoftware portfolio\nmanagement, ERP systems,\ncustom applications, software\nintegration patterns,\nmicroservices architecture\nTechnology Architecture\nThe hardware, software, and\nnetwork infrastructure that\nprovides the hosting and\nruntime environment for\napplications\n\"What physical and virtual\ninfrastructure is required to run\nthe application architecture\nsecurely and scalably?\"\nServers, cloud infrastructure\n(IaaS/PaaS), operating\nsystems, network topologies,\ndata centers, security\ninfrastructure, end-user\nhardware\nARCHITECTURE ARTIFACTS\n\n===== PAGE 6 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 6 of 43\nArchitecture Artifact — an architectural work product that describes an aspect of the architecture. In simple terms, artifacts are\nthe deliverables, documents, diagrams, models, matrices, and catalogs produced throughout the EA lifecycle to capture and\ncommunicate architectural designs.\nClassification of Artifacts (3 major categories) — MEMORIZE\nCategory\nWhat It Is\nAnswers\nExamples\nCatalogs\nA hierarchical list or inventory\nof things\n\"What inventory do we have?\"\nApplication Portfolio Catalog,\nData Entity Catalog\nMatrices\nA grid that shows the\nrelationship between two or\nmore architectural elements\n\"How do elements relate to\neach other?\"\nApplication-to-Function Matrix,\nActor-to-Role Matrix\nDiagrams / Models\nVisual representations of\narchitecture components and\ntheir topology or dynamics\n\"What does the structure or\nflow look like?\"\nNetwork Diagram, Business\nProcess Flow\nExamples of Artifacts Used at Each Layer\nLayer\nType\nSpecific Example\nDescription & Purpose\nBusiness\nDiagram / Model\nBusiness Process Model\n(BPMN)\nVisual flowchart of step-by-step\nbusiness workflows, decision\npoints, and actors (e.g.,\nOrder-to-Cash)\nBusiness\nCatalog\nBusiness Capability Map\nHierarchical decomposition of\nwhat the business does to fulfill\nits mission (e.g., Marketing ->\nLead Generation -> Campaign\nManagement)\nData\nDiagram\nEntity-Relationship Diagram\n(ERD)\nVisual model of data entities\n(e.g., Customer, Order,\nProduct) and\nrelationships/cardinality\nData\nMatrix\nInformation Flow Matrix\nGrid mapping which\nprocesses/applications create,\nread, update, or delete (CRUD)\nspecific data entities\nApplication\nCatalog\nApplication Portfolio Catalog\nComprehensive inventory of all\nsoftware systems, including\nversion, owner, lifecycle status,\nand cost\nApplication\nDiagram\nApplication Communication\nDiagram\nVisual map of how applications\nintegrate, interface, and\nexchange data (APIs, message\nqueues, batch files)\nTechnology\nDiagram\nNetwork Topology Diagram\nArchitectural blueprint of\nrouters, firewalls, subnets,\nzones (DMZ, internal), and\ncloud connection points\nTechnology\nCatalog\nTechnology Standards Catalog\nApproved hardware, software,\nand cloud services\nspecifications permitted in the\norganization\nINTEROPERABILITY AND ARTIFACT TRACEABILITY\n\n===== PAGE 7 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 7 of 43\nKey concept — a frequent and critical pitfall in EA is the creation of siloed or disconnected artifacts — e.g., polished business\nprocess models with no structural or logical connection to the underlying IT software and hardware. When architectural\ncomponents are isolated, organizations lose alignment between strategic goals and technical execution.\nEffective EA requires rigorous traceability across layers: every technical asset maps directly back to a business need, and\nevery business objective has explicit digital support.\nThe Anatomy of Cross-Layer Traceability (4 layers) — MEMORIZE\n1. Business Layer (Strategy & Capabilities) — the chain begins with enterprise strategy; a defined Business Capability\n(e.g., Customer Support) dictates operational responsibilities. Artifact: business capability maps and high-level organizational\nmodels.\n2. Data Layer (Information & Entities) — business capabilities cannot function without information (e.g., customer support\nrequires handling, creating, and modifying data elements). Artifact: Conceptual and Logical Entity-Relationship Diagrams\n(ERDs) identifying critical data entities such as the Customer Ticket and its metadata (timestamps, priority levels, resolution\nstatus).\n3. Application Layer (Software & Services) — data must be processed and manipulated by software systems that\nautomate human workflows. Artifact: Application Portfolio catalogs and Application Communication diagrams (e.g., enterprise\nCRM or Zendesk ticketing platform).\n4. Technology Layer (Infrastructure & Hardware) — software applications must reside on physical or virtual hardware,\noperating systems, and network topologies to execute. Artifact: Network Topology and Deployment diagrams (e.g., CRM\nhosted on AWS EC2 behind secure virtual private clouds / VPCs).","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":400,"totalLines":2262,"truncated":true}},"title":"xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt","time":{"start":1789817810593,"end":1789817810663}},"id":"prt_0b9746d2a0016c7DzSAJ5MLy5K","sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","messageID":"msg_0b9745fbc0013AdEby4QIaebfF"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1789817810693,"sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","part":{"id":"prt_0b9746eed001vFBccaGP085spN","reason":"tool-calls","messageID":"msg_0b9745fbc0013AdEby4QIaebfF","sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":35960,"input":97,"output":279,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":35584}},"cost":0}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1789817811645,"sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","part":{"id":"prt_0b97472b90015A6vOgXuupUKfO","messageID":"msg_0b9746ef9001fONNr605Orwshm","sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789817812998,"sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_050ec03a402248be97b98b21","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt","offset":401,"limit":500},"output":"<path>C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n401: Why Traceability Matters: Impact Analysis (MEMORIZE)\n402: • Proactive Risk Mitigation — e.g., deprecating an AWS server instance instantly reveals which applications, data entities,\n403: and business processes will be disrupted.\n404: • Cost Optimization — leaders identify redundant applications or underutilized infrastructure by tracking them back to the\n405: business capabilities they support (or fail to support).\n406: • Strategic Agility — when executive leadership shifts strategy, cross-layer matrices pinpoint exactly which data models,\n407: software integrations, and cloud resources need updating.\n408: \n409: ===== PAGE 8 =====\n410: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n411: Page 8 of 43\n412: L3: DEVELOPING AN ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE\n413: DOCUMENTATION\n414: Key concept — Every organization already has an architecture. Every organization runs on a combination of systems,\n415: processes, and infrastructure, regardless of whether any of it has been formally documented. Systems are added over time, by\n416: different teams, for different reasons, often without a shared record of how they connect.\n417: The practical consequence: without documentation, decision-makers work from partial or outdated information about their\n418: own organization.\n419: Why documentation comes before planning\n420: • Plans built without an accurate baseline tend to underestimate cost, timeline, and complexity.\n421: • Any structured planning process starts with an accurate assessment of the current situation.\n422: • Organizations cannot reliably plan system upgrades, budget allocations, or process changes without first knowing what\n423: already exists.\n424: Documentation and its role\n425: • Systems and processes change regularly, but documentation is often updated far less frequently -> documentation and\n426: reality drift apart.\n427: • Documentation is frequently treated as a one-time deliverable rather than an ongoing responsibility.\n428: Different stakeholders, different needs\n429: • Technical Staff — need clear, accurate detail on existing systems before making changes or building integrations.\n430: • Leadership — need comparable, reliable information to justify budget and resource decisions.\n431: • Audit and Compliance — need a documented record of what changed, when, and why.\n432: CURRENT OPERATIONS AND FUTURE PLANNING\n433: Organizations must maintain what is currently running while planning what comes next. Future planning tends to get more\n434: attention and investment than current-state documentation, even though both are necessary.\n435: Current Architectural Views (\"As-Is\" state) — like taking a complete snapshot of a company's technology and processes right\n436: now. Why it matters: we need to know exactly what we have before we build or change anything. The Baseline: prevents\n437: crashing the system when adding new features. The Blueprint: gives developers and planners a safe, reliable map to work from.\n438: Keeping track of changes (EACR)\n439: • Standard Format (EACR) — companies use an EA Change Request to update systems smoothly (like turning in a\n440: standardized college paper).\n441: • Electronic Template — making forms digital and easy to use encourages stakeholders to participate and follow the rules.\n442: • The History Book — archiving these requests creates a detailed history guide for developers to follow during\n443: implementation.\n444: The Tech Inventory — EA acts as a giant checklist for all the hardware (computers, servers) and software a company owns.\n445: • Smart Budgeting — tells leaders how much money to set aside for maintenance, daily operations, and training.\n446: • Justifying Upgrades — provides concrete proof when authorizing new technology purchases.\n447: • Automated Tracking — uses barcodes and software systems to keep the list 100% accurate.\n448: Mapping out activities (IDEF-O)\n449: • Inputs & Outputs — visual models (like IDEF-O) clearly show what data goes in and what comes out.\n450: • The Rules — explicitly defines the \"Controls\" (rules to follow) and the \"Mechanisms\" (tools to use).\n451: • No Guessing — acts as a strict, foolproof blueprint so developers don't guess when writing code.\n452: Following the data flow — see exactly how a user's request routes through active services (Visualizing Paths); mapping the\n453: flow before building prevents accidental cutting of vital connections (Playing it Safe); shows step-by-step how data is consumed,\n454: transformed, and outputted (Process Logic).\n455: Connecting the dots — ERDs (Entity-Relationship Diagrams) define how different parts of the business relate (e.g., how a\n456: 'Buyer' connects to a 'Listing'). Leaders spot missing links (Finding the Gaps) and mastering the \"as-is\" state supports strategic\n457: decisions.\n458: \n459: ===== PAGE 9 =====\n460: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n461: Page 9 of 43\n462: FUTURE ARCHITECTURAL VIEWS\n463: The future view documents the IT resources expected to be active several years ahead — as opposed to the \"as-is\" view,\n464: which documents what exists today.\n465: • Why gaps appear: without deliberate EA planning, future IT resources drift out of alignment with strategic goals and\n466: business services, and duplication builds up. A well-built future view shows exactly how that duplication will be eliminated.\n467: • Same method, same levels: future artifacts are built using the same planning assumptions, documentation, and modeling\n468: techniques as current-view artifacts — at every level — so change is always traceable.\n469: • Analogy: \"Having future EA views is like having a full set of blueprints for the modification of an existing home.\"\n470: Why future architectural views matter (MEMORIZE)\n471: • Resource Planning — a coherent view of changing priorities, processes, and resources across the enterprise.\n472: • Decision-Making Support — compare current vs. planned states side by side before committing budget or staff.\n473: • Enterprise-Wide Communication — a shared \"to-be\" picture keeps business units and IT teams talking about the same\n474: target.\n475: • Upward & Downward Alignment — approved future changes stay traceable to strategic goals above and implementation\n476: detail below.\n477: DEVELOPING A CONOPS SCENARIO (Concept of Operations)\n478: What a CONOPS scenario documents — good and bad operating environments, drawn from the SWOT Analysis, so the\n479: enterprise can plan probable defensive responses and offensive initiatives in advance.\n480: The Storytelling Technique (Dr. Robert Neilson) — a short story told through the eyes of a central character living through\n481: future events, tied to specific planning assumptions in three areas of change:\n482: • Process — How it gets done\n483: • People — Who is involved\n484: • Technology — What tools are needed\n485: Stories are easy to remember — and highlighting the assumptions reveals exactly which resources and capabilities the scenario\n486: needs.\n487: The \"Chess\" Analogy — anticipating how an opponent might react to your moves is part of what makes a chess master;\n488: top-level play means thinking through several alternative sequences of moves and counter-moves — a dynamic balance between\n489: acting and reacting.\n490: FUTURE ARTIFACTS (FIVE LEVELS — MEMORIZE)\n491: Strategic Level — \"Directions and priorities\"\n492: • Documents the enterprise's overarching goals, direction, and priorities that guide every other level of planning.\n493: • EA Components: Strategic Plan; E-Commerce / E-Government Plan.\n494: • Future-View Artifacts: Strategic Plan, SWOT Analysis, CONOPS Scenario, CONOPS Diagram, Balanced Scorecard.\n495: • EXAMPLE: \"Grow e-government service adoption 30% in 3 years\" — tracked on the Balanced Scorecard.\n496: Business Level — \"Services and processes\"\n497: • Documents the products, services, and processes the enterprise uses to carry out its strategic goals.\n498: • EA Components: Supply Chains, Business Services, IT Capital Planning Portfolio.\n499: • Future-View Artifacts: Business Plan, Node Connectivity Diagram, Swim Lane Process Diagram, Business Process /\n500: Service Model, Use Case Narrative & Diagram, Investment Business Case.\n501: • EXAMPLE: a Swim Lane Diagram tracing a permit application from Citizen -> Apply -> Portal -> Submit -> Records Office.\n502: Information Level — \"Knowledge, systems, databases\"\n503: • Documents the data, knowledge, and information flows that support the enterprise's business services.\n504: • EA Components: Knowledge Warehouses, Information Systems, Databases.\n505: • Future-View Artifacts: Knowledge Mgmt. Plan, Information Exchange Matrix, Logical / Physical Data Model, Activity/Entity\n506: (CRUD) Matrix, Data Dictionary / Object Library.\n507: \n508: ===== PAGE 10 =====\n509: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n510: Page 10 of 43\n511: • EXAMPLE: CRUD Matrix entry: 'Permit Record' — Created by Intake, Updated by Reviewer, Deleted by Records Admin.\n512: Systems / Services Level — \"Applications and integrations\"\n513: • Documents the software applications and services that create, process, and exchange information.\n514: • EA Components: Software Applications, Web Services, Service Bus & Middleware, ERP Solutions.\n515: • Future-View Artifacts: System Interface Diagram, System Data Flow Diagram, System/Operations Matrix, System\n516: Evolution Diagram, Web Application Diagram.\n517: • EXAMPLE: a System Interface Diagram showing the Permit Portal calling a Payment Gateway API.\n518: Infrastructure Level — \"Data, telecom, video, cable plant, and security\"\n519: • Documents the physical networks, hardware, and facilities — voice, data, and video — that support enterprise systems.\n520: • Key Future-View Artifacts: Network Connectivity Diagram, Network Inventory, Capital Equipment Inventory, Building\n521: Blueprints, Rack Elevation Diagram.\n522: • Focuses on changes to cable plant, wiring closets, backbone hardware, servers, and remote access resources.\n523: • EXAMPLE: a Network Connectivity Diagram showing a new fiber link between City Hall and the Data Center.\n524: KEEPING FUTURE VIEWS TRUSTWORTHY — FREEZE PERIODS\n525: The Challenge: as changes are implemented, they move from the future view into the current view — so at any moment, what's\n526: \"current\" and what's \"planned\" can blur.\n527: The Fix — Freeze Periods: freeze the current and future views at regular intervals — e.g., twice a year. Changes accumulate\n528: for 4–5 months, then publish as a new release in month six.\n529: A BROADER PERSPECTIVE: TOGAF'S VIEW — THREE ARCHITECTURE STATES\n530: TOGAF formalizes the \"current vs. future view\" as three explicit architecture states:\n531: 1. Baseline Architecture — the current, \"as-is\" structure of the enterprise; the starting point for any transformation.\n532: 2. Transition Architecture — one or more intermediate states that bridge the gap between baseline and target on a realistic\n533: timeline.\n534: 3. Target Architecture — the desired future state: capabilities, processes, and technologies aligned to strategic goals.\n535: Whether it's called a \"future view\" (EA3 Cube) or a \"target architecture\" (TOGAF), the core discipline is the same: document\n536: where you're headed with the same rigor used to document where you are.\n537: ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE MANAGEMENT PLAN\n538: EA Management Plan — guides the transition from the current architecture to the desired future architecture; it coordinates IT\n539: projects, resources, standards, governance, and implementation activities. It is a living document that is regularly updated.\n540: Purpose of the EA Management Plan\n541: • Coordinates multiple IT implementation and upgrade projects.\n542: • Supports prioritization, decision-making, and oversight.\n543: • Connects business strategy with processes, systems, information, and technology.\n544: • Provides a roadmap for moving from the current EA to the future EA.\n545: • Analogy: functions like an architect's project plan for remodeling a house — showing the design, approach, schedule, and\n546: sequence of work.\n547: MAIN PARTS OF AN EA MANAGEMENT PLAN (MEMORIZE — 4)\n548: 1. EA Program Management\n549: 2. Current Architecture Summary\n550: 3. Future Architecture Summary\n551: 4. Glossary and References\n552: Together, these sections explain how the EA program is governed, what currently exists, what must change, and how the plan's\n553: terminology is defined.\n554: \n555: ===== PAGE 11 =====\n556: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n557: Page 11 of 43\n558: 1. EA PROGRAM MANAGEMENT\n559: Explains how the EA program will be managed.\n560: • Governance and principles — establishes policies and decision-making procedures.\n561: • Strategy and business support — ensures EA activities support organizational goals.\n562: • Roles and responsibilities — defines the duties of sponsors, CIOs, architects, managers, analysts, and other\n563: stakeholders.\n564: • Program budget — documents annual and long-term costs, including total cost of ownership.\n565: • Performance measures — evaluates whether the EA program is effective and efficient.\n566: EA governance, roles, and performance measures\n567: • EA principles may promote information sharing, stakeholder participation, open standards, and the use of IT as a means to\n568: achieve business goals.\n569: • The Chief Architect manages the EA program, documentation, standards, and configuration management.\n570: • Business and technical architects identify requirements, analyze problems, and design solutions.\n571: • Performance measures: Outcome measures (improvements in integration, user satisfaction, or IT investment decisions)\n572: and Output measures (number of projects completed, scope changes, or planning activities).\n573: 2. CURRENT ARCHITECTURE SUMMARY\n574: Describes how existing resources support the organization. It includes: strategic goals and initiatives; business services and\n575: information flows; systems and applications; technology infrastructure; IT security; EA standards; workforce skills and training\n576: requirements. Helps identify the current role, strengths, weaknesses, and performance gaps of IT.\n577: Current architecture analysis examines: how EA components support strategic goals and business processes; how\n578: information moves between departments and systems; whether applications are integrated, scalable, cost-effective, and\n579: user-friendly; whether networks and infrastructure reliably support voice, data, video, and mobile services; whether technical\n580: standards are followed; what skills and training employees need. NOTE: sensitive security details should be documented in a\n581: separate IT Security Plan to avoid exposing vulnerabilities.\n582: 3. FUTURE ARCHITECTURE SUMMARY\n583: Describes the desired direction of the enterprise.\n584: • Future operating scenarios — possible business environments (status quo, aggressive growth, or responding\n585: defensively to a market downturn).\n586: • Planning assumptions — expected changes in business processes, people, technology, and resources.\n587: • Updated architecture views — planned changes documented from strategic goals down to technology components.\n588: • The selected scenario becomes the basis for future EA decisions.\n589: SEQUENCING AND CONFIGURATION MANAGEMENT\n590: • EA Sequencing Plan — lists projects, tasks, milestones, dependencies, and timeframes; ensures projects occur in the\n591: correct order; prevents problems caused by implementing dependent systems too early; helps coordinate upgrades,\n592: migrations, consolidations, and retirements.\n593: • Configuration Management — reviews and controls proposed EA changes; applies technical standards; manages\n594: waivers and approval deadlines; maintains documentation and version control; uses an Enterprise Architecture Change\n595: Request reviewed by the Chief Architect and stakeholders.\n596: L3 KEY TAKEAWAYS\n597: • The EA Management Plan is a roadmap for managing enterprise-wide architectural change.\n598: • It links business strategy to IT systems, information, infrastructure, and workforce requirements.\n599: • It documents both current conditions and future goals.\n600: • Sequencing ensures implementation projects are coordinated.\n601: • Configuration management protects consistency, standards, and version control.\n602: • Because the enterprise continuously changes, the plan must be regularly updated and maintained in an EA repository.\n603: \n604: ===== PAGE 12 =====\n605: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n606: Page 12 of 43\n607: L4: USING AN ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE\n608: INTRODUCTION\n609: Enterprise Architecture (EA) — a structured approach to organizing an organization's business, data, applications, and\n610: technology. It provides a blueprint that shows: how different parts of an organization work together; how technology supports\n611: business goals; how systems and processes should be designed; how changes and improvements should be managed.\n612: Why do we need to use EA?\n613: • Without EA: systems become disorganized; departments may use incompatible systems; projects can exceed time and\n614: budget; security risks increase; information becomes difficult to manage.\n615: • With EA: better planning; system integration; improved security; reduced duplication; better decision-making; alignment\n616: between IT and business goals.\n617: Remember the three areas as a simple sequence: Plan, Protect, and Manage.\n618: • 4.1 Planning & Project Management — projects must be determined by objectives, requirements, resources, schedules,\n619: budgets, and potential risks.\n620: • 4.2 Security — how systems, applications, networks, and information will be protected (access control, data protection,\n621: security policies, managing risks).\n622: • 4.3 Repository & Support Tools — a repository where EA information (diagrams, documentation, standards, policies,\n623: system descriptions, models) can be stored and maintained.\n624: REAL-LIFE EXAMPLE: AMAZON — Enterprise Architecture in Action\n625: Amazon's ability to serve millions of customers depends on many interconnected systems.\n626: How one customer order is fulfilled (Enterprise Architecture view):\n627: 1. Customer Places Order\n628: 2. E-Commerce System\n629: 3. Payment System\n630: 4. Inventory System\n631: 5. Fulfillment System\n632: 6. Logistics System\n633: 7. Customer Receives Order\n634: Business Goals: Customer obsession, Operational excellence, Innovation, Long-term value.\n635: PLANNING & PROJECT MANAGEMENT\n636: • An EA only creates value when it is executed.\n637: • Planning — What & Why: converts the gap between the As-Is and the To-Be architecture into a list of initiatives, and\n638: decides which goes first.\n639: • Project Management — How & When: controls scope, schedule, cost, quality, resources, and risk so every initiative is\n640: delivered as designed.\n641: • Why it matters: stops duplicate and siloed systems from being bought and built; ties every peso of IT spending to a\n642: business goal; turns the blueprint into a fundable, dated roadmap.\n643: ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE PLANNING (EAP)\n644: EAP — the planning discipline that sits between the architecture and the projects. Definition (Spewak, 1992): \"Defining the\n645: architectures needed for the business use of information — and the plan to implement them.\"\n646: Note the two halves: an architecture AND a plan. Most failed EA efforts produce only the first — plenty of diagrams, no\n647: sequence, no budget, no owner.\n648: The Guiding Principle — MEMORIZE:\n649: BUSINESS -> DATA -> APPLICATIONS -> TECHNOLOGY\n650: Applications manage data. Technology supports applications. Everything must serve a business process. EAP is\n651: business-driven and data-driven — never technology-driven. Reverse the order and you get a solution hunting for a problem.\n652: \n653: ===== PAGE 13 =====\n654: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n655: Page 13 of 43\n656: How EAP works — Four Layers\n657: 1. Getting started — planning initiation: scope, executive sponsor, team, methodology, tools, and a dated workplan.\n658: Skipping this is the most common cause of a stalled EA effort.\n659: 2. Where we are today — business modelling plus a survey of current systems and technology, collected into an\n660: Information Resource Catalog. This is where the duplicates surface.\n661: 3. Where we want to be — data architecture first, then applications, then technology. These describe WHAT is needed, not\n662: how it will be coded.\n663: 4. How we get there — the implementation and migration plan: sequence, cost, benefit, resources, and roadmap. This is\n664: where architecture finally becomes a budget.\n665: SEQUENCING & PRIORITIZATION — Three More Tests\n666: • Dependency — foundations before features: no dashboard without a clean data source.\n667: • Risk & complexity — high technical risk? Pilot first, then roll out.\n668: • Compliance deadline — non-negotiable work, e.g., the Data Privacy Act.\n669: • Rule of thumb: never run two projects that change the same system at the same time.\n670: FROM ARCHITECTURE TO PROJECTS (4 steps — MEMORIZE)\n671: 1. Gap Analysis — compare the As-Is against the To-Be to find what is missing, duplicated, and obsolete. Every project\n672: must trace back to a proven gap.\n673: 2. Work Packages — group related gaps into small deliverables. Each package gets a clear scope, a single owner, and one\n674: concrete outcome.\n675: 3. Business Case — attach cost, benefit, and risk to each package. Leadership funds outcomes, not diagrams.\n676: 4. Roadmap — place the packages on a timeline with transition architectures in between, so the business keeps running\n677: while it changes.\n678: WHO DOES WHAT (COMPARISON)\n679: Role\n680: Cares about\n681: Asks\n682: Horizon\n683: Delivers\n684: Enterprise\n685: Architecture Team\n686: Long-term coherence of\n687: the whole enterprise\n688: \"Does this fit the target\n689: architecture?\"\n690: Several years, many\n691: projects\n692: Principles, standards,\n693: reference models,\n694: roadmap\n695: Project Manager /\n696: PMO\n697: Delivering this one\n698: project\n699: \"Are we on scope,\n700: schedule, and budget?\"\n701: Life of a single project\n702: Project plans, status\n703: reports, the working\n704: system\n705: Architecture Review\n706: Board (ARB)\n707: Checkpoints at initiation,\n708: at design, and before\n709: go-live\n710: Each review ends in\n711: approved / approved\n712: with conditions /\n713: rejected\n714: Per review\n715: Any deviation needs a\n716: documented exception\n717: with an expiry date —\n718: otherwise the\n719: \"temporary fix\" becomes\n720: permanent technical\n721: debt\n722: PITFALLS, SUCCESS FACTORS & METRICS (MEMORIZE)\n723: Common Pitfalls (4):\n724: 1. Ivory-tower architecture — beautiful models nobody follows\n725: 2. No executive sponsor — the plan becomes a suggestion\n726: 3. Big-bang scope — everything at once, nothing finished\n727: 4. Stale artifacts — six months later, nobody trusts them\n728: Success Factors (4):\n729: 1. Active sponsor — someone who can decide and fund\n730: 2. Small increments — visible wins every few months\n731: 3. Reviews at project gates — part of approval, not optional\n732: \n733: ===== PAGE 14 =====\n734: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n735: Page 14 of 43\n736: 4. A living repository — owned, updated, and used\n737: How we measure it:\n738: • % of projects architecturally compliant\n739: • Redundant apps retired or consolidated\n740: • Delivery time per work package\n741: • Benefits realized vs the business case\n742: SECURITY IN ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE\n743: Security means keeping the architecture and everything built on it safe from unauthorized access, misuse, or damage. It's part\n744: of using the EA, not a separate topic. After the architecture is built, it becomes \"live\" — real data, real systems, real users.\n745: Security should be considered while implementing the architecture, not after something goes wrong.\n746: The 4 Main Areas of Security (MEMORIZE):\n747: 1. Data Security — controlling who can see, change, or take the organization's data.\n748: 2. Network/Technology Security — protecting servers, connections, and infrastructure.\n749: 3. Application Security — protecting the software/systems people use daily.\n750: 4. Physical Security — protecting the actual hardware and facilities.\n751: Controlling who can access what\n752: • Security policies — the organization maps out its data, applications, and technology, then puts the plan into use.\n753: • Role-based access control (RBAC) — people only get access to what they need for their job (e.g., HR staff can't touch\n754: finance data).\n755: • Authentication — verifying who a user is before letting them in (passwords, ID checks, etc.).\n756: Security needs planning too — security tasks should be part of the project timeline, not an afterthought (setting rules, assigning\n757: access, testing systems); needs teamwork between the EA/IT team and other departments; if security issues are caught too late,\n758: it can delay the whole project.\n759: What happens if security is ignored — data can be stolen, leaked, or changed by the wrong people; systems can be disrupted\n760: causing downtime and lost work; the organization can lose the trust of employees, clients, or partners.\n761: Key takeaway — Security protects the value of everything the EA was built to do. It's an ongoing job — not something you do\n762: once and forget. A strong architecture is only as good as the security protecting it.\n763: CORE FUNCTION OF AN EA REPOSITORY\n764: Central Knowledge Base — an EA Repository serves as the master storage facility for all architectural assets, models,\n765: principles, and guidelines across the enterprise. It maintains single-source-of-truth visibility for Current (As-Is), Future (To-Be),\n766: and Transition states across Business, Data, Application, and Infrastructure domains.\n767: Strategic Decision Support — by capturing dependencies between business processes and underlying technology, the\n768: repository powers dependency mapping, impact analysis, and gap assessments — helping leadership evaluate downstream\n769: technical risk and strategic fit before capital is allocated.\n770: Core Repository Components (3):\n771: 1. Architecture Metamodel — defines the underlying schema, entity types, taxonomy, and relationship rules governing how\n772: architectural artifacts connect to one another.\n773: 2. Architecture Landscape — catalogs real-time operational views, target blueprints, and active roadmap transitions across\n774: all business units and IT domains.\n775: 3. Governance & Standards — houses regulatory compliance frameworks, technology reference models (TRMs), design\n776: principles, and architecture board review logs.\n777: Managing architecture artifacts — Catalogs (structured inventories: application catalogs, business capabilities, server asset\n778: lists), Matrices (cross-mapping tables: Application-to-Data CRUD matrix, Role-to-Process matrix), Diagrams (graphical\n779: representations of topologies, integration flows, and process models).\n780: KEY CAPABILITIES OF EA SUPPORT TOOLS (4 — MEMORIZE)\n781: 1. Automated Diagramming & Layout — generates dynamic visual models directly from repository data, eliminating\n782: manual drawing and keeping diagrams synchronized with data.\n783: \n784: ===== PAGE 15 =====\n785: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n786: Page 15 of 43\n787: 2. Impact & Risk Analysis — performs traceability queries to evaluate how replacing or modifying an application impacts\n788: connected systems and business operations.\n789: 3. Collaborative Governance — provides role-based access control (RBAC), approval workflows, and audit trails for\n790: Architecture Review Board (ARB) approvals.\n791: 4. Automated Data Ingestion — connects via APIs to cloud platforms, CMDBs, and source repositories to continuously\n792: refresh landscape data.\n793: EA TOOLING LANDSCAPE COMPARISON (COMPARISON TABLE)\n794: Support Tool\n795: Primary Focus\n796: Target Environment\n797: Key Strength\n798: LeanIX\n799: Application Portfolio\n800: Management\n801: Agile, cloud-native enterprise IT\n802: Fast SaaS setup, automated\n803: landscape discovery\n804: Sparx Enterprise Architect\n805: Deep Modeling & System\n806: Specs\n807: Complex software & systems\n808: engineering\n809: Comprehensive support for\n810: TOGAF, ArchiMate, UML,\n811: SysML\n812: Software AG Alfabet\n813: Strategic IT Governance &\n814: Portfolio\n815: Large enterprise & financial\n816: institutions\n817: Robust financial planning, risk\n818: & investment alignment\n819: Archi (Open Source)\n820: ArchiMate Modeling\n821: Educational, research, and lean\n822: teams\n823: Free, open-source, lightweight\n824: graphical modeling\n825: REPOSITORY GOVERNANCE LIFECYCLE (4 STAGES)\n826: 1. Data Collection — generates dynamic visual models directly from repository data, eliminating manual drawing and\n827: keeping diagrams synchronized with data.\n828: 2. Compliance Check — performs traceability queries to evaluate how replacing/modifying an application impacts\n829: connected systems and business operations.\n830: 3. Central Staging — provides role-based access control (RBAC), approval workflows, and audit trails for Architecture\n831: Review Board (ARB) approvals.\n832: 4. Continuous Audit — connects via APIs to cloud platforms, CMDBs, and source repositories to continuously refresh\n833: landscape data.\n834: INTEGRATING EA WITH OPERATIONAL IT\n835: SDLC & DevOps Alignment — integrating the EA repository with project management and delivery tools (such as Jira,\n836: Confluence, and CI/CD pipelines) ensures developer compliance with approved target architectures. Architectural guardrails\n837: are pushed directly into developer workflows, preventing accidental creation of technical debt during sprint cycles.\n838: CMDB & ITSM Synchronization — bi-directional integration with Configuration Management Databases (CMDBs) like\n839: ServiceNow or AWS Config reconciles planned architecture with actual runtime reality — providing live verification that\n840: deployed physical assets match the strategic models stored in the EA repository.\n841: \n842: ===== PAGE 16 =====\n843: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n844: Page 16 of 43\n845: L5: ALL ABOUT SERVICE-ORIENTED ARCHITECTURE (SOA)\n846: WHAT IS SOA?\n847: SOA (Service-Oriented Architecture) — an approach in software development where we use 'services' to create our\n848: applications.\n849: Service — a software component we use that is assigned a task. We use services in order to make components reusable and\n850: modular, making developing faster.\n851: What counts as a \"service\" in software systems: performs a specific function; has a clearly defined interface; is usable by\n852: other parts of the system; hides its internal implementation from its consumers; is relatively independent from other services;\n853: communicates through a defined method or protocol.\n854: EXAMPLE — online shopping system: User Service (manages user accounts), Product Service (manages products), Order\n855: Service (processes orders), Payment Service (handles payments), Shipping Service (handles delivery).\n856: CORE PRINCIPLES OF SOA (4 — MEMORIZE)\n857: 1. REUSABILITY — use a service in multiple parts of the system (e.g., Website -> Payment Service <- Mobile App). This\n858: prevents developers from creating the same functionality multiple times.\n859: 2. LOOSE COUPLING — proper documentation and description of the service; changes in one service do not affect other\n860: components using the service. AWS: \"...having as little dependency as possible on external resources ... They should also be\n861: stateless without retaining any information from past sessions or transactions.\" Example: an Order Service can request a\n862: payment from a Payment Service without knowing how it processes the payment internally.\n863: 3. ABSTRACTION — customers & clients should not be able to see important logic/code. The app only needs to know how\n864: to request a payment, what information to provide, and what response to expect — not the internal logic.\n865: 4. MODULARITY — SOA divides a large system into smaller, manageable services, each with its own responsibility (e.g.,\n866: large online shopping system split into User, Product, Order, Payment, Shipping services) — easier to develop, maintain, and\n867: modify.\n868: THE MAIN COMPONENTS OF SOA (3 — MEMORIZE)\n869: 1. Service Provider — creates, maintains, and provides a service (e.g., a company provides a Payment Service that other\n870: applications can use).\n871: 2. Service Consumer — the application or system that requests and uses a service (e.g., an online shopping website\n872: requests the Payment Service to process a customer's payment).\n873: 3. Service Registry — acts as a directory of available services; contains information about what services are available and\n874: how consumers can access them.\n875: HOW SOA WORKS\n876: SOA breaks an application into independent, reusable services that each handle one business function and talk to each other\n877: over a network — rather than living inside one large, tightly-wired program.\n878: How independent services communicate:\n879: • Synchronous — the caller sends a request and waits for an immediate reply before continuing — common with REST\n880: APIs.\n881: • Asynchronous / Message-Based — the caller sends a message and keeps working; a response, if any, arrives later via a\n882: queue or bus.\n883: • Service Discovery — consumers locate providers at runtime through a service registry (e.g., UDDI) instead of a\n884: hard-coded address.\n885: COMMON COMMUNICATION PROTOCOLS: REST vs SOAP (COMPARISON)\n886: Aspect\n887: REST\n888: SOAP\n889: Full name\n890: Representational State Transfer\n891: Simple Object Access Protocol\n892: Type\n893: An architectural style, not a strict standard\n894: A strict, formal messaging standard\n895: \n896: ===== PAGE 17 =====\n897: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n898: Page 17 of 43\n899: Aspect\n900: REST\n\n(Showing lines 401-900 of 2262. Use offset=901 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"Why Traceability Matters: Impact Analysis (MEMORIZE)\n• Proactive Risk Mitigation — e.g., deprecating an AWS server instance instantly reveals which applications, data entities,\nand business processes will be disrupted.\n• Cost Optimization — leaders identify redundant applications or underutilized infrastructure by tracking them back to the\nbusiness capabilities they support (or fail to support).\n• Strategic Agility — when executive leadership shifts strategy, cross-layer matrices pinpoint exactly which data models,\nsoftware integrations, and cloud resources need updating.\n\n===== PAGE 8 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 8 of 43\nL3: DEVELOPING AN ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE\nDOCUMENTATION\nKey concept — Every organization already has an architecture. Every organization runs on a combination of systems,\nprocesses, and infrastructure, regardless of whether any of it has been formally documented. Systems are added over time, by\ndifferent teams, for different reasons, often without a shared record of how they connect.\nThe practical consequence: without documentation, decision-makers work from partial or outdated information about their\nown organization.\nWhy documentation comes before planning\n• Plans built without an accurate baseline tend to underestimate cost, timeline, and complexity.","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt","text":"Why Traceability Matters: Impact Analysis (MEMORIZE)\n• Proactive Risk Mitigation — e.g., deprecating an AWS server instance instantly reveals which applications, data entities,\nand business processes will be disrupted.\n• Cost Optimization — leaders identify redundant applications or underutilized infrastructure by tracking them back to the\nbusiness capabilities they support (or fail to support).\n• Strategic Agility — when executive leadership shifts strategy, cross-layer matrices pinpoint exactly which data models,\nsoftware integrations, and cloud resources need updating.\n\n===== PAGE 8 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 8 of 43\nL3: DEVELOPING AN ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE\nDOCUMENTATION\nKey concept — Every organization already has an architecture. Every organization runs on a combination of systems,\nprocesses, and infrastructure, regardless of whether any of it has been formally documented. Systems are added over time, by\ndifferent teams, for different reasons, often without a shared record of how they connect.\nThe practical consequence: without documentation, decision-makers work from partial or outdated information about their\nown organization.\nWhy documentation comes before planning\n• Plans built without an accurate baseline tend to underestimate cost, timeline, and complexity.\n• Any structured planning process starts with an accurate assessment of the current situation.\n• Organizations cannot reliably plan system upgrades, budget allocations, or process changes without first knowing what\nalready exists.\nDocumentation and its role\n• Systems and processes change regularly, but documentation is often updated far less frequently -> documentation and\nreality drift apart.\n• Documentation is frequently treated as a one-time deliverable rather than an ongoing responsibility.\nDifferent stakeholders, different needs\n• Technical Staff — need clear, accurate detail on existing systems before making changes or building integrations.\n• Leadership — need comparable, reliable information to justify budget and resource decisions.\n• Audit and Compliance — need a documented record of what changed, when, and why.\nCURRENT OPERATIONS AND FUTURE PLANNING\nOrganizations must maintain what is currently running while planning what comes next. Future planning tends to get more\nattention and investment than current-state documentation, even though both are necessary.\nCurrent Architectural Views (\"As-Is\" state) — like taking a complete snapshot of a company's technology and processes right\nnow. Why it matters: we need to know exactly what we have before we build or change anything. The Baseline: prevents\ncrashing the system when adding new features. The Blueprint: gives developers and planners a safe, reliable map to work from.\nKeeping track of changes (EACR)\n• Standard Format (EACR) — companies use an EA Change Request to update systems smoothly (like turning in a\nstandardized college paper).\n• Electronic Template — making forms digital and easy to use encourages stakeholders to participate and follow the rules.\n• The History Book — archiving these requests creates a detailed history guide for developers to follow during\nimplementation.\nThe Tech Inventory — EA acts as a giant checklist for all the hardware (computers, servers) and software a company owns.\n• Smart Budgeting — tells leaders how much money to set aside for maintenance, daily operations, and training.\n• Justifying Upgrades — provides concrete proof when authorizing new technology purchases.\n• Automated Tracking — uses barcodes and software systems to keep the list 100% accurate.\nMapping out activities (IDEF-O)\n• Inputs & Outputs — visual models (like IDEF-O) clearly show what data goes in and what comes out.\n• The Rules — explicitly defines the \"Controls\" (rules to follow) and the \"Mechanisms\" (tools to use).\n• No Guessing — acts as a strict, foolproof blueprint so developers don't guess when writing code.\nFollowing the data flow — see exactly how a user's request routes through active services (Visualizing Paths); mapping the\nflow before building prevents accidental cutting of vital connections (Playing it Safe); shows step-by-step how data is consumed,\ntransformed, and outputted (Process Logic).\nConnecting the dots — ERDs (Entity-Relationship Diagrams) define how different parts of the business relate (e.g., how a\n'Buyer' connects to a 'Listing'). Leaders spot missing links (Finding the Gaps) and mastering the \"as-is\" state supports strategic\ndecisions.\n\n===== PAGE 9 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 9 of 43\nFUTURE ARCHITECTURAL VIEWS\nThe future view documents the IT resources expected to be active several years ahead — as opposed to the \"as-is\" view,\nwhich documents what exists today.\n• Why gaps appear: without deliberate EA planning, future IT resources drift out of alignment with strategic goals and\nbusiness services, and duplication builds up. A well-built future view shows exactly how that duplication will be eliminated.\n• Same method, same levels: future artifacts are built using the same planning assumptions, documentation, and modeling\ntechniques as current-view artifacts — at every level — so change is always traceable.\n• Analogy: \"Having future EA views is like having a full set of blueprints for the modification of an existing home.\"\nWhy future architectural views matter (MEMORIZE)\n• Resource Planning — a coherent view of changing priorities, processes, and resources across the enterprise.\n• Decision-Making Support — compare current vs. planned states side by side before committing budget or staff.\n• Enterprise-Wide Communication — a shared \"to-be\" picture keeps business units and IT teams talking about the same\ntarget.\n• Upward & Downward Alignment — approved future changes stay traceable to strategic goals above and implementation\ndetail below.\nDEVELOPING A CONOPS SCENARIO (Concept of Operations)\nWhat a CONOPS scenario documents — good and bad operating environments, drawn from the SWOT Analysis, so the\nenterprise can plan probable defensive responses and offensive initiatives in advance.\nThe Storytelling Technique (Dr. Robert Neilson) — a short story told through the eyes of a central character living through\nfuture events, tied to specific planning assumptions in three areas of change:\n• Process — How it gets done\n• People — Who is involved\n• Technology — What tools are needed\nStories are easy to remember — and highlighting the assumptions reveals exactly which resources and capabilities the scenario\nneeds.\nThe \"Chess\" Analogy — anticipating how an opponent might react to your moves is part of what makes a chess master;\ntop-level play means thinking through several alternative sequences of moves and counter-moves — a dynamic balance between\nacting and reacting.\nFUTURE ARTIFACTS (FIVE LEVELS — MEMORIZE)\nStrategic Level — \"Directions and priorities\"\n• Documents the enterprise's overarching goals, direction, and priorities that guide every other level of planning.\n• EA Components: Strategic Plan; E-Commerce / E-Government Plan.\n• Future-View Artifacts: Strategic Plan, SWOT Analysis, CONOPS Scenario, CONOPS Diagram, Balanced Scorecard.\n• EXAMPLE: \"Grow e-government service adoption 30% in 3 years\" — tracked on the Balanced Scorecard.\nBusiness Level — \"Services and processes\"\n• Documents the products, services, and processes the enterprise uses to carry out its strategic goals.\n• EA Components: Supply Chains, Business Services, IT Capital Planning Portfolio.\n• Future-View Artifacts: Business Plan, Node Connectivity Diagram, Swim Lane Process Diagram, Business Process /\nService Model, Use Case Narrative & Diagram, Investment Business Case.\n• EXAMPLE: a Swim Lane Diagram tracing a permit application from Citizen -> Apply -> Portal -> Submit -> Records Office.\nInformation Level — \"Knowledge, systems, databases\"\n• Documents the data, knowledge, and information flows that support the enterprise's business services.\n• EA Components: Knowledge Warehouses, Information Systems, Databases.\n• Future-View Artifacts: Knowledge Mgmt. Plan, Information Exchange Matrix, Logical / Physical Data Model, Activity/Entity\n(CRUD) Matrix, Data Dictionary / Object Library.\n\n===== PAGE 10 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 10 of 43\n• EXAMPLE: CRUD Matrix entry: 'Permit Record' — Created by Intake, Updated by Reviewer, Deleted by Records Admin.\nSystems / Services Level — \"Applications and integrations\"\n• Documents the software applications and services that create, process, and exchange information.\n• EA Components: Software Applications, Web Services, Service Bus & Middleware, ERP Solutions.\n• Future-View Artifacts: System Interface Diagram, System Data Flow Diagram, System/Operations Matrix, System\nEvolution Diagram, Web Application Diagram.\n• EXAMPLE: a System Interface Diagram showing the Permit Portal calling a Payment Gateway API.\nInfrastructure Level — \"Data, telecom, video, cable plant, and security\"\n• Documents the physical networks, hardware, and facilities — voice, data, and video — that support enterprise systems.\n• Key Future-View Artifacts: Network Connectivity Diagram, Network Inventory, Capital Equipment Inventory, Building\nBlueprints, Rack Elevation Diagram.\n• Focuses on changes to cable plant, wiring closets, backbone hardware, servers, and remote access resources.\n• EXAMPLE: a Network Connectivity Diagram showing a new fiber link between City Hall and the Data Center.\nKEEPING FUTURE VIEWS TRUSTWORTHY — FREEZE PERIODS\nThe Challenge: as changes are implemented, they move from the future view into the current view — so at any moment, what's\n\"current\" and what's \"planned\" can blur.\nThe Fix — Freeze Periods: freeze the current and future views at regular intervals — e.g., twice a year. Changes accumulate\nfor 4–5 months, then publish as a new release in month six.\nA BROADER PERSPECTIVE: TOGAF'S VIEW — THREE ARCHITECTURE STATES\nTOGAF formalizes the \"current vs. future view\" as three explicit architecture states:\n1. Baseline Architecture — the current, \"as-is\" structure of the enterprise; the starting point for any transformation.\n2. Transition Architecture — one or more intermediate states that bridge the gap between baseline and target on a realistic\ntimeline.\n3. Target Architecture — the desired future state: capabilities, processes, and technologies aligned to strategic goals.\nWhether it's called a \"future view\" (EA3 Cube) or a \"target architecture\" (TOGAF), the core discipline is the same: document\nwhere you're headed with the same rigor used to document where you are.\nENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE MANAGEMENT PLAN\nEA Management Plan — guides the transition from the current architecture to the desired future architecture; it coordinates IT\nprojects, resources, standards, governance, and implementation activities. It is a living document that is regularly updated.\nPurpose of the EA Management Plan\n• Coordinates multiple IT implementation and upgrade projects.\n• Supports prioritization, decision-making, and oversight.\n• Connects business strategy with processes, systems, information, and technology.\n• Provides a roadmap for moving from the current EA to the future EA.\n• Analogy: functions like an architect's project plan for remodeling a house — showing the design, approach, schedule, and\nsequence of work.\nMAIN PARTS OF AN EA MANAGEMENT PLAN (MEMORIZE — 4)\n1. EA Program Management\n2. Current Architecture Summary\n3. Future Architecture Summary\n4. Glossary and References\nTogether, these sections explain how the EA program is governed, what currently exists, what must change, and how the plan's\nterminology is defined.\n\n===== PAGE 11 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 11 of 43\n1. EA PROGRAM MANAGEMENT\nExplains how the EA program will be managed.\n• Governance and principles — establishes policies and decision-making procedures.\n• Strategy and business support — ensures EA activities support organizational goals.\n• Roles and responsibilities — defines the duties of sponsors, CIOs, architects, managers, analysts, and other\nstakeholders.\n• Program budget — documents annual and long-term costs, including total cost of ownership.\n• Performance measures — evaluates whether the EA program is effective and efficient.\nEA governance, roles, and performance measures\n• EA principles may promote information sharing, stakeholder participation, open standards, and the use of IT as a means to\nachieve business goals.\n• The Chief Architect manages the EA program, documentation, standards, and configuration management.\n• Business and technical architects identify requirements, analyze problems, and design solutions.\n• Performance measures: Outcome measures (improvements in integration, user satisfaction, or IT investment decisions)\nand Output measures (number of projects completed, scope changes, or planning activities).\n2. CURRENT ARCHITECTURE SUMMARY\nDescribes how existing resources support the organization. It includes: strategic goals and initiatives; business services and\ninformation flows; systems and applications; technology infrastructure; IT security; EA standards; workforce skills and training\nrequirements. Helps identify the current role, strengths, weaknesses, and performance gaps of IT.\nCurrent architecture analysis examines: how EA components support strategic goals and business processes; how\ninformation moves between departments and systems; whether applications are integrated, scalable, cost-effective, and\nuser-friendly; whether networks and infrastructure reliably support voice, data, video, and mobile services; whether technical\nstandards are followed; what skills and training employees need. NOTE: sensitive security details should be documented in a\nseparate IT Security Plan to avoid exposing vulnerabilities.\n3. FUTURE ARCHITECTURE SUMMARY\nDescribes the desired direction of the enterprise.\n• Future operating scenarios — possible business environments (status quo, aggressive growth, or responding\ndefensively to a market downturn).\n• Planning assumptions — expected changes in business processes, people, technology, and resources.\n• Updated architecture views — planned changes documented from strategic goals down to technology components.\n• The selected scenario becomes the basis for future EA decisions.\nSEQUENCING AND CONFIGURATION MANAGEMENT\n• EA Sequencing Plan — lists projects, tasks, milestones, dependencies, and timeframes; ensures projects occur in the\ncorrect order; prevents problems caused by implementing dependent systems too early; helps coordinate upgrades,\nmigrations, consolidations, and retirements.\n• Configuration Management — reviews and controls proposed EA changes; applies technical standards; manages\nwaivers and approval deadlines; maintains documentation and version control; uses an Enterprise Architecture Change\nRequest reviewed by the Chief Architect and stakeholders.\nL3 KEY TAKEAWAYS\n• The EA Management Plan is a roadmap for managing enterprise-wide architectural change.\n• It links business strategy to IT systems, information, infrastructure, and workforce requirements.\n• It documents both current conditions and future goals.\n• Sequencing ensures implementation projects are coordinated.\n• Configuration management protects consistency, standards, and version control.\n• Because the enterprise continuously changes, the plan must be regularly updated and maintained in an EA repository.\n\n===== PAGE 12 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 12 of 43\nL4: USING AN ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE\nINTRODUCTION\nEnterprise Architecture (EA) — a structured approach to organizing an organization's business, data, applications, and\ntechnology. It provides a blueprint that shows: how different parts of an organization work together; how technology supports\nbusiness goals; how systems and processes should be designed; how changes and improvements should be managed.\nWhy do we need to use EA?\n• Without EA: systems become disorganized; departments may use incompatible systems; projects can exceed time and\nbudget; security risks increase; information becomes difficult to manage.\n• With EA: better planning; system integration; improved security; reduced duplication; better decision-making; alignment\nbetween IT and business goals.\nRemember the three areas as a simple sequence: Plan, Protect, and Manage.\n• 4.1 Planning & Project Management — projects must be determined by objectives, requirements, resources, schedules,\nbudgets, and potential risks.\n• 4.2 Security — how systems, applications, networks, and information will be protected (access control, data protection,\nsecurity policies, managing risks).\n• 4.3 Repository & Support Tools — a repository where EA information (diagrams, documentation, standards, policies,\nsystem descriptions, models) can be stored and maintained.\nREAL-LIFE EXAMPLE: AMAZON — Enterprise Architecture in Action\nAmazon's ability to serve millions of customers depends on many interconnected systems.\nHow one customer order is fulfilled (Enterprise Architecture view):\n1. Customer Places Order\n2. E-Commerce System\n3. Payment System\n4. Inventory System\n5. Fulfillment System\n6. Logistics System\n7. Customer Receives Order\nBusiness Goals: Customer obsession, Operational excellence, Innovation, Long-term value.\nPLANNING & PROJECT MANAGEMENT\n• An EA only creates value when it is executed.\n• Planning — What & Why: converts the gap between the As-Is and the To-Be architecture into a list of initiatives, and\ndecides which goes first.\n• Project Management — How & When: controls scope, schedule, cost, quality, resources, and risk so every initiative is\ndelivered as designed.\n• Why it matters: stops duplicate and siloed systems from being bought and built; ties every peso of IT spending to a\nbusiness goal; turns the blueprint into a fundable, dated roadmap.\nENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE PLANNING (EAP)\nEAP — the planning discipline that sits between the architecture and the projects. Definition (Spewak, 1992): \"Defining the\narchitectures needed for the business use of information — and the plan to implement them.\"\nNote the two halves: an architecture AND a plan. Most failed EA efforts produce only the first — plenty of diagrams, no\nsequence, no budget, no owner.\nThe Guiding Principle — MEMORIZE:\nBUSINESS -> DATA -> APPLICATIONS -> TECHNOLOGY\nApplications manage data. Technology supports applications. Everything must serve a business process. EAP is\nbusiness-driven and data-driven — never technology-driven. Reverse the order and you get a solution hunting for a problem.\n\n===== PAGE 13 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 13 of 43\nHow EAP works — Four Layers\n1. Getting started — planning initiation: scope, executive sponsor, team, methodology, tools, and a dated workplan.\nSkipping this is the most common cause of a stalled EA effort.\n2. Where we are today — business modelling plus a survey of current systems and technology, collected into an\nInformation Resource Catalog. This is where the duplicates surface.\n3. Where we want to be — data architecture first, then applications, then technology. These describe WHAT is needed, not\nhow it will be coded.\n4. How we get there — the implementation and migration plan: sequence, cost, benefit, resources, and roadmap. This is\nwhere architecture finally becomes a budget.\nSEQUENCING & PRIORITIZATION — Three More Tests\n• Dependency — foundations before features: no dashboard without a clean data source.\n• Risk & complexity — high technical risk? Pilot first, then roll out.\n• Compliance deadline — non-negotiable work, e.g., the Data Privacy Act.\n• Rule of thumb: never run two projects that change the same system at the same time.\nFROM ARCHITECTURE TO PROJECTS (4 steps — MEMORIZE)\n1. Gap Analysis — compare the As-Is against the To-Be to find what is missing, duplicated, and obsolete. Every project\nmust trace back to a proven gap.\n2. Work Packages — group related gaps into small deliverables. Each package gets a clear scope, a single owner, and one\nconcrete outcome.\n3. Business Case — attach cost, benefit, and risk to each package. Leadership funds outcomes, not diagrams.\n4. Roadmap — place the packages on a timeline with transition architectures in between, so the business keeps running\nwhile it changes.\nWHO DOES WHAT (COMPARISON)\nRole\nCares about\nAsks\nHorizon\nDelivers\nEnterprise\nArchitecture Team\nLong-term coherence of\nthe whole enterprise\n\"Does this fit the target\narchitecture?\"\nSeveral years, many\nprojects\nPrinciples, standards,\nreference models,\nroadmap\nProject Manager /\nPMO\nDelivering this one\nproject\n\"Are we on scope,\nschedule, and budget?\"\nLife of a single project\nProject plans, status\nreports, the working\nsystem\nArchitecture Review\nBoard (ARB)\nCheckpoints at initiation,\nat design, and before\ngo-live\nEach review ends in\napproved / approved\nwith conditions /\nrejected\nPer review\nAny deviation needs a\ndocumented exception\nwith an expiry date —\notherwise the\n\"temporary fix\" becomes\npermanent technical\ndebt\nPITFALLS, SUCCESS FACTORS & METRICS (MEMORIZE)\nCommon Pitfalls (4):\n1. Ivory-tower architecture — beautiful models nobody follows\n2. No executive sponsor — the plan becomes a suggestion\n3. Big-bang scope — everything at once, nothing finished\n4. Stale artifacts — six months later, nobody trusts them\nSuccess Factors (4):\n1. Active sponsor — someone who can decide and fund\n2. Small increments — visible wins every few months\n3. Reviews at project gates — part of approval, not optional\n\n===== PAGE 14 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 14 of 43\n4. A living repository — owned, updated, and used\nHow we measure it:\n• % of projects architecturally compliant\n• Redundant apps retired or consolidated\n• Delivery time per work package\n• Benefits realized vs the business case\nSECURITY IN ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE\nSecurity means keeping the architecture and everything built on it safe from unauthorized access, misuse, or damage. It's part\nof using the EA, not a separate topic. After the architecture is built, it becomes \"live\" — real data, real systems, real users.\nSecurity should be considered while implementing the architecture, not after something goes wrong.\nThe 4 Main Areas of Security (MEMORIZE):\n1. Data Security — controlling who can see, change, or take the organization's data.\n2. Network/Technology Security — protecting servers, connections, and infrastructure.\n3. Application Security — protecting the software/systems people use daily.\n4. Physical Security — protecting the actual hardware and facilities.\nControlling who can access what\n• Security policies — the organization maps out its data, applications, and technology, then puts the plan into use.\n• Role-based access control (RBAC) — people only get access to what they need for their job (e.g., HR staff can't touch\nfinance data).\n• Authentication — verifying who a user is before letting them in (passwords, ID checks, etc.).\nSecurity needs planning too — security tasks should be part of the project timeline, not an afterthought (setting rules, assigning\naccess, testing systems); needs teamwork between the EA/IT team and other departments; if security issues are caught too late,\nit can delay the whole project.\nWhat happens if security is ignored — data can be stolen, leaked, or changed by the wrong people; systems can be disrupted\ncausing downtime and lost work; the organization can lose the trust of employees, clients, or partners.\nKey takeaway — Security protects the value of everything the EA was built to do. It's an ongoing job — not something you do\nonce and forget. A strong architecture is only as good as the security protecting it.\nCORE FUNCTION OF AN EA REPOSITORY\nCentral Knowledge Base — an EA Repository serves as the master storage facility for all architectural assets, models,\nprinciples, and guidelines across the enterprise. It maintains single-source-of-truth visibility for Current (As-Is), Future (To-Be),\nand Transition states across Business, Data, Application, and Infrastructure domains.\nStrategic Decision Support — by capturing dependencies between business processes and underlying technology, the\nrepository powers dependency mapping, impact analysis, and gap assessments — helping leadership evaluate downstream\ntechnical risk and strategic fit before capital is allocated.\nCore Repository Components (3):\n1. Architecture Metamodel — defines the underlying schema, entity types, taxonomy, and relationship rules governing how\narchitectural artifacts connect to one another.\n2. Architecture Landscape — catalogs real-time operational views, target blueprints, and active roadmap transitions across\nall business units and IT domains.\n3. Governance & Standards — houses regulatory compliance frameworks, technology reference models (TRMs), design\nprinciples, and architecture board review logs.\nManaging architecture artifacts — Catalogs (structured inventories: application catalogs, business capabilities, server asset\nlists), Matrices (cross-mapping tables: Application-to-Data CRUD matrix, Role-to-Process matrix), Diagrams (graphical\nrepresentations of topologies, integration flows, and process models).\nKEY CAPABILITIES OF EA SUPPORT TOOLS (4 — MEMORIZE)\n1. Automated Diagramming & Layout — generates dynamic visual models directly from repository data, eliminating\nmanual drawing and keeping diagrams synchronized with data.\n\n===== PAGE 15 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 15 of 43\n2. Impact & Risk Analysis — performs traceability queries to evaluate how replacing or modifying an application impacts\nconnected systems and business operations.\n3. Collaborative Governance — provides role-based access control (RBAC), approval workflows, and audit trails for\nArchitecture Review Board (ARB) approvals.\n4. Automated Data Ingestion — connects via APIs to cloud platforms, CMDBs, and source repositories to continuously\nrefresh landscape data.\nEA TOOLING LANDSCAPE COMPARISON (COMPARISON TABLE)\nSupport Tool\nPrimary Focus\nTarget Environment\nKey Strength\nLeanIX\nApplication Portfolio\nManagement\nAgile, cloud-native enterprise IT\nFast SaaS setup, automated\nlandscape discovery\nSparx Enterprise Architect\nDeep Modeling & System\nSpecs\nComplex software & systems\nengineering\nComprehensive support for\nTOGAF, ArchiMate, UML,\nSysML\nSoftware AG Alfabet\nStrategic IT Governance &\nPortfolio\nLarge enterprise & financial\ninstitutions\nRobust financial planning, risk\n& investment alignment\nArchi (Open Source)\nArchiMate Modeling\nEducational, research, and lean\nteams\nFree, open-source, lightweight\ngraphical modeling\nREPOSITORY GOVERNANCE LIFECYCLE (4 STAGES)\n1. Data Collection — generates dynamic visual models directly from repository data, eliminating manual drawing and\nkeeping diagrams synchronized with data.\n2. Compliance Check — performs traceability queries to evaluate how replacing/modifying an application impacts\nconnected systems and business operations.\n3. Central Staging — provides role-based access control (RBAC), approval workflows, and audit trails for Architecture\nReview Board (ARB) approvals.\n4. Continuous Audit — connects via APIs to cloud platforms, CMDBs, and source repositories to continuously refresh\nlandscape data.\nINTEGRATING EA WITH OPERATIONAL IT\nSDLC & DevOps Alignment — integrating the EA repository with project management and delivery tools (such as Jira,\nConfluence, and CI/CD pipelines) ensures developer compliance with approved target architectures. Architectural guardrails\nare pushed directly into developer workflows, preventing accidental creation of technical debt during sprint cycles.\nCMDB & ITSM Synchronization — bi-directional integration with Configuration Management Databases (CMDBs) like\nServiceNow or AWS Config reconciles planned architecture with actual runtime reality — providing live verification that\ndeployed physical assets match the strategic models stored in the EA repository.\n\n===== PAGE 16 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 16 of 43\nL5: ALL ABOUT SERVICE-ORIENTED ARCHITECTURE (SOA)\nWHAT IS SOA?\nSOA (Service-Oriented Architecture) — an approach in software development where we use 'services' to create our\napplications.\nService — a software component we use that is assigned a task. We use services in order to make components reusable and\nmodular, making developing faster.\nWhat counts as a \"service\" in software systems: performs a specific function; has a clearly defined interface; is usable by\nother parts of the system; hides its internal implementation from its consumers; is relatively independent from other services;\ncommunicates through a defined method or protocol.\nEXAMPLE — online shopping system: User Service (manages user accounts), Product Service (manages products), Order\nService (processes orders), Payment Service (handles payments), Shipping Service (handles delivery).\nCORE PRINCIPLES OF SOA (4 — MEMORIZE)\n1. REUSABILITY — use a service in multiple parts of the system (e.g., Website -> Payment Service <- Mobile App). This\nprevents developers from creating the same functionality multiple times.\n2. LOOSE COUPLING — proper documentation and description of the service; changes in one service do not affect other\ncomponents using the service. AWS: \"...having as little dependency as possible on external resources ... They should also be\nstateless without retaining any information from past sessions or transactions.\" Example: an Order Service can request a\npayment from a Payment Service without knowing how it processes the payment internally.\n3. ABSTRACTION — customers & clients should not be able to see important logic/code. The app only needs to know how\nto request a payment, what information to provide, and what response to expect — not the internal logic.\n4. MODULARITY — SOA divides a large system into smaller, manageable services, each with its own responsibility (e.g.,\nlarge online shopping system split into User, Product, Order, Payment, Shipping services) — easier to develop, maintain, and\nmodify.\nTHE MAIN COMPONENTS OF SOA (3 — MEMORIZE)\n1. Service Provider — creates, maintains, and provides a service (e.g., a company provides a Payment Service that other\napplications can use).\n2. Service Consumer — the application or system that requests and uses a service (e.g., an online shopping website\nrequests the Payment Service to process a customer's payment).\n3. Service Registry — acts as a directory of available services; contains information about what services are available and\nhow consumers can access them.\nHOW SOA WORKS\nSOA breaks an application into independent, reusable services that each handle one business function and talk to each other\nover a network — rather than living inside one large, tightly-wired program.\nHow independent services communicate:\n• Synchronous — the caller sends a request and waits for an immediate reply before continuing — common with REST\nAPIs.\n• Asynchronous / Message-Based — the caller sends a message and keeps working; a response, if any, arrives later via a\nqueue or bus.\n• Service Discovery — consumers locate providers at runtime through a service registry (e.g., UDDI) instead of a\nhard-coded address.\nCOMMON COMMUNICATION PROTOCOLS: REST vs SOAP (COMPARISON)\nAspect\nREST\nSOAP\nFull name\nRepresentational State Transfer\nSimple Object Access Protocol\nType\nAn architectural style, not a strict standard\nA strict, formal messaging standard\n\n===== PAGE 17 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 17 of 43\nAspect\nREST","lineStart":401,"lineEnd":900,"totalLines":2262,"truncated":true}},"title":"xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt","time":{"start":1789817812980,"end":1789817812994}},"id":"prt_0b9747708001tdipiJttPRWd4e","sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","messageID":"msg_0b9746ef9001fONNr605Orwshm"}}
