{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1789817813073,"sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","part":{"id":"prt_0b9747846001KIdDDP66S7x7pS","reason":"tool-calls","messageID":"msg_0b9746ef9001fONNr605Orwshm","sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":41587,"input":5533,"output":214,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":35840}},"cost":0}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1789817814148,"sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","part":{"id":"prt_0b9747c7c001jvIE4P1HPl98jA","messageID":"msg_0b974784e001thYYsVmp7MetnP","sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789817814715,"sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_1beb211554f64bc28e3fae5b","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt","limit":500,"offset":901},"output":"<path>C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n901: SOAP\n902: Format\n903: Typically JSON (lightweight)\n904: Messages always formatted as XML\n905: envelopes\n906: Operations\n907: Uses standard HTTP verbs: GET, POST,\n908: PUT, DELETE\n909: Works over HTTP, SMTP, and other\n910: transport protocols\n911: State / Security\n912: Stateless — each request contains\n913: everything needed to process it\n914: Built-in standards for security (WS-Security)\n915: and transactions\n916: Best for\n917: Simpler, faster, widely used for web &\n918: mobile APIs\n919: Enterprise systems needing high reliability\n920: (banking, finance)\n921: COMMON DATA FORMATS: XML vs JSON (COMPARISON)\n922: Aspect\n923: XML\n924: JSON\n925: Structure\n926: Verbose, tag-based markup\n927: Lightweight, key-value structure\n928: Validation\n929: Strong support for schemas/validation\n930: Maps directly to objects in most\n931: programming languages\n932: Usage\n933: Common in SOAP messages & legacy\n934: enterprise systems\n935: The default format for REST APIs\n936: THE ENTERPRISE SERVICE BUS (ESB)\n937: ESB (Enterprise Service Bus) — a piece of middleware that sits between services, handling routing, translation, and message\n938: delivery so that no service ever has to know the technical details of any other service it talks to. Instead of every application\n939: connecting directly to every other application, each one only needs to know how to talk to the bus.\n940: Core Functions of an ESB (6 — MEMORIZE):\n941: 1. Routing — directs each incoming message to the correct destination service based on content or business rules.\n942: 2. Transformation — converts data formats between systems, such as XML <-> JSON, so mismatched services can still\n943: communicate.\n944: 3. Protocol Mediation — bridges services that use different transport protocols, such as HTTP, JMS, or FTP.\n945: 4. Orchestration — coordinates calls to multiple services in sequence to complete a single business process.\n946: 5. Monitoring & Logging — tracks message flow, errors, and performance across the entire system in real time.\n947: 6. Security Enforcement — applies authentication, authorization, and encryption to messages as they pass through.\n948: Diagram understanding: Web App, Mobile App, CRM System, and Legacy System all connect to the Enterprise Service Bus,\n949: which connects to Payment, Inventory, and Shipping Services. All communication flows through the ESB — no service calls\n950: another directly.\n951: REAL-WORLD EXAMPLES OF SOA IN APPS (6)\n952: 1. Online Shopping — separate services for catalog search, cart, payment, and shipment tracking, combined at checkout.\n953: 2. Banking Systems — one banking app calls independent services for balances, transfers, loan processing, and fraud\n954: detection.\n955: 3. Airline Booking — ticketing, seat selection, payment, and loyalty-point services behind one booking interface.\n956: 4. Streaming Platforms — recommendation, billing, content-delivery, and user-profile services combine into one app.\n957: 5. Ride-Hailing Apps — mapping, pricing, driver-matching, and payment services communicate in real time for a single ride.\n958: 6. Healthcare Records — patient records, billing, and scheduling systems exchange data through shared services.\n959: ANALYZING SOA\n960: Key concept — evaluating SOA requires understanding both its benefits and complexities. While SOA enables rapid scale and\n961: iteration, it fundamentally shifts the burden from code complexity to network complexity.\n962: Primary Business Benefits (MEMORIZE):\n963: \n964: ===== PAGE 18 =====\n965: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n966: Page 18 of 43\n967: • AGILITY — teams can develop, update, and deploy individual services independently -> drastically reduces time to bring\n968: new features to market.\n969: • REUSABILITY — services are designed as self-contained business functions; a user authentication service can be reused\n970: across multiple applications.\n971: • SCALABILITY — scale only the specific services under high demand instead of the entire application -> optimizes\n972: resource usage and costs.\n973: Technical Advantages:\n974: • Technology Diversity — services can be written in entirely different programming languages (polyglot programming)\n975: since they communicate over standard protocols (like HTTP).\n976: • Fault Isolation — if one service crashes, the rest of the application can often continue to function.\n977: • Streamlined Maintenance — smaller codebases make it easier to understand logic, find bugs, and perform maintenance.\n978: • Independent Deployment — updates can be pushed to specific services without taking the whole system offline.\n979: Inherent Drawbacks & Challenges:\n980: • Network Overhead — heavy reliance on network communication introduces unavoidable latency compared to in-memory\n981: function calls; securing data in transit adds complex authentication and encryption challenges.\n982: • System Complexity — individual services are simple, but the system as a whole becomes highly complex\n983: (dozens/hundreds of independent moving parts); monitoring, debugging, and tracing across service boundaries requires\n984: specialized tooling and expertise.\n985: • Anything large scale requires a whole lot of maintenance.\n986: When to choose SOA? Many systems need to connect; services need to be reused; different systems need to share data; old\n987: systems need to work with new ones; systems use different technologies; business processes are complex; systems need to\n988: work independently.\n989: To sum up (SOA):\n990: • SOA trades code complexity for network complexity, enabling rapid iteration.\n991: • It provides agility through independent deployments, targeted scaling, and fault isolation.\n992: • Challenges include unavoidable network latency, complex security, and difficult system monitoring.\n993: • SOA is ideal when multiple complex or legacy systems must connect and share data.\n994: MONOLITHIC vs SOA (COMPARISON TABLE — MEMORIZE)\n995: Trait\n996: Monolithic Architecture\n997: Service-Oriented Architecture (SOA)\n998: Deployment\n999: Deployed as a single, indivisible unit\n1000: Services deployed independently of each\n1001: other\n1002: Technology Stack\n1003: Uniform; locked into a single\n1004: language/framework\n1005: Diverse; each service can use the best tool\n1006: for the job\n1007: Scaling\n1008: Must duplicate the entire application to\n1009: scale\n1010: Scale only the specific services under\n1011: heavy load\n1012: Failure Impact\n1013: A bug in one module can crash the entire\n1014: system\n1015: Failures are isolated to the specific affected\n1016: service\n1017: THE EVOLUTION OF SOA\n1018: A Brief History of Software Architecture (MEMORIZE)\n1019: Era\n1020: Characteristics\n1021: MONOLITHIC (1990s)\n1022: One codebase, one development. Simple to start, hard to scale or\n1023: change safely. Every feature ships together; even minor updates\n1024: require testing and redeploying the entire application\n1025: SOA (2000s)\n1026: Business logic split into reusable services, integrated over the\n1027: Enterprise Service Bus. Let large enterprises reuse core capabilities\n1028: across many applications instead of rebuilding them each time\n1029: \n1030: ===== PAGE 19 =====\n1031: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1032: Page 19 of 43\n1033: Era\n1034: Characteristics\n1035: MICROSERVICES (2010s - now)\n1036: Small, independently deployable services communicating over\n1037: lightweight APIs. Teams build, test, and release each service on its\n1038: own schedule, using whichever language/database fits best\n1039: The Problem That Forced SOA's Invention\n1040: • Scalability bottlenecks — the entire application had to scale as one unit even if only a single function was under load;\n1041: modules were so interdependent that a small change could break unrelated functionality.\n1042: • Slow, risky deployments — a one-line fix meant rebuilding and redeploying the whole system; release cycles stretched to\n1043: weeks or months.\n1044: • One codebase meant one language, one stack — migrating to new technology meant rewriting the whole system.\n1045: SOA's Answer: Loosely Coupled Services\n1046: • Services, not one app — business capabilities (billing, inventory, auth) become independent services with defined\n1047: contracts; each owned, maintained, and scaled by its own dedicated system.\n1048: • Enterprise Service Bus (ESB) — a central bus routes, transforms, and mediates messages between services — no\n1049: service calls another directly; also handles logging, security, and protocol translation enterprise-wide.\n1050: • Standardized contracts — interfaces described with WSDL/SOAP, so services on different platforms interoperate (a Java\n1051: service can talk to a .NET or mainframe service).\n1052: • Reusability across the business — the same service (e.g., \"check credit\") can be reused by many applications instead of\n1053: rebuilt.\n1054: Why SOA Evolved Into Microservices\n1055: SOA growing pains (4):\n1056: • The ESB became a single point of failure and a performance bottleneck as message volume grew.\n1057: • Heavy WS-* / SOAP contracts made services slow to build and change.\n1058: • Centralized governance slowed teams down (every contract change needed sign-off).\n1059: • Shared services meant one team's bug or scaling need affected everyone relying on that service.\n1060: Microservices' response (4):\n1061: • No central bus — services call each other directly over lightweight REST/gRPC APIs.\n1062: • Each service owns its data and can be built in any language or stack best suited to the job.\n1063: • Small, autonomous teams own a service end-to-end and deploy independently, often many times a day.\n1064: • Failure is isolated — one service going down doesn't take the whole system with it.\n1065: SOA vs MICROSERVICES (COMPARISON TABLE — MEMORIZE)\n1066: Aspect\n1067: SOA\n1068: Microservices\n1069: Communication\n1070: Enterprise Service Bus (ESB) mediates\n1071: every message\n1072: Direct REST/gRPC calls straight between\n1073: services\n1074: Scope of a service\n1075: Broad, enterprise-wide capability, built to be\n1076: reused widely\n1077: Small, single business function per service\n1078: Data ownership\n1079: Often shared databases across multiple\n1080: services\n1081: Each service owns and manages its own\n1082: data\n1083: Deployment\n1084: Coordinated, usually released together as a\n1085: group\n1086: Independent — each service deploys on its\n1087: own schedule\n1088: Governance\n1089: Centralized standards & contracts enforced\n1090: org-wide\n1091: Decentralized, owned and evolved by each\n1092: team\n1093: SOA's Legacy in Today's Cloud (4 — MEMORIZE)\n1094: \n1095: ===== PAGE 20 =====\n1096: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1097: Page 20 of 43\n1098: 1. API gateways — the modern successor to the ESB: routing, auth, and rate-limiting for cloud APIs; centralizes\n1099: cross-cutting concerns like throttling and monitoring.\n1100: 2. Service mesh — tools like Istio manage service-to-service communication, echoing SOA's mediation layer; adds retries,\n1101: encryption, and observability without changing application code.\n1102: 3. Containers & orchestration — Docker and Kubernetes let SOA's \"independent service\" idea run at cloud scale;\n1103: services packaged once and deployed consistently across any cloud provider.\n1104: 4. Hybrid & legacy integration — cloud platforms still use SOA-style adapters to connect to older enterprise systems\n1105: (many banks and airlines rely on this pattern to modernize gradually).\n1106: L5 KEY TAKEAWAYS\n1107: 1. Monolithic systems ran into scalability, coupling, and deployment problems as they grew — development slowed and\n1108: every release carried more risk.\n1109: 2. SOA answered this by splitting logic into services connected through a central ESB, letting large organizations reuse\n1110: services instead of rebuilding logic repeatedly.\n1111: 3. SOA's own overhead — the ESB and centralized governance — pushed the industry toward lighter, independently\n1112: deployable microservices.\n1113: 4. SOA's core principles — reusable, contract-based services — live on in today's API gateways, service meshes, and\n1114: containerized cloud platforms.\n1115: \n1116: ===== PAGE 21 =====\n1117: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1118: Page 21 of 43\n1119: L6: INTEGRATING TECHNOLOGY\n1120: XML FOUNDATIONS\n1121: XML (eXtensible Markup Language) — a standard for writing data as plain text, using tags that describe what the data means.\n1122: Properties of XML (4):\n1123: 1. Self-describing — tags tell you what each value is.\n1124: 2. Extensible — create your own tags to fit your needs.\n1125: 3. Platform Neutral — plain text; many systems and apps can read it.\n1126: 4. Data, not design — XML stores meaning, not appearance.\n1127: EXAMPLE: <student><name>Jerick Dizon</name><course>BSIT</course><year>3</year></student> vs normal text\n1128: \"Jerick Dizon, BSIT, 3\".\n1129: Where XML is used in real life (5):\n1130: 1. Microsoft Office files — a .docx is a ZIP folder full of XML files (text, fonts, formatting all stored as XML).\n1131: 2. Android apps — every app screen layout is written in XML.\n1132: 3. RSS feeds and podcasts — podcast apps download an XML file listing episodes.\n1133: 4. Configuration files — Java, .NET and many systems store settings in XML.\n1134: 5. Business documents between companies — invoices, purchase orders, and bank transactions exchanged as XML\n1135: files.\n1136: Parts of an XML Document (6 — MEMORIZE with example)\n1137: Example document:\n1138: <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n1139: <library>\n1140:  <book isbn=\"978-0-13-235088\">\n1141:    <title>Clean Code</title>\n1142:    <author>Robert Martin</author>\n1143:    <year>2008</year>\n1144:  </book>\n1145: </library>\n1146: 1. Prolog — <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>; always the very first line. version says which XML standard is\n1147: used; encoding says which character set — UTF-8 lets one file hold English, Filipino, Chinese, and Arabic text.\n1148: 2. Root Element — <library>...</library>; the outermost element; there is exactly one per document and everything\n1149: else sits inside it.\n1150: 3. Child Element — <book> is a child of <library>; <title>, <author>, <year> are children of <book>. An element has\n1151: three parts: opening tag, content, closing tag.\n1152: 4. Attribute — isbn=\"978-0-13-235088\"; always written inside the opening tag; format is name=\"value\" and the value\n1153: must be in quotes; an attribute name cannot repeat on the same element.\n1154: 5. Element Content — the text between the tags (e.g., \"Robert Martin\"); the actual data being stored.\n1155: 6. Nesting — the parent-child layering that makes XML a tree. Indentation is only for human readability — the computer\n1156: reads the nesting from the tags.\n1157: WELL-FORMED vs VALID (MEMORIZE)\n1158: • Well-formed (correct syntax): exactly one root element; every opening tag has a closing tag; tags never overlap; attribute\n1159: values are in quotes; names are case-sensitive.\n1160: • Valid (well-formed AND follows a schema): only allowed elements appear; required elements are present; values match\n1161: their declared data types.\n1162: Examples:\n1163: • NOT well-formed: <student><name>Ana<age>19</name></age></student>\n1164: • Well-formed but NOT valid: <student><name>Ana</name><age>banana</age></student>\n1165: \n1166: ===== PAGE 22 =====\n1167: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1168: Page 22 of 43\n1169: • Well-formed and valid: <student><name>Ana</name><age>19</age></student>\n1170: DTD vs XSD (COMPARISON — MEMORIZE)\n1171: Aspect\n1172: DTD (Document Type Definition)\n1173: XSD (XML Schema Definition)\n1174: Status\n1175: The old way\n1176: Used today\n1177: Syntax\n1178: Written in its own syntax, not in XML\n1179: A W3C standard, written in XML itself\n1180: Data types\n1181: No data types — everything is just text\n1182: Real data types: integer, date, decimal,\n1183: boolean\n1184: Features\n1185: No namespace support\n1186: Supports ranges, patterns, and lists of\n1187: allowed values; namespace-aware; types\n1188: can be reused\n1189: XPATH, XQUERY AND XSLT (MEMORIZE the roles)\n1190: • XPath — FINDS. Locates one specific part of the document, written like a folder path (e.g., /library/book/title). It\n1191: works like a file path; it doesn't change anything, it just says which part you mean.\n1192: • XQuery — SEARCHES. Searches XML and returns only the parts you ask for — what SQL does for tables (e.g., for $b\n1193: in //book where $b/year > 2005 return $b/title returns titles of all books published after 2005).\n1194: • XSLT — CONVERTS. Turns an XML file into a different format: HTML, plain text, or other XML (e.g., an <xsl:template\n1195: match=\"book\"> turns each <book> into an HTML list item). Same data, different output — the original file is never changed.\n1196: READING XML IN A PROGRAM (4 approaches — MEMORIZE)\n1197: Approach\n1198: How It Works\n1199: Best For\n1200: DOM\n1201: Loads the whole document into memory as\n1202: a tree\n1203: Easy to read and edit; best for small files\n1204: you need to modify (uses memory equal to\n1205: the file size)\n1206: SAX\n1207: The parser streams through and fires\n1208: events at your code\n1209: Very little memory; read once, forward only,\n1210: cannot edit; best for very large files\n1211: StAX\n1212: Your code asks for the next piece when\n1213: ready\n1214: Same efficiency as SAX; you control the\n1215: loop; best for streaming where you need to\n1216: skip sections\n1217: Data Binding (JAXB)\n1218: Converts XML directly into objects (e.g.,\n1219: <students> becomes a student object\n1220: with getName())\n1221: Best for when the schema is stable\n1222: TREES MEET TABLES — THE IMPEDANCE MISMATCH\n1223: • XML is a nested, ordered tree; a relational database is a flat table. Storing one in the other is never free.\n1224: • Order matters in XML; table rows have no order.\n1225: • Elements repeat or disappear — one order has 1 item, another has 50; a table can't have 50 columns.\n1226: • Mixed content has no column equivalent.\n1227: • The gap between the XML tree and database tables is the impedance mismatch.\n1228: 2 KINDS OF XML, 3 WAYS TO STORE (MEMORIZE)\n1229: First, classify the document:\n1230: 1. Data-Centric — regular, predictable structure; order doesn't matter (invoices, purchase orders, config files) -> store in a\n1231: relational database.\n1232: 2. Document-Centric — irregular, mixed content; order matters (manuals, contracts, articles) -> store in a native XML\n1233: database.\n1234: Second, choose storage approach (3):\n1235: \n1236: ===== PAGE 23 =====\n1237: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1238: Page 23 of 43\n1239: 1. XML-enabled database — a normal relational database with XML features added (Oracle XML DB, SQL Server, Db2\n1240: pureXML). Gain: speed and familiar tools. Cost: the original document is lost.\n1241: 2. Native XML database — the document itself is the unit of storage (eXist-db, BaseX, MarkLogic). Gain: perfect fidelity,\n1242: flexible structure. Cost: slower joins, fewer experts.\n1243: 3. Hybrid / XML column — keep the whole document in one column, copy key fields into normal columns. Gain: both fidelity\n1244: and speed. Cost: data stored twice, must stay in sync.\n1245: Three internal storage techniques:\n1246: • Shredding — the tree is broken into rows and columns. Fast joins and reports, but the original document no longer exists.\n1247: • CLOB / text column — stored as one long string. Perfect fidelity and cheap writes, but every query must parse it again.\n1248: • Native tree — parsed once into an indexed form. Fast XPath, but larger on disk and vendor-specific.\n1249: XML IN PRACTICE\n1250: XML Integration — the strategic practice of using XML as a universal, text-based standard to exchange structured business\n1251: data between incompatible enterprise systems, databases, and external business partners without redesigning their core\n1252: architectures.\n1253: • The Mission — bridges isolated enterprise silos (ERP, CRM, legacy mainframes) across conflicting operating systems and\n1254: programming languages.\n1255: • The \"Lingua Franca\" — acts as a vendor-neutral, self-describing common language with explicit tags that any hardware\n1256: or software platform can interpret.\n1257: • Enterprise Impact — forms the essential data payload for Enterprise Application Integration (EAI), B2B supply chains,\n1258: and automated business workflows.\n1259: The Integration Problem & Solution:\n1260: • Problem — Enterprise Silos: companies rely on multiple distinct systems (ERPs like SAP, CRMs, legacy databases,\n1261: custom logistics apps); each runs on different OS, protocols, and proprietary formats; traditional point-to-point connections\n1262: create an unmaintainable \"spaghetti\" mesh.\n1263: • Solution — Universal XML Lingua Franca: platform independence, self-describing data, and decoupled architecture —\n1264: systems send canonical XML payloads to middleware or service buses rather than binding directly to each other.\n1265: How XML powers integration (3):\n1266: 1. XSLT Transformation — bridges differing schemas without modifying original databases: converts supplier PO formats\n1267: to canonical enterprise standards; maps incompatible field names automatically; transforms raw XML into HTML or JSON.\n1268: 2. Schema Validation (XSD) — enforces strict structural rules and data integrity at the system boundary: guarantees\n1269: required fields and correct data types; rejects corrupted or malformed payloads early; prevents invalid records from polluting\n1270: databases.\n1271: 3. Standardized Formats — enables global B2B collaboration through shared domain vocabularies: - ISO 20022 — global\n1272: interbank payments - HL7 / CDA — healthcare & clinical data - RosettaNet / ebXML — B2B supply chains\n1273: Summary of XML Integration: XML transforms isolated silos into a cohesive enterprise data network. XSD protects data\n1274: integrity; XSLT resolves schema mismatches. Governed, high-stakes industries (banking, healthcare) still rely on XML for\n1275: certified compliance.\n1276: WEB SERVICE TECHNOLOGIES\n1277: Web service technologies — standards, protocols, and formats that allow different software applications to communicate and\n1278: exchange information over a network. They allow systems developed using different programming languages, operating systems,\n1279: and platforms to work together (e.g., a mobile app communicating with a web server for user or product data).\n1280: SOAP (Simple Object Access Protocol) — a protocol used for exchanging structured information between applications over a\n1281: network. Traditionally uses XML for messages and follows strict rules.\n1282: • Advantages: standardized communication structure; supports enterprise-level security and transactions; reliable for\n1283: complex business processes; works across different platforms.\n1284: • Disadvantages: more complex to implement; messages can be relatively large; requires more processing than simpler\n1285: approaches.\n1286: \n1287: ===== PAGE 24 =====\n1288: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1289: Page 24 of 43\n1290: REST (Representational State Transfer) — an architectural style used to design web-based services and APIs. Commonly\n1291: uses standard HTTP methods; simpler and more lightweight than SOAP.\n1292: • Advantages: simple and easier to implement; lightweight; works well with web and mobile applications; scales well;\n1293: commonly uses JSON.\n1294: • Disadvantages: does not provide the same level of built-in standards as SOAP; security mechanisms usually\n1295: implemented separately; may not be ideal for complex enterprise transactions.\n1296: XML (Extensible Markup Language) — a format used to organize and exchange structured information; became especially\n1297: important in traditional web services because SOAP uses it to structure its messages. Characteristics: human-readable;\n1298: platform-independent; can represent complex data; customizable tags; commonly associated with SOAP services.\n1299: JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) — a lightweight format for exchanging data; especially common in REST APIs and modern\n1300: web/mobile applications; generally more compact and easier to process than XML. Characteristics: lightweight; easy to read;\n1301: easy to process; commonly used with REST; widely supported by programming languages.\n1302: HTTP and HTTPS — HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) is the primary communication protocol used on the Web, commonly\n1303: used by REST APIs; a client sends requests to a server and receives responses. HTTPS (HTTP Secure) is the secure version —\n1304: it uses TLS encryption to protect information in transit, important when handling sensitive information (passwords, payment info,\n1305: personal data).\n1306: WSDL (Web Services Description Language) — an XML-based language traditionally used to describe SOAP web services.\n1307: Key concept: WSDL acts like a technical contract or specification for a SOAP service — it provides: what services are\n1308: available, what operations the service supports, what information can be sent/received, and how the service can be accessed.\n1309: UDDI (Universal Description, Discovery, and Integration) — created to help organizations publish and discover web services;\n1310: functioned like a directory where businesses and applications could find information about available services. Important in the\n1311: early development of web services but less commonly used in modern web development today.\n1312: Importance of web service technologies (5): Interoperability (different systems can communicate), Integration (separate\n1313: applications work together), Reusability (same service used by multiple applications), Scalability (support growing numbers of\n1314: users/applications), Automation (systems exchange information automatically).\n1315: ADVANCED WEB SERVICES — WS-* TECHNOLOGIES\n1316: WS-* (WS- Technologies) — a family of specifications built on top of SOAP and XML, designed to support enterprise-grade\n1317: web services. They are composable — built using XML, WSDL, and SOAP extensibility models. While basic SOAP handles\n1318: message exchange, it doesn't address security, reliability, or transaction management on its own — that is the gap the\n1319: WS- family fills.\n1320: Core WS- Specifications:\n1321: • WSDL — an XML-based service description language that complements UDDI by providing a uniform way to describe\n1322: services and their network protocol bindings.\n1323: • UDDI — provides an open, platform-independent framework for describing services, discovering businesses, and\n1324: integrating services over the internet.\n1325: • WS-Security — adds authentication, encryption, and message integrity to SOAP messages; often paired with security\n1326: tokens like SAML assertions, which help prevent man-in-the-middle and replay attacks.\n1327: • WS-ReliableMessaging — ensures messages are still delivered correctly even over unstable networks.\n1328: WS-* in practice — these specifications rarely work in isolation; most systems combine several at once. WS-Policy expressions\n1329: can be embedded into a WSDL file; WS-SecurityPolicy can define concrete scenarios (e.g., requiring a username token with a\n1330: plain text password for authentication). This layering keeps enterprise systems both flexible and secure at scale.\n1331: WEB SERVICES INTEGRATION\n1332: Web services integration — the process of connecting multiple independent services or systems so they function together as\n1333: one cohesive whole — necessary because organizations rely on a mix of platforms, languages, and legacy systems never\n1334: designed to work together directly.\n1335: Integration approaches and patterns:\n1336: • Orchestration — relies on a central process that directs how different services interact, coordinating them from the top\n1337: down.\n1338: • Choreography — services communicate directly with one another in a peer-to-peer manner, with no single controlling\n1339: process.\n1340: \n1341: ===== PAGE 25 =====\n1342: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1343: Page 25 of 43\n1344: • Middleware — many enterprise systems rely on middleware, such as an Enterprise Service Bus, to manage\n1345: communication between services.\n1346: • Web services standards such as SOAP, WSDL, UDDI, and WS-I profiles make cross-platform integration technically\n1347: possible.\n1348: \n1349: ===== PAGE 26 =====\n1350: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1351: Page 26 of 43\n1352: L7: INTEGRATING APPLICATIONS\n1353: APPLICATION INTEGRATION\n1354: Application Integration — the process of enabling independently designed applications — often built by different vendors, using\n1355: different technologies, platforms, or data structures — to communicate, exchange data, and work together as a coordinated\n1356: system.\n1357: Goal: create a seamless flow of information and processes across systems, reducing or eliminating manual data transfer.\n1358: Why it's needed: organizations use specialized applications such as CRM, ERP, HR, and accounting systems that operate\n1359: independently and store data differently.\n1360: Why application integration matters:\n1361: • Eliminates data silos — connects isolated data across departments (sales, HR, finance) so teams access consistent,\n1362: up-to-date information.\n1363: • Reduces manual data entry and human error — automates data flow, freeing staff for higher-value work.\n1364: • Improves data accuracy and consistency — keeps records aligned across systems (an update in one place is reflected\n1365: elsewhere).\n1366: • Speeds up decision-making — provides leadership with integrated, up-to-date data.\n1367: INTEGRATION STYLES (MEMORIZE each definition)\n1368: POINT-TO-POINT INTEGRATION — a direct, custom-built connection between two systems, built specifically for that pair,\n1369: generally not reusable.\n1370: • Characteristics: simple and fast to implement for a small number of systems.\n1371: • Limitation: becomes difficult to manage and scale as more systems are added — leads to a tangled \"spaghetti\"\n1372: architecture.\n1373: • Example: a website directly connects to a payment gateway without an intermediary.\n1374: HUB-AND-SPOKE INTEGRATION — a central hub (integration engine) manages and routes communication between multiple\n1375: systems, handling data translation and routing.\n1376: • How it works: each system connects to the hub, reducing the total number of connections.\n1377: • Benefit: easier to manage and scale than point-to-point.\n1378: • Example: a central integration hub connects HR, payroll, and benefits systems.\n1379: ENTERPRISE SERVICE BUS (ESB) — a middleware layer that facilitates, transforms, and manages communication between\n1380: multiple applications.\n1381: • How it works: acts as a common \"communication backbone,\" routing and transforming messages between systems using\n1382: different formats and protocols.\n1383: • Benefit: centralized integration capabilities, easier to manage and scale in larger environments.\n1384: • Example: a bank uses an ESB to connect core banking, mobile app, and ATM network (XML and JSON).\n1385: API-BASED / MICROSERVICES INTEGRATION — a modern, flexible approach where applications or services expose specific\n1386: functions through APIs that other systems can call.\n1387: • How it works: independent components communicate through well-defined APIs; components can be updated, replaced, or\n1388: scaled without changing the whole system.\n1389: • Benefit: highly scalable and flexible; widely used in cloud, web, and mobile apps.\n1390: • Example: a ride-sharing app uses APIs to integrate Google Maps (navigation), Stripe (payments), and Twilio (SMS).\n1391: REAL-TIME vs BATCH INTEGRATION (COMPARISON)\n1392: Aspect\n1393: Real-time integration\n1394: Batch integration\n1395: Timing\n1396: Data synced immediately or with very little\n1397: delay\n1398: Data collected and transferred at scheduled\n1399: intervals\n1400: \n\n(Showing lines 901-1400 of 2262. Use offset=1401 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"SOAP\nFormat\nTypically JSON (lightweight)\nMessages always formatted as XML\nenvelopes\nOperations\nUses standard HTTP verbs: GET, POST,\nPUT, DELETE\nWorks over HTTP, SMTP, and other\ntransport protocols\nState / Security\nStateless — each request contains\neverything needed to process it\nBuilt-in standards for security (WS-Security)\nand transactions\nBest for\nSimpler, faster, widely used for web &\nmobile APIs\nEnterprise systems needing high reliability\n(banking, finance)","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt","text":"SOAP\nFormat\nTypically JSON (lightweight)\nMessages always formatted as XML\nenvelopes\nOperations\nUses standard HTTP verbs: GET, POST,\nPUT, DELETE\nWorks over HTTP, SMTP, and other\ntransport protocols\nState / Security\nStateless — each request contains\neverything needed to process it\nBuilt-in standards for security (WS-Security)\nand transactions\nBest for\nSimpler, faster, widely used for web &\nmobile APIs\nEnterprise systems needing high reliability\n(banking, finance)\nCOMMON DATA FORMATS: XML vs JSON (COMPARISON)\nAspect\nXML\nJSON\nStructure\nVerbose, tag-based markup\nLightweight, key-value structure\nValidation\nStrong support for schemas/validation\nMaps directly to objects in most\nprogramming languages\nUsage\nCommon in SOAP messages & legacy\nenterprise systems\nThe default format for REST APIs\nTHE ENTERPRISE SERVICE BUS (ESB)\nESB (Enterprise Service Bus) — a piece of middleware that sits between services, handling routing, translation, and message\ndelivery so that no service ever has to know the technical details of any other service it talks to. Instead of every application\nconnecting directly to every other application, each one only needs to know how to talk to the bus.\nCore Functions of an ESB (6 — MEMORIZE):\n1. Routing — directs each incoming message to the correct destination service based on content or business rules.\n2. Transformation — converts data formats between systems, such as XML <-> JSON, so mismatched services can still\ncommunicate.\n3. Protocol Mediation — bridges services that use different transport protocols, such as HTTP, JMS, or FTP.\n4. Orchestration — coordinates calls to multiple services in sequence to complete a single business process.\n5. Monitoring & Logging — tracks message flow, errors, and performance across the entire system in real time.\n6. Security Enforcement — applies authentication, authorization, and encryption to messages as they pass through.\nDiagram understanding: Web App, Mobile App, CRM System, and Legacy System all connect to the Enterprise Service Bus,\nwhich connects to Payment, Inventory, and Shipping Services. All communication flows through the ESB — no service calls\nanother directly.\nREAL-WORLD EXAMPLES OF SOA IN APPS (6)\n1. Online Shopping — separate services for catalog search, cart, payment, and shipment tracking, combined at checkout.\n2. Banking Systems — one banking app calls independent services for balances, transfers, loan processing, and fraud\ndetection.\n3. Airline Booking — ticketing, seat selection, payment, and loyalty-point services behind one booking interface.\n4. Streaming Platforms — recommendation, billing, content-delivery, and user-profile services combine into one app.\n5. Ride-Hailing Apps — mapping, pricing, driver-matching, and payment services communicate in real time for a single ride.\n6. Healthcare Records — patient records, billing, and scheduling systems exchange data through shared services.\nANALYZING SOA\nKey concept — evaluating SOA requires understanding both its benefits and complexities. While SOA enables rapid scale and\niteration, it fundamentally shifts the burden from code complexity to network complexity.\nPrimary Business Benefits (MEMORIZE):\n\n===== PAGE 18 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 18 of 43\n• AGILITY — teams can develop, update, and deploy individual services independently -> drastically reduces time to bring\nnew features to market.\n• REUSABILITY — services are designed as self-contained business functions; a user authentication service can be reused\nacross multiple applications.\n• SCALABILITY — scale only the specific services under high demand instead of the entire application -> optimizes\nresource usage and costs.\nTechnical Advantages:\n• Technology Diversity — services can be written in entirely different programming languages (polyglot programming)\nsince they communicate over standard protocols (like HTTP).\n• Fault Isolation — if one service crashes, the rest of the application can often continue to function.\n• Streamlined Maintenance — smaller codebases make it easier to understand logic, find bugs, and perform maintenance.\n• Independent Deployment — updates can be pushed to specific services without taking the whole system offline.\nInherent Drawbacks & Challenges:\n• Network Overhead — heavy reliance on network communication introduces unavoidable latency compared to in-memory\nfunction calls; securing data in transit adds complex authentication and encryption challenges.\n• System Complexity — individual services are simple, but the system as a whole becomes highly complex\n(dozens/hundreds of independent moving parts); monitoring, debugging, and tracing across service boundaries requires\nspecialized tooling and expertise.\n• Anything large scale requires a whole lot of maintenance.\nWhen to choose SOA? Many systems need to connect; services need to be reused; different systems need to share data; old\nsystems need to work with new ones; systems use different technologies; business processes are complex; systems need to\nwork independently.\nTo sum up (SOA):\n• SOA trades code complexity for network complexity, enabling rapid iteration.\n• It provides agility through independent deployments, targeted scaling, and fault isolation.\n• Challenges include unavoidable network latency, complex security, and difficult system monitoring.\n• SOA is ideal when multiple complex or legacy systems must connect and share data.\nMONOLITHIC vs SOA (COMPARISON TABLE — MEMORIZE)\nTrait\nMonolithic Architecture\nService-Oriented Architecture (SOA)\nDeployment\nDeployed as a single, indivisible unit\nServices deployed independently of each\nother\nTechnology Stack\nUniform; locked into a single\nlanguage/framework\nDiverse; each service can use the best tool\nfor the job\nScaling\nMust duplicate the entire application to\nscale\nScale only the specific services under\nheavy load\nFailure Impact\nA bug in one module can crash the entire\nsystem\nFailures are isolated to the specific affected\nservice\nTHE EVOLUTION OF SOA\nA Brief History of Software Architecture (MEMORIZE)\nEra\nCharacteristics\nMONOLITHIC (1990s)\nOne codebase, one development. Simple to start, hard to scale or\nchange safely. Every feature ships together; even minor updates\nrequire testing and redeploying the entire application\nSOA (2000s)\nBusiness logic split into reusable services, integrated over the\nEnterprise Service Bus. Let large enterprises reuse core capabilities\nacross many applications instead of rebuilding them each time\n\n===== PAGE 19 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 19 of 43\nEra\nCharacteristics\nMICROSERVICES (2010s - now)\nSmall, independently deployable services communicating over\nlightweight APIs. Teams build, test, and release each service on its\nown schedule, using whichever language/database fits best\nThe Problem That Forced SOA's Invention\n• Scalability bottlenecks — the entire application had to scale as one unit even if only a single function was under load;\nmodules were so interdependent that a small change could break unrelated functionality.\n• Slow, risky deployments — a one-line fix meant rebuilding and redeploying the whole system; release cycles stretched to\nweeks or months.\n• One codebase meant one language, one stack — migrating to new technology meant rewriting the whole system.\nSOA's Answer: Loosely Coupled Services\n• Services, not one app — business capabilities (billing, inventory, auth) become independent services with defined\ncontracts; each owned, maintained, and scaled by its own dedicated system.\n• Enterprise Service Bus (ESB) — a central bus routes, transforms, and mediates messages between services — no\nservice calls another directly; also handles logging, security, and protocol translation enterprise-wide.\n• Standardized contracts — interfaces described with WSDL/SOAP, so services on different platforms interoperate (a Java\nservice can talk to a .NET or mainframe service).\n• Reusability across the business — the same service (e.g., \"check credit\") can be reused by many applications instead of\nrebuilt.\nWhy SOA Evolved Into Microservices\nSOA growing pains (4):\n• The ESB became a single point of failure and a performance bottleneck as message volume grew.\n• Heavy WS-* / SOAP contracts made services slow to build and change.\n• Centralized governance slowed teams down (every contract change needed sign-off).\n• Shared services meant one team's bug or scaling need affected everyone relying on that service.\nMicroservices' response (4):\n• No central bus — services call each other directly over lightweight REST/gRPC APIs.\n• Each service owns its data and can be built in any language or stack best suited to the job.\n• Small, autonomous teams own a service end-to-end and deploy independently, often many times a day.\n• Failure is isolated — one service going down doesn't take the whole system with it.\nSOA vs MICROSERVICES (COMPARISON TABLE — MEMORIZE)\nAspect\nSOA\nMicroservices\nCommunication\nEnterprise Service Bus (ESB) mediates\nevery message\nDirect REST/gRPC calls straight between\nservices\nScope of a service\nBroad, enterprise-wide capability, built to be\nreused widely\nSmall, single business function per service\nData ownership\nOften shared databases across multiple\nservices\nEach service owns and manages its own\ndata\nDeployment\nCoordinated, usually released together as a\ngroup\nIndependent — each service deploys on its\nown schedule\nGovernance\nCentralized standards & contracts enforced\norg-wide\nDecentralized, owned and evolved by each\nteam\nSOA's Legacy in Today's Cloud (4 — MEMORIZE)\n\n===== PAGE 20 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 20 of 43\n1. API gateways — the modern successor to the ESB: routing, auth, and rate-limiting for cloud APIs; centralizes\ncross-cutting concerns like throttling and monitoring.\n2. Service mesh — tools like Istio manage service-to-service communication, echoing SOA's mediation layer; adds retries,\nencryption, and observability without changing application code.\n3. Containers & orchestration — Docker and Kubernetes let SOA's \"independent service\" idea run at cloud scale;\nservices packaged once and deployed consistently across any cloud provider.\n4. Hybrid & legacy integration — cloud platforms still use SOA-style adapters to connect to older enterprise systems\n(many banks and airlines rely on this pattern to modernize gradually).\nL5 KEY TAKEAWAYS\n1. Monolithic systems ran into scalability, coupling, and deployment problems as they grew — development slowed and\nevery release carried more risk.\n2. SOA answered this by splitting logic into services connected through a central ESB, letting large organizations reuse\nservices instead of rebuilding logic repeatedly.\n3. SOA's own overhead — the ESB and centralized governance — pushed the industry toward lighter, independently\ndeployable microservices.\n4. SOA's core principles — reusable, contract-based services — live on in today's API gateways, service meshes, and\ncontainerized cloud platforms.\n\n===== PAGE 21 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 21 of 43\nL6: INTEGRATING TECHNOLOGY\nXML FOUNDATIONS\nXML (eXtensible Markup Language) — a standard for writing data as plain text, using tags that describe what the data means.\nProperties of XML (4):\n1. Self-describing — tags tell you what each value is.\n2. Extensible — create your own tags to fit your needs.\n3. Platform Neutral — plain text; many systems and apps can read it.\n4. Data, not design — XML stores meaning, not appearance.\nEXAMPLE: <student><name>Jerick Dizon</name><course>BSIT</course><year>3</year></student> vs normal text\n\"Jerick Dizon, BSIT, 3\".\nWhere XML is used in real life (5):\n1. Microsoft Office files — a .docx is a ZIP folder full of XML files (text, fonts, formatting all stored as XML).\n2. Android apps — every app screen layout is written in XML.\n3. RSS feeds and podcasts — podcast apps download an XML file listing episodes.\n4. Configuration files — Java, .NET and many systems store settings in XML.\n5. Business documents between companies — invoices, purchase orders, and bank transactions exchanged as XML\nfiles.\nParts of an XML Document (6 — MEMORIZE with example)\nExample document:\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<library>\n <book isbn=\"978-0-13-235088\">\n   <title>Clean Code</title>\n   <author>Robert Martin</author>\n   <year>2008</year>\n </book>\n</library>\n1. Prolog — <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>; always the very first line. version says which XML standard is\nused; encoding says which character set — UTF-8 lets one file hold English, Filipino, Chinese, and Arabic text.\n2. Root Element — <library>...</library>; the outermost element; there is exactly one per document and everything\nelse sits inside it.\n3. Child Element — <book> is a child of <library>; <title>, <author>, <year> are children of <book>. An element has\nthree parts: opening tag, content, closing tag.\n4. Attribute — isbn=\"978-0-13-235088\"; always written inside the opening tag; format is name=\"value\" and the value\nmust be in quotes; an attribute name cannot repeat on the same element.\n5. Element Content — the text between the tags (e.g., \"Robert Martin\"); the actual data being stored.\n6. Nesting — the parent-child layering that makes XML a tree. Indentation is only for human readability — the computer\nreads the nesting from the tags.\nWELL-FORMED vs VALID (MEMORIZE)\n• Well-formed (correct syntax): exactly one root element; every opening tag has a closing tag; tags never overlap; attribute\nvalues are in quotes; names are case-sensitive.\n• Valid (well-formed AND follows a schema): only allowed elements appear; required elements are present; values match\ntheir declared data types.\nExamples:\n• NOT well-formed: <student><name>Ana<age>19</name></age></student>\n• Well-formed but NOT valid: <student><name>Ana</name><age>banana</age></student>\n\n===== PAGE 22 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 22 of 43\n• Well-formed and valid: <student><name>Ana</name><age>19</age></student>\nDTD vs XSD (COMPARISON — MEMORIZE)\nAspect\nDTD (Document Type Definition)\nXSD (XML Schema Definition)\nStatus\nThe old way\nUsed today\nSyntax\nWritten in its own syntax, not in XML\nA W3C standard, written in XML itself\nData types\nNo data types — everything is just text\nReal data types: integer, date, decimal,\nboolean\nFeatures\nNo namespace support\nSupports ranges, patterns, and lists of\nallowed values; namespace-aware; types\ncan be reused\nXPATH, XQUERY AND XSLT (MEMORIZE the roles)\n• XPath — FINDS. Locates one specific part of the document, written like a folder path (e.g., /library/book/title). It\nworks like a file path; it doesn't change anything, it just says which part you mean.\n• XQuery — SEARCHES. Searches XML and returns only the parts you ask for — what SQL does for tables (e.g., for $b\nin //book where $b/year > 2005 return $b/title returns titles of all books published after 2005).\n• XSLT — CONVERTS. Turns an XML file into a different format: HTML, plain text, or other XML (e.g., an <xsl:template\nmatch=\"book\"> turns each <book> into an HTML list item). Same data, different output — the original file is never changed.\nREADING XML IN A PROGRAM (4 approaches — MEMORIZE)\nApproach\nHow It Works\nBest For\nDOM\nLoads the whole document into memory as\na tree\nEasy to read and edit; best for small files\nyou need to modify (uses memory equal to\nthe file size)\nSAX\nThe parser streams through and fires\nevents at your code\nVery little memory; read once, forward only,\ncannot edit; best for very large files\nStAX\nYour code asks for the next piece when\nready\nSame efficiency as SAX; you control the\nloop; best for streaming where you need to\nskip sections\nData Binding (JAXB)\nConverts XML directly into objects (e.g.,\n<students> becomes a student object\nwith getName())\nBest for when the schema is stable\nTREES MEET TABLES — THE IMPEDANCE MISMATCH\n• XML is a nested, ordered tree; a relational database is a flat table. Storing one in the other is never free.\n• Order matters in XML; table rows have no order.\n• Elements repeat or disappear — one order has 1 item, another has 50; a table can't have 50 columns.\n• Mixed content has no column equivalent.\n• The gap between the XML tree and database tables is the impedance mismatch.\n2 KINDS OF XML, 3 WAYS TO STORE (MEMORIZE)\nFirst, classify the document:\n1. Data-Centric — regular, predictable structure; order doesn't matter (invoices, purchase orders, config files) -> store in a\nrelational database.\n2. Document-Centric — irregular, mixed content; order matters (manuals, contracts, articles) -> store in a native XML\ndatabase.\nSecond, choose storage approach (3):\n\n===== PAGE 23 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 23 of 43\n1. XML-enabled database — a normal relational database with XML features added (Oracle XML DB, SQL Server, Db2\npureXML). Gain: speed and familiar tools. Cost: the original document is lost.\n2. Native XML database — the document itself is the unit of storage (eXist-db, BaseX, MarkLogic). Gain: perfect fidelity,\nflexible structure. Cost: slower joins, fewer experts.\n3. Hybrid / XML column — keep the whole document in one column, copy key fields into normal columns. Gain: both fidelity\nand speed. Cost: data stored twice, must stay in sync.\nThree internal storage techniques:\n• Shredding — the tree is broken into rows and columns. Fast joins and reports, but the original document no longer exists.\n• CLOB / text column — stored as one long string. Perfect fidelity and cheap writes, but every query must parse it again.\n• Native tree — parsed once into an indexed form. Fast XPath, but larger on disk and vendor-specific.\nXML IN PRACTICE\nXML Integration — the strategic practice of using XML as a universal, text-based standard to exchange structured business\ndata between incompatible enterprise systems, databases, and external business partners without redesigning their core\narchitectures.\n• The Mission — bridges isolated enterprise silos (ERP, CRM, legacy mainframes) across conflicting operating systems and\nprogramming languages.\n• The \"Lingua Franca\" — acts as a vendor-neutral, self-describing common language with explicit tags that any hardware\nor software platform can interpret.\n• Enterprise Impact — forms the essential data payload for Enterprise Application Integration (EAI), B2B supply chains,\nand automated business workflows.\nThe Integration Problem & Solution:\n• Problem — Enterprise Silos: companies rely on multiple distinct systems (ERPs like SAP, CRMs, legacy databases,\ncustom logistics apps); each runs on different OS, protocols, and proprietary formats; traditional point-to-point connections\ncreate an unmaintainable \"spaghetti\" mesh.\n• Solution — Universal XML Lingua Franca: platform independence, self-describing data, and decoupled architecture —\nsystems send canonical XML payloads to middleware or service buses rather than binding directly to each other.\nHow XML powers integration (3):\n1. XSLT Transformation — bridges differing schemas without modifying original databases: converts supplier PO formats\nto canonical enterprise standards; maps incompatible field names automatically; transforms raw XML into HTML or JSON.\n2. Schema Validation (XSD) — enforces strict structural rules and data integrity at the system boundary: guarantees\nrequired fields and correct data types; rejects corrupted or malformed payloads early; prevents invalid records from polluting\ndatabases.\n3. Standardized Formats — enables global B2B collaboration through shared domain vocabularies: - ISO 20022 — global\ninterbank payments - HL7 / CDA — healthcare & clinical data - RosettaNet / ebXML — B2B supply chains\nSummary of XML Integration: XML transforms isolated silos into a cohesive enterprise data network. XSD protects data\nintegrity; XSLT resolves schema mismatches. Governed, high-stakes industries (banking, healthcare) still rely on XML for\ncertified compliance.\nWEB SERVICE TECHNOLOGIES\nWeb service technologies — standards, protocols, and formats that allow different software applications to communicate and\nexchange information over a network. They allow systems developed using different programming languages, operating systems,\nand platforms to work together (e.g., a mobile app communicating with a web server for user or product data).\nSOAP (Simple Object Access Protocol) — a protocol used for exchanging structured information between applications over a\nnetwork. Traditionally uses XML for messages and follows strict rules.\n• Advantages: standardized communication structure; supports enterprise-level security and transactions; reliable for\ncomplex business processes; works across different platforms.\n• Disadvantages: more complex to implement; messages can be relatively large; requires more processing than simpler\napproaches.\n\n===== PAGE 24 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 24 of 43\nREST (Representational State Transfer) — an architectural style used to design web-based services and APIs. Commonly\nuses standard HTTP methods; simpler and more lightweight than SOAP.\n• Advantages: simple and easier to implement; lightweight; works well with web and mobile applications; scales well;\ncommonly uses JSON.\n• Disadvantages: does not provide the same level of built-in standards as SOAP; security mechanisms usually\nimplemented separately; may not be ideal for complex enterprise transactions.\nXML (Extensible Markup Language) — a format used to organize and exchange structured information; became especially\nimportant in traditional web services because SOAP uses it to structure its messages. Characteristics: human-readable;\nplatform-independent; can represent complex data; customizable tags; commonly associated with SOAP services.\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) — a lightweight format for exchanging data; especially common in REST APIs and modern\nweb/mobile applications; generally more compact and easier to process than XML. Characteristics: lightweight; easy to read;\neasy to process; commonly used with REST; widely supported by programming languages.\nHTTP and HTTPS — HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) is the primary communication protocol used on the Web, commonly\nused by REST APIs; a client sends requests to a server and receives responses. HTTPS (HTTP Secure) is the secure version —\nit uses TLS encryption to protect information in transit, important when handling sensitive information (passwords, payment info,\npersonal data).\nWSDL (Web Services Description Language) — an XML-based language traditionally used to describe SOAP web services.\nKey concept: WSDL acts like a technical contract or specification for a SOAP service — it provides: what services are\navailable, what operations the service supports, what information can be sent/received, and how the service can be accessed.\nUDDI (Universal Description, Discovery, and Integration) — created to help organizations publish and discover web services;\nfunctioned like a directory where businesses and applications could find information about available services. Important in the\nearly development of web services but less commonly used in modern web development today.\nImportance of web service technologies (5): Interoperability (different systems can communicate), Integration (separate\napplications work together), Reusability (same service used by multiple applications), Scalability (support growing numbers of\nusers/applications), Automation (systems exchange information automatically).\nADVANCED WEB SERVICES — WS-* TECHNOLOGIES\nWS-* (WS- Technologies) — a family of specifications built on top of SOAP and XML, designed to support enterprise-grade\nweb services. They are composable — built using XML, WSDL, and SOAP extensibility models. While basic SOAP handles\nmessage exchange, it doesn't address security, reliability, or transaction management on its own — that is the gap the\nWS- family fills.\nCore WS- Specifications:\n• WSDL — an XML-based service description language that complements UDDI by providing a uniform way to describe\nservices and their network protocol bindings.\n• UDDI — provides an open, platform-independent framework for describing services, discovering businesses, and\nintegrating services over the internet.\n• WS-Security — adds authentication, encryption, and message integrity to SOAP messages; often paired with security\ntokens like SAML assertions, which help prevent man-in-the-middle and replay attacks.\n• WS-ReliableMessaging — ensures messages are still delivered correctly even over unstable networks.\nWS-* in practice — these specifications rarely work in isolation; most systems combine several at once. WS-Policy expressions\ncan be embedded into a WSDL file; WS-SecurityPolicy can define concrete scenarios (e.g., requiring a username token with a\nplain text password for authentication). This layering keeps enterprise systems both flexible and secure at scale.\nWEB SERVICES INTEGRATION\nWeb services integration — the process of connecting multiple independent services or systems so they function together as\none cohesive whole — necessary because organizations rely on a mix of platforms, languages, and legacy systems never\ndesigned to work together directly.\nIntegration approaches and patterns:\n• Orchestration — relies on a central process that directs how different services interact, coordinating them from the top\ndown.\n• Choreography — services communicate directly with one another in a peer-to-peer manner, with no single controlling\nprocess.\n\n===== PAGE 25 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 25 of 43\n• Middleware — many enterprise systems rely on middleware, such as an Enterprise Service Bus, to manage\ncommunication between services.\n• Web services standards such as SOAP, WSDL, UDDI, and WS-I profiles make cross-platform integration technically\npossible.\n\n===== PAGE 26 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 26 of 43\nL7: INTEGRATING APPLICATIONS\nAPPLICATION INTEGRATION\nApplication Integration — the process of enabling independently designed applications — often built by different vendors, using\ndifferent technologies, platforms, or data structures — to communicate, exchange data, and work together as a coordinated\nsystem.\nGoal: create a seamless flow of information and processes across systems, reducing or eliminating manual data transfer.\nWhy it's needed: organizations use specialized applications such as CRM, ERP, HR, and accounting systems that operate\nindependently and store data differently.\nWhy application integration matters:\n• Eliminates data silos — connects isolated data across departments (sales, HR, finance) so teams access consistent,\nup-to-date information.\n• Reduces manual data entry and human error — automates data flow, freeing staff for higher-value work.\n• Improves data accuracy and consistency — keeps records aligned across systems (an update in one place is reflected\nelsewhere).\n• Speeds up decision-making — provides leadership with integrated, up-to-date data.\nINTEGRATION STYLES (MEMORIZE each definition)\nPOINT-TO-POINT INTEGRATION — a direct, custom-built connection between two systems, built specifically for that pair,\ngenerally not reusable.\n• Characteristics: simple and fast to implement for a small number of systems.\n• Limitation: becomes difficult to manage and scale as more systems are added — leads to a tangled \"spaghetti\"\narchitecture.\n• Example: a website directly connects to a payment gateway without an intermediary.\nHUB-AND-SPOKE INTEGRATION — a central hub (integration engine) manages and routes communication between multiple\nsystems, handling data translation and routing.\n• How it works: each system connects to the hub, reducing the total number of connections.\n• Benefit: easier to manage and scale than point-to-point.\n• Example: a central integration hub connects HR, payroll, and benefits systems.\nENTERPRISE SERVICE BUS (ESB) — a middleware layer that facilitates, transforms, and manages communication between\nmultiple applications.\n• How it works: acts as a common \"communication backbone,\" routing and transforming messages between systems using\ndifferent formats and protocols.\n• Benefit: centralized integration capabilities, easier to manage and scale in larger environments.\n• Example: a bank uses an ESB to connect core banking, mobile app, and ATM network (XML and JSON).\nAPI-BASED / MICROSERVICES INTEGRATION — a modern, flexible approach where applications or services expose specific\nfunctions through APIs that other systems can call.\n• How it works: independent components communicate through well-defined APIs; components can be updated, replaced, or\nscaled without changing the whole system.\n• Benefit: highly scalable and flexible; widely used in cloud, web, and mobile apps.\n• Example: a ride-sharing app uses APIs to integrate Google Maps (navigation), Stripe (payments), and Twilio (SMS).\nREAL-TIME vs BATCH INTEGRATION (COMPARISON)\nAspect\nReal-time integration\nBatch integration\nTiming\nData synced immediately or with very little\ndelay\nData collected and transferred at scheduled\nintervals\n","lineStart":901,"lineEnd":1400,"totalLines":2262,"truncated":true}},"title":"xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt","time":{"start":1789817814666,"end":1789817814698}},"id":"prt_0b9747d70001BKJQiH3ncF6YYx","sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","messageID":"msg_0b974784e001thYYsVmp7MetnP"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1789817814762,"sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","part":{"id":"prt_0b9747ed9001d7LLa2mQ6JPx9T","reason":"tool-calls","messageID":"msg_0b974784e001thYYsVmp7MetnP","sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":49330,"input":7750,"output":108,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":41472}},"cost":0}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1789817817076,"sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","part":{"id":"prt_0b97487f0001euVJCKyZxLVV9a","messageID":"msg_0b9747ee5001MZlHnr4OGvGdsw","sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789817817555,"sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_dbe97bb1148945ccb1f47762","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt","limit":500,"offset":1401},"output":"<path>C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1401: ===== PAGE 27 =====\n1402: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1403: Page 27 of 43\n1404: Aspect\n1405: Real-time integration\n1406: Batch integration\n1407: Best for\n1408: Immediacy is critical (e.g., stock trading\n1409: platform updates balances right after every\n1410: trade)\n1411: Large volumes that don't need immediate\n1412: processing (e.g., payroll syncs attendance\n1413: nightly)\n1414: Advantage\n1415: Systems reflect the most current\n1416: information\n1417: Reduces processing overhead and system\n1418: load\n1419: Common challenges in application integration: data format mismatches (requires transformation), security risks (more entry\n1420: points for attacks), system downtime/dependency (one failure disrupts dependents), complexity as systems grow (more\n1421: maintenance and points of failure).\n1422: LEGACY SYSTEM INTEGRATION\n1423: Legacy system integration — the process of connecting and enabling communication between older, often outdated systems\n1424: (legacy systems) and newer systems or technologies — essential for legacy systems to keep functioning within a modern IT\n1425: environment.\n1426: Core approaches (5): API Wrapping, Middleware Connectivity, Enterprise Service Bus, Integration Platform as a Service\n1427: (iPaaS), ETL & Data Synchronization.\n1428: Why do businesses still use them?\n1429: • Cost savings — avoids the massive expense and risk of rebuilding core databases.\n1430: • Better efficiency — stops slow, manual data copying.\n1431: • New features — adds modern tools (cloud analytics, automation, AI) on top of older systems.\n1432: • Unified data — different teams share a single, accurate view.\n1433: • Faster results — updates workflows quickly instead of waiting years for full replacement.\n1434: COMPARISON: Legacy Integration vs Implementing New Systems (COMPARISON TABLE)\n1435: Feature\n1436: Legacy System Integration\n1437: New System Implementation\n1438: Cost\n1439: Lower upfront by building on existing\n1440: infrastructure\n1441: Higher investment for more capability\n1442: Disruption\n1443: Minimal disruption to daily workflows and\n1444: operations\n1445: High risk of operational downtime during\n1446: the transition\n1447: Training\n1448: Minimal training needed — staff already\n1449: know the system\n1450: Extensive training required for new\n1451: interfaces and workflows\n1452: Scalability\n1453: Limited by the architectural ceiling of the old\n1454: system\n1455: Highly scalable and easily customizable for\n1456: future growth\n1457: Challenges of integrating legacy systems (6):\n1458: 1. Architectural Mismatch — older monolithic designs struggle to communicate with modern, API-driven or\n1459: microservices-based cloud environments.\n1460: 2. Data Inconsistency — legacy databases use proprietary formats requiring complex translation logic.\n1461: 3. Security Gaps — outdated encryption and authentication protocols create vulnerabilities.\n1462: 4. Skill Shortages — finding engineers who understand obsolete languages is increasingly difficult.\n1463: 5. Missing Documentation — poor records make troubleshooting and integration risky.\n1464: 6. Downtime Risks — connecting/updating these systems often forces costly operational pauses or crashes.\n1465: Best practices (focus on) — define clear business outcomes, map dependencies, and adopt incremental patterns like the\n1466: Strangler Fig pattern to minimize operational risk.\n1467: • Strategic Planning & Assessment: Define Business Outcomes; Map all dependencies; Prioritize by impact.\n1468: • Architectural & Technical Patterns: Use incremental approaches; Employing Anti-Corruption Layer (ACL); Leverage\n1469: modern middleware.\n1470: • Data & Testing Operations: Treat data as separate workstream; Automate testing; Ensure robust monitoring.\n1471: \n1472: ===== PAGE 28 =====\n1473: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1474: Page 28 of 43\n1475: ENTERPRISE INTEGRATION\n1476: Enterprise Integration (Enterprise Application Integration / EAI) — the process of linking separate applications, databases,\n1477: and hardware platforms across an entire organization to work together as a coordinated ecosystem.\n1478: Goal: create a seamless, automated flow of information and end-to-end processes across corporate systems, eliminating data\n1479: isolation and manual work.\n1480: Key objectives (4):\n1481: 1. Data Consistency — synchronizes records across departments to establish a reliable, single source of truth.\n1482: 2. System Interoperability — bridges legacy software, on-premises infrastructure, and modern cloud services using\n1483: standardized communication protocols.\n1484: 3. Business Process Automation — automates complex multi-system workflows (e.g., order-to-cash, employee\n1485: onboarding).\n1486: 4. Real-Time Visibility — delivers integrated, real-time analytics and cross-functional reporting for executive\n1487: decision-making.\n1488: Enterprise integration architectures (4):\n1489: • Enterprise Service Bus (ESB) — a centralized middleware backbone managing routing, message transformation, and\n1490: protocol translation.\n1491: • Hub-and-Spoke Model — a central broker routes traffic and translates data between connected \"spoke\" applications,\n1492: reducing total connections.\n1493: • Service-Oriented Architecture (SOA) — applications expose core capabilities as reusable, loosely coupled services\n1494: across a network.\n1495: • Event-Driven Architecture (EDA) — systems publish event messages to an enterprise broker, letting subscribing\n1496: services react asynchronously in near-real time.\n1497: Core enterprise integration patterns (4):\n1498: • Messaging Channels — dedicated pathways for applications to send and receive asynchronous data payloads.\n1499: • Content-Based Router — inspects incoming message contents and automatically routes data to the correct destination\n1500: based on business rules.\n1501: • Message Translator — converts data payloads between incompatible structures and formats (e.g., XML to JSON, EDI to\n1502: SQL).\n1503: • Publish-Subscribe Channel — delivers messages from a single sender to multiple subscribed systems simultaneously\n1504: without direct coupling.\n1505: Business & operational benefits (4): Operational Efficiency (cuts manual entry/error), Organizational Agility (adopt/replace\n1506: tools without rebuilding IT infrastructure), Scalability (growing volumes through organized middleware), Cost Optimization\n1507: (preserves and extends legacy investments).\n1508: Challenges in enterprise integration (4): High Architectural Complexity, Single Point of Failure (centralized hubs/ESBs), Data\n1509: Security & Compliance, Resource & Skill Requirements.\n1510: Best practices (4): Enforce Loose Coupling; Adopt API Standardization; Implement Centralized Monitoring; Design for High\n1511: Availability (failover, load balancing, message retry queues).\n1512: INTEGRATION STRATEGIES\n1513: Integration strategy — determines how systems should communicate, where integration logic should live, and how the\n1514: environment can scale and remain maintainable (Architecture, Data Flow, Messaging, APIs & Events).\n1515: Common integration strategies (5):\n1516: • Point-to-Point — direct connection between two systems; simple for a small number of integrations, difficult to manage at\n1517: scale.\n1518: • Hub-and-Spoke — systems connect through a central hub that routes and transforms communication.\n1519: • ESB — middleware acts as a communication backbone for routing, transformation, and service interactions.\n1520: • API-Based — systems expose reusable functions and data through defined APIs.\n1521: • Event-Driven — systems react to published events, supporting asynchronous, near-real-time flows.\n1522: POINT-TO-POINT vs CENTRALIZED INTEGRATION (COMPARISON TABLE)\n1523: \n1524: ===== PAGE 29 =====\n1525: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1526: Page 29 of 43\n1527: Key trade-off: simplicity at small scale versus centralized control and manageability as the environment grows.\n1528: Aspect\n1529: Point-to-Point\n1530: Hub-and-Spoke / ESB\n1531: Setup\n1532: Fast for a few connections\n1533: More planning; centralized integration layer\n1534: Management\n1535: Every connection managed separately\n1536: Central routing and policies improve\n1537: visibility\n1538: Scalability\n1539: Connection count grows quickly\n1540: New systems can often connect through the\n1541: hub\n1542: Best fit\n1543: Small/simple environments\n1544: Larger, more complex enterprise\n1545: environments\n1546: API-based integration — APIs provide defined interfaces that allow applications to request data or functions without depending\n1547: on internal implementation details. Mobile/web app requests a service -> API layer authenticates, routes, and defines the\n1548: interface -> back-end provides data/functionality. Reusable (same API serves multiple clients), Controlled (auth at the interface),\n1549: Flexible (back-ends evolve without exposing internals).\n1550: Event-driven integration — instead of one system directly calling every other, a system publishes an event that interested\n1551: consumers react to (Event Producer e.g., \"Order Created\" -> Event Broker publishes/routes -> Consumers: Inventory, Payment,\n1552: Notification). Provides loose coupling, asynchronous flow, near-real-time response — useful when many services may need\n1553: to react to the same business event.\n1554: Cloud & iPaaS integration — iPaaS (Integration Platform as a Service) provides cloud-hosted tools for connecting\n1555: applications and data across cloud and on-premises environments (on-premises legacy apps/databases <-> iPaaS\n1556: connect/transform/monitor <-> cloud SaaS, cloud databases, analytics). Benefits: faster connection setup (reusable connectors),\n1557: hybrid environments, centralized monitoring.\n1558: Choosing the right integration strategy — no single strategy fits every organization; match to business needs, technical\n1559: constraints, and future growth:\n1560: • Number of systems — few systems tolerate direct connections; many need stronger coordination.\n1561: • Real-time needs — critical immediate updates favor APIs or event-driven flows.\n1562: • Data volume — large scheduled transfers suit batch-oriented approaches.\n1563: • Security — sensitive information needs strong authentication, authorization, monitoring.\n1564: • Scalability — choose patterns that remain manageable as integrations increase.\n1565: • Budget & skills — consider platform cost, maintenance effort, and available expertise.\n1566: \n1567: ===== PAGE 30 =====\n1568: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1569: Page 30 of 43\n1570: L8: ENTERPRISE INTEGRATION\n1571: ENTERPRISE INTEGRATION\n1572: Enterprise Integration — the process of linking different IT systems, applications, and data sources so they can work\n1573: together and share data seamlessly. Goal: make separate systems (e.g., HR, sales, inventory, finance) function as one unified\n1574: system. Key idea: it's about connecting, not replacing, existing systems.\n1575: Why it matters: companies use many systems that don't \"talk\" to each other -> data silos, duplicate entries, inconsistent records.\n1576: Integration removes these gaps. Benefits: real-time data sharing, faster decisions, reduced manual work/errors, cost savings,\n1577: better customer service.\n1578: The integration challenge: enterprises use systems built on different platforms, languages, and data formats (old legacy\n1579: systems vs modern cloud apps) — e.g., a sales system in one format, an accounting system in another. This mismatch is why\n1580: common standards (like XML) and communication methods (like Web Services) were developed.\n1581: Approaches to integration: Point-to-Point (direct connections; simple but hard to scale); Hub-and-Spoke (central hub manages\n1582: connections); Enterprise Service Bus (middleware that lets many systems communicate through one channel); API/Web\n1583: Services-based (modern approach using standardized calls).\n1584: Key technologies overview:\n1585: • XML — a standard format for structuring and exchanging data between different systems, regardless of platform.\n1586: • Web Services — standardized methods (e.g., SOAP, REST) that let applications communicate over a network.\n1587: • SOA (Service-Oriented Architecture) — a design approach where business functions are built as reusable \"services\"\n1588: that can be combined and reused across the enterprise.\n1589: XML INTEGRATION\n1590: XML Integration — the practice of exchanging structured data between systems using XML as a common format — reliable,\n1591: consistent, secure, and fast.\n1592: Why best practices matter (failure modes — MEMORIZE):\n1593: • Inconsistent data — same field, different tags across systems -> mapping breaks silently.\n1594: • Invalid information — missing or malformed values corrupt downstream records.\n1595: • Security risk — unvalidated input opens the door to injection and disclosure.\n1596: • Slow processing — bloated payloads and deep nesting drag down throughput.\n1597: • Hard troubleshooting — vague failures with no context waste hours per incident.\n1598: • Compatibility issues — undocumented schema changes quietly break trading partners.\n1599: Clear structure & data consistency:\n1600: • Structure: meaningful names (e.g., <shippingAddress>, not <sa>); logical hierarchy (order -> customer -> contact); flat &\n1601: DRY (avoid unnecessary nesting; never duplicate a value).\n1602: • Consistency: same tag every time (one name per concept, applied identically); consistent types & formats (quantity\n1603: always a number; dates always ISO 8601); define required fields (mark what must be present).\n1604: Validate & secure the exchange:\n1605: • Validation — layered checks: well-formed syntax; schema (XSD) check; matching tags & root; required fields & types.\n1606: • Security — protect every hop: use HTTPS (encrypt XML in transit); never trust input (validate against schema and\n1607: business rules); guard sensitive data (mask/encrypt PII at rest and in tags); authenticate (tokens, certs, or API keys); least\n1608: privilege; block XML attacks (disable external entities; cap size/depth).\n1609: Optimize performance & handle errors:\n1610: • Performance: send only necessary data; keep XML reasonably sized (paginate/stream large collections; gzip when\n1611: useful); process data efficiently (prefer streaming/SAX over full DOM for large files).\n1612: • Error handling: detect & identify (catch the failure where it occurs, pin down the root cause); give clear feedback (specific,\n1613: actionable message — not a generic error code).\n1614: Best practices, compounded — what you gain: reliable integrations, faster processing, accurate data exchange, easier \n1615: troubleshooting, better security, better compatibility. Design for the reader (structure, name, document XML as if a new engineer \n1616: must understand it cold); trust nothing by default (well-formed is not the same as safe); optimize the whole lifecycle (lean\n1617: \n1618: ===== PAGE 31 =====\n1619: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1620: Page 31 of 43\n1621: payloads, clear errors, living documentation).\n1622: WEB SERVICES\n1623: What are web services? — a standardized way for applications to communicate over a network, regardless of platform or\n1624: language. Platform-independent (works across Windows, Linux, mobile); protocols used: HTTP/HTTPS (sometimes SMTP/FTP);\n1625: data formats: XML or JSON; loosely coupled. Examples: payment gateways, courier tracking, social media login.\n1626: APIs: the gateway to web services — an API (Application Programming Interface) defines how software components should\n1627: interact.\n1628: • REST APIs — most common today; used in web & mobile apps.\n1629: • SOAP APIs — enterprise-grade; used in banking/finance.\n1630: • GraphQL — flexible querying; request only what you need.\n1631: • APIs act as a \"contract\" — defines available requests and expected responses; good APIs are well-documented (e.g.,\n1632: Swagger/OpenAPI). Real-world examples: Google Maps API, Lalamove/J&T API, Stripe API.\n1633: SOAP vs REST (COMPARISON TABLE — MEMORIZE)\n1634: Feature\n1635: SOAP\n1636: REST\n1637: Type\n1638: Protocol\n1639: Architectural style\n1640: Format\n1641: XML only\n1642: JSON, XML, plain text\n1643: Security\n1644: Built-in (WS-Security)\n1645: Depends on implementation\n1646: Speed\n1647: Slower (heavier messages)\n1648: Faster (lightweight)\n1649: Best for\n1650: Banking, enterprise, government\n1651: Web & mobile apps\n1652: Example\n1653: Interbank transfer systems\n1654: Facebook, Google Maps API\n1655: Security & authentication:\n1656: • Security: HTTPS/SSL/TLS encryption (protects data in transit); input validation (prevents SQL Injection, XSS);\n1657: WS-Security for SOAP (message-level security); rate limiting & firewalls (prevents abuse/DDoS); data encryption at rest.\n1658: • Authentication: API Keys, OAuth 2.0, JWT; Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA).\n1659: • Remember: Authentication = \"who are you?\" vs Authorization = \"what can you do?\"\n1660: Reliability & performance:\n1661: • Reliability: redundancy & failover systems; retry mechanisms; message queuing (RabbitMQ, Kafka); health monitoring &\n1662: SLA (e.g., 99.9% uptime guarantee).\n1663: • Performance: caching; load balancing; data compression (GZIP) & pagination; CDN (serves content closer to the user).\n1664: Web services best practices — checklist: use HTTPS always; choose the right protocol (SOAP vs REST); implement proper\n1665: authentication (OAuth, JWT, MFA); validate all inputs; design for failure (redundancy, retries, failover); optimize with caching, load\n1666: balancing, CDNs; document your API clearly (Swagger/OpenAPI); version your APIs (e.g., /api/v1/); monitor performance and\n1667: uptime continuously.\n1668: SERVICE-ORIENTED ENTERPRISE (SOE)\n1669: Service-Oriented Enterprise (SOE) — connects business systems through reusable services. It allows different applications\n1670: and departments to communicate and share data. It focuses on integration, flexibility, and reuse of existing systems.\n1671: Example: a school connects its student information system, payment system, library system, and learning platform through\n1672: services.\n1673: Service-Oriented Architecture (SOA) — an approach that organizes enterprise systems into independent, reusable services.\n1674: Services communicate through standard interfaces and protocols. Key characteristics: loose coupling, reusability,\n1675: interoperability, and scalability.\n1676: Main components of a Service-Oriented Enterprise (5 — MEMORIZE):\n1677: 1. Service Provider — creates and maintains a service.\n1678: 2. Service Consumer — application or user that uses the service.\n1679: 3. Service Registry / Broker — helps consumers find available services.\n1680: \n1681: ===== PAGE 32 =====\n1682: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1683: Page 32 of 43\n1684: 4. Service Interface — defines how a service can be accessed.\n1685: 5. Enterprise Service Bus (ESB) — connects and manages communication between services.\n1686: Building the Service-Oriented Enterprise (6 steps):\n1687: 1. Identify business processes that need integration.\n1688: 2. Identify existing systems and services that can be reused.\n1689: 3. Design services based on specific business functions.\n1690: 4. Create standard interfaces for communication.\n1691: 5. Integrate, test, and monitor the services.\n1692: 6. Maintain and improve services as business needs change.\n1693: Benefits and Challenges:\n1694: • Benefits: better system integration and flexibility; reuse of services reduces development time and costs; easier\n1695: maintenance and expansion.\n1696: • Challenges: security and access control; complex integration between different systems; requires proper service\n1697: management and monitoring.\n1698: Summary: a Service-Oriented Enterprise connects business systems through reusable services; SOA provides the structure for\n1699: designing and integrating these services; key components include providers, consumers, interfaces, registries, and ESBs. The\n1700: goal is an enterprise that is integrated, flexible, reusable, and easier to manage.\n1701: \n1702: ===== PAGE 33 =====\n1703: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1704: Page 33 of 43\n1705: IDENTIFICATION BANK\n1706: Match the exact wording you may see in the exam.\n1707: L2 Identification\n1708: A detailed, step-by-step approach that describes how an Enterprise Architecture program is established, developed,\n1709: managed, maintained, and used within an organization. -> EA Implementation Methodology\n1710: A structured set of tools, models, and guidelines used to organize and design an enterprise's architecture. -> EA Framework\n1711: Developed by John Zachman in 1987; not a methodology but a classification schema organized as a 6x6 matrix. -> Zachman\n1712: Framework\n1713: An EA framework used to help organizations design, plan, implement, and manage their enterprise architecture. -> TOGAF\n1714: TOGAF's method that helps organizations identify business requirements, analyze the current architecture, design the target\n1715: architecture, identify changes, plan implementation, and manage/review the architecture. -> ADM (Architecture\n1716: Development Method)\n1717: The ADM phase that establishes EA principles and governance and defines roles, responsibilities, and processes. ->\n1718: Preliminary Phase — Preparation\n1719: The ADM phase that defines goals and scope, identifies stakeholders, and creates the overall architecture vision. -> Phase A\n1720: — Architecture Vision\n1721: The ADM phase that defines infrastructure, networks, platforms, and technologies. -> Phase D — Technology Architecture\n1722: The ADM element that operates throughout all phases, tracking and managing changing requirements. -> Requirements\n1723: Management\n1724: A framework developed by the U.S. Federal Government to organize and manage enterprise architecture. -> FEAF\n1725: The reference model that measures how well an organization and its IT systems achieve strategic goals and desired\n1726: outcomes. -> PRM (Performance Reference Model)\n1727: The reference model that defines how data is categorized, described, shared, and managed across the enterprise. -> DRM\n1728: (Data Reference Model)\n1729: The reference model that defines security-related capabilities, controls, and requirements for protecting enterprise systems\n1730: and information. -> SRM (Security Reference Model)\n1731: The reference model that describes the organization's business functions, services, and activities. -> BRM (Business\n1732: Reference Model)\n1733: The reference model that organizes IT services and application components that support business functions. -> ARM\n1734: (Application Reference Model)\n1735: The reference model that provides a common structure for technologies, platforms, and technical standards used by the\n1736: enterprise. -> TRM (Technical Reference Model)\n1737: An EA methodology developed by Gartner that views Enterprise Architecture as a continuous process of change and\n1738: improvement, focused on business outcomes rather than technology alone. -> Gartner EA Methodology\n1739: The foundation of the EA framework; defines the organization's business strategy, governance, organization structure, and\n1740: key business processes. -> Business Architecture\n1741: The structure and management of an organization's logical and physical data assets and data management resources. ->\n1742: Data (Information) Architecture\n1743: A blueprint for the individual applications deployment, their interactions, and their relationships to core business processes. ->\n1744: Application Architecture\n1745: The hardware, software, and network infrastructure that provides the hosting and runtime environment for applications. ->\n1746: Technology Architecture\n1747: An architectural work product that describes an aspect of the architecture — the deliverables, documents, diagrams, models,\n1748: matrices, and catalogs produced throughout the EA lifecycle. -> Architecture Artifact\n1749: A hierarchical list or inventory of things that answers \"What inventory do we have?\" -> Catalog\n1750: A grid that shows the relationship between two or more architectural elements that answers \"How do elements relate to each\n1751: other?\" -> Matrix\n1752: Visual representations of architecture components and their topology or dynamics that answer \"What does the structure or\n1753: flow look like?\" -> Diagram / Model\n1754: \n1755: ===== PAGE 34 =====\n1756: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1757: Page 34 of 43\n1758: The pitfall where architectural components are isolated and alignment is lost between strategic goals and technical execution.\n1759: -> Siloed / disconnected artifacts\n1760: The four domains into which most standard EA frameworks (such as TOGAF) categorize the enterprise. -> Business, Data,\n1761: Application, Technology\n1762: L3 Identification\n1763: The formal request companies use to update systems smoothly, archiving a history for developers to follow during\n1764: implementation. -> EA Change Request (EACR)\n1765: A visual modeling technique that clearly shows the Inputs & Outputs, the \"Controls\" (rules), and the \"Mechanisms\" (tools) of a\n1766: process. -> IDEF-O\n1767: Diagrams that define how different parts of the business relate to each other (e.g., how a 'Buyer' connects to a 'Listing'). ->\n1768: ERD (Entity-Relationship Diagram)\n1769: A scenario document that covers good and bad operating environments, drawn from the SWOT Analysis, to plan defensive\n1770: responses and offensive initiatives in advance. -> CONOPS Scenario (Concept of Operations)\n1771: A short story told through the eyes of a central character living through future events, tied to planning assumptions in\n1772: process, people, and technology. -> The Storytelling Technique (Dr. Robert Neilson)\n1773: The three areas of change in the CONOPS storytelling technique. -> Process (How), People (Who), Technology (What\n1774: tools)\n1775: The technique of 'freezing' the current and future views at regular intervals (e.g., twice a year), publishing changes as a new\n1776: release in month six. -> Freeze Periods\n1777: The current, \"as-is\" structure of the enterprise — the starting point for any transformation. -> Baseline Architecture\n1778: One or more intermediate states that bridge the gap between baseline and target on a realistic timeline. -> Transition\n1779: Architecture\n1780: The desired future state — capabilities, processes, and technologies aligned to strategic goals. -> Target Architecture\n1781: The plan that guides the transition from the current architecture to the desired future architecture; a living document that\n1782: coordinates IT projects, resources, standards, governance, and implementation. -> EA Management Plan\n1783: The four main parts of an EA Management Plan. -> EA Program Management, Current Architecture Summary, Future\n1784: Architecture Summary, Glossary and References\n1785: The person who manages the EA program, documentation, standards, and configuration management. -> Chief Architect\n1786: Improvements in integration, user satisfaction, or IT investment decisions (a type of performance measure). -> Outcome\n1787: measures\n1788: Number of projects completed, scope changes, or planning activities (a type of performance measure). -> Output measures\n1789: The future-view artifact used to track \"Grow e-government service adoption 30% in 3 years.\" -> Balanced Scorecard\n1790: The tool used to trace watertight reasoning from Citizen -> Apply -> Portal -> Submit -> Records Office. -> Swim Lane\n1791: (Process) Diagram\n1792: The level of future artifacts that document the physical networks, hardware, and facilities — voice, data, and video. ->\n1793: Infrastructure Level\n1794: The level of future artifacts that documents lifecycle management of a Permit Record with CRUD operations. -> Information\n1795: Level\n1796: L4 Identification\n1797: A structured approach to organizing an organization's business, data, applications, and technology. -> Enterprise\n1798: Architecture (EA)\n1799: The simple sequence that summarizes the three areas for using an Enterprise Architecture. -> Plan, Protect, and Manage\n1800: The EA planning discipline that sits between the architecture and the projects — \"defining the architectures needed for the\n1801: business use of information — and the plan to implement them\" (Spewak, 1992). -> Enterprise Architecture Planning\n1802: (EAP)\n1803: The EAP guiding principle stating that applications manage data and technology supports applications. -> BUSINESS ->\n1804: DATA -> APPLICATIONS -> TECHNOLOGY\n1805: The rule of thumb that prevents two projects from changing the same system at the same time. -> Never run two projects\n1806: that change the same system at the same time\n1807: \n1808: ===== PAGE 35 =====\n1809: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1810: Page 35 of 43\n1811: The comparison of the As-Is against the To-Be to find what is missing, duplicated, and obsolete; every project must trace\n1812: back to this. -> Gap Analysis\n1813: The board that holds checkpoints at initiation, at design, and before go-live, ending each review in approved, approved with\n1814: conditions, or rejected. -> Architecture Review Board (ARB)\n1815: A documented exception with an expiry date — otherwise the \"temporary fix\" becomes permanent technical debt. ->\n1816: Deviation exception\n1817: The pitfall described as \"beautiful models nobody follows.\" -> Ivory-tower architecture\n1818: The four common EA pitfalls. -> Ivory-tower architecture, no executive sponsor, big-bang scope, stale artifacts\n1819: The four success factors of EA use. -> Active sponsor, small increments, reviews at project gates, living repository\n1820: The security model where people only get access to what they need for their job (e.g., HR staff can't touch finance data). ->\n1821: Role-based access control (RBAC)\n1822: The four main areas of security. -> Data, Network/Technology, Application, Physical\n1823: The master storage facility for all architectural assets, models, principles, and guidelines; maintains single-source-of-truth\n1824: visibility. -> EA Repository\n1825: The repository component that defines the underlying schema, entity types, taxonomy, and relationship rules governing how\n1826: artifacts connect. -> Architecture Metamodel\n1827: The repository component that catalogs real-time operational views, target blueprints, and active roadmap transitions. ->\n1828: Architecture Landscape\n1829: The EA support tool capability that generates dynamic visual models directly from repository data, keeping diagrams\n1830: synchronized. -> Automated Diagramming & Layout\n1831: The EA support tool capability that connects via APIs to cloud platforms, CMDBs, and source repositories to continuously\n1832: refresh landscape data. -> Automated Data Ingestion\n1833: The open-source EA tool known for ArchiMate modeling — free, lightweight graphical modeling. -> Archi (Open Source)\n1834: The EA tool known for comprehensive support for TOGAF, ArchiMate, UML, and SysML. -> Sparx Enterprise Architect\n1835: The EA tool focused on strategic IT governance & portfolio for large enterprises and financial institutions. -> Software AG\n1836: Alfabet\n1837: The EA tool focused on Application Portfolio Management for agile, cloud-native enterprise IT. -> LeanIX\n1838: The tools used to keep developer compliance: Jira, Confluence, and CI/CD pipelines. -> SDLC & DevOps alignment tools\n1839: The databases like ServiceNow or AWS Config that reconcile planned architecture with actual runtime reality. -> CMDB\n1840: (Configuration Management Database)\n1841: L5 Identification\n1842: An approach in software development where we use 'services' to create our applications. -> SOA (Service-Oriented\n1843: Architecture)\n1844: A software component we use that is assigned a task; used to make components reusable and modular. -> Service\n1845: The SOA principle that a service can be used in multiple parts of the system (e.g., Website -> Payment Service <- Mobile\n1846: App). -> Reusability\n1847: The SOA principle that changes in one service do not affect other components using the service; services should be\n1848: stateless. -> Loose Coupling\n1849: The SOA principle that customers & clients should not be able to see important logic/code. -> Abstraction\n1850: The SOA principle of dividing a large system into smaller, manageable services, each with its own responsibility. ->\n1851: Modularity\n1852: The component that creates, maintains, and provides a service. -> Service Provider\n1853: The application or system that requests and uses a service. -> Service Consumer\n1854: A directory of available services, containing information about what services are available and how consumers can access\n1855: them. -> Service Registry\n1856: The communication mode where the caller sends a request and waits for an immediate reply before continuing — common\n1857: with REST APIs. -> Synchronous communication\n1858: The communication mode where the caller sends a message and keeps working; a response arrives later via a queue or bus.\n1859: -> Asynchronous / Message-Based communication\n1860: \n1861: ===== PAGE 36 =====\n1862: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1863: Page 36 of 43\n1864: The runtime discovery mechanism where consumers locate providers through a registry (e.g., UDDI) instead of a hard-coded\n1865: address. -> Service Discovery\n1866: An architectural style (not a strict standard) that uses HTTP verbs GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, typically exchanges JSON,\n1867: and is stateless. -> REST\n1868: A strict, formal messaging standard where messages are always formatted as XML envelopes with built-in WS-Security. ->\n1869: SOAP\n1870: The data format that is verbose, tag-based, common in SOAP messages and legacy enterprise systems. -> XML\n1871: The lightweight, key-value data format that maps directly to objects and is the default for REST APIs. -> JSON\n1872: The piece of middleware that sits between services, handling routing, translation, and message delivery. -> Enterprise\n1873: Service Bus (ESB)\n1874: The ESB function that converts data formats between systems, such as XML <-> JSON. -> Transformation\n1875: The ESB function that bridges services using different transport protocols, such as HTTP, JMS, or FTP. -> Protocol\n1876: Mediation\n1877: The ESB function that coordinates calls to multiple services in sequence to complete a single business process. ->\n1878: Orchestration\n1879: The era of software architecture defined by one codebase, one development — simple to start, hard to scale. -> Monolithic\n1880: (1990s)\n1881: The era of software architecture where business logic was split into reusable services integrated over the Enterprise Service\n1882: Bus. -> SOA (2000s)\n1883: The era of software architecture with small, independently deployable services communicating over lightweight APIs. ->\n1884: Microservices (2010s - now)\n1885: The modern successor to the ESB — routing, auth, and rate-limiting for cloud APIs. -> API gateway\n1886: The tools (like Istio) that manage service-to-service communication, adding retries, encryption, and observability. -> Service\n1887: mesh\n1888: Docker and Kubernetes as the modern implementation of SOA's \"independent service\" idea at cloud scale. -> Containers &\n1889: orchestration\n1890: L6 Identification\n1891: A standard for writing data as plain text, using tags that describe what the data means. -> XML (eXtensible Markup\n1892: Language)\n1893: The XML property that means tags tell you what each value is. -> Self-describing\n1894: The part of an XML document that is always the very first line and declares version and encoding. -> Prolog\n1895: The part of an XML document that is the outermost element; exactly one per document. -> Root Element\n1896: XML metadata written inside the opening tag in the form name=\"value\"; the name cannot repeat on the same element. ->\n1897: Attribute\n1898: The property of an XML document with exactly one root, no overlapping tags, quoted attribute values, and case-sensitive\n1899: names. -> Well-formed (correct syntax)\n1900: Well-formed XML that also follows a schema (only allowed elements, required elements present, values match data types).\n\n(Showing lines 1401-1900 of 2262. Use offset=1901 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"===== PAGE 27 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 27 of 43\nAspect\nReal-time integration\nBatch integration\nBest for\nImmediacy is critical (e.g., stock trading\nplatform updates balances right after every\ntrade)\nLarge volumes that don't need immediate\nprocessing (e.g., payroll syncs attendance\nnightly)\nAdvantage\nSystems reflect the most current\ninformation\nReduces processing overhead and system\nload\nCommon challenges in application integration: data format mismatches (requires transformation), security risks (more entry\npoints for attacks), system downtime/dependency (one failure disrupts dependents), complexity as systems grow (more","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt","text":"===== PAGE 27 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 27 of 43\nAspect\nReal-time integration\nBatch integration\nBest for\nImmediacy is critical (e.g., stock trading\nplatform updates balances right after every\ntrade)\nLarge volumes that don't need immediate\nprocessing (e.g., payroll syncs attendance\nnightly)\nAdvantage\nSystems reflect the most current\ninformation\nReduces processing overhead and system\nload\nCommon challenges in application integration: data format mismatches (requires transformation), security risks (more entry\npoints for attacks), system downtime/dependency (one failure disrupts dependents), complexity as systems grow (more\nmaintenance and points of failure).\nLEGACY SYSTEM INTEGRATION\nLegacy system integration — the process of connecting and enabling communication between older, often outdated systems\n(legacy systems) and newer systems or technologies — essential for legacy systems to keep functioning within a modern IT\nenvironment.\nCore approaches (5): API Wrapping, Middleware Connectivity, Enterprise Service Bus, Integration Platform as a Service\n(iPaaS), ETL & Data Synchronization.\nWhy do businesses still use them?\n• Cost savings — avoids the massive expense and risk of rebuilding core databases.\n• Better efficiency — stops slow, manual data copying.\n• New features — adds modern tools (cloud analytics, automation, AI) on top of older systems.\n• Unified data — different teams share a single, accurate view.\n• Faster results — updates workflows quickly instead of waiting years for full replacement.\nCOMPARISON: Legacy Integration vs Implementing New Systems (COMPARISON TABLE)\nFeature\nLegacy System Integration\nNew System Implementation\nCost\nLower upfront by building on existing\ninfrastructure\nHigher investment for more capability\nDisruption\nMinimal disruption to daily workflows and\noperations\nHigh risk of operational downtime during\nthe transition\nTraining\nMinimal training needed — staff already\nknow the system\nExtensive training required for new\ninterfaces and workflows\nScalability\nLimited by the architectural ceiling of the old\nsystem\nHighly scalable and easily customizable for\nfuture growth\nChallenges of integrating legacy systems (6):\n1. Architectural Mismatch — older monolithic designs struggle to communicate with modern, API-driven or\nmicroservices-based cloud environments.\n2. Data Inconsistency — legacy databases use proprietary formats requiring complex translation logic.\n3. Security Gaps — outdated encryption and authentication protocols create vulnerabilities.\n4. Skill Shortages — finding engineers who understand obsolete languages is increasingly difficult.\n5. Missing Documentation — poor records make troubleshooting and integration risky.\n6. Downtime Risks — connecting/updating these systems often forces costly operational pauses or crashes.\nBest practices (focus on) — define clear business outcomes, map dependencies, and adopt incremental patterns like the\nStrangler Fig pattern to minimize operational risk.\n• Strategic Planning & Assessment: Define Business Outcomes; Map all dependencies; Prioritize by impact.\n• Architectural & Technical Patterns: Use incremental approaches; Employing Anti-Corruption Layer (ACL); Leverage\nmodern middleware.\n• Data & Testing Operations: Treat data as separate workstream; Automate testing; Ensure robust monitoring.\n\n===== PAGE 28 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 28 of 43\nENTERPRISE INTEGRATION\nEnterprise Integration (Enterprise Application Integration / EAI) — the process of linking separate applications, databases,\nand hardware platforms across an entire organization to work together as a coordinated ecosystem.\nGoal: create a seamless, automated flow of information and end-to-end processes across corporate systems, eliminating data\nisolation and manual work.\nKey objectives (4):\n1. Data Consistency — synchronizes records across departments to establish a reliable, single source of truth.\n2. System Interoperability — bridges legacy software, on-premises infrastructure, and modern cloud services using\nstandardized communication protocols.\n3. Business Process Automation — automates complex multi-system workflows (e.g., order-to-cash, employee\nonboarding).\n4. Real-Time Visibility — delivers integrated, real-time analytics and cross-functional reporting for executive\ndecision-making.\nEnterprise integration architectures (4):\n• Enterprise Service Bus (ESB) — a centralized middleware backbone managing routing, message transformation, and\nprotocol translation.\n• Hub-and-Spoke Model — a central broker routes traffic and translates data between connected \"spoke\" applications,\nreducing total connections.\n• Service-Oriented Architecture (SOA) — applications expose core capabilities as reusable, loosely coupled services\nacross a network.\n• Event-Driven Architecture (EDA) — systems publish event messages to an enterprise broker, letting subscribing\nservices react asynchronously in near-real time.\nCore enterprise integration patterns (4):\n• Messaging Channels — dedicated pathways for applications to send and receive asynchronous data payloads.\n• Content-Based Router — inspects incoming message contents and automatically routes data to the correct destination\nbased on business rules.\n• Message Translator — converts data payloads between incompatible structures and formats (e.g., XML to JSON, EDI to\nSQL).\n• Publish-Subscribe Channel — delivers messages from a single sender to multiple subscribed systems simultaneously\nwithout direct coupling.\nBusiness & operational benefits (4): Operational Efficiency (cuts manual entry/error), Organizational Agility (adopt/replace\ntools without rebuilding IT infrastructure), Scalability (growing volumes through organized middleware), Cost Optimization\n(preserves and extends legacy investments).\nChallenges in enterprise integration (4): High Architectural Complexity, Single Point of Failure (centralized hubs/ESBs), Data\nSecurity & Compliance, Resource & Skill Requirements.\nBest practices (4): Enforce Loose Coupling; Adopt API Standardization; Implement Centralized Monitoring; Design for High\nAvailability (failover, load balancing, message retry queues).\nINTEGRATION STRATEGIES\nIntegration strategy — determines how systems should communicate, where integration logic should live, and how the\nenvironment can scale and remain maintainable (Architecture, Data Flow, Messaging, APIs & Events).\nCommon integration strategies (5):\n• Point-to-Point — direct connection between two systems; simple for a small number of integrations, difficult to manage at\nscale.\n• Hub-and-Spoke — systems connect through a central hub that routes and transforms communication.\n• ESB — middleware acts as a communication backbone for routing, transformation, and service interactions.\n• API-Based — systems expose reusable functions and data through defined APIs.\n• Event-Driven — systems react to published events, supporting asynchronous, near-real-time flows.\nPOINT-TO-POINT vs CENTRALIZED INTEGRATION (COMPARISON TABLE)\n\n===== PAGE 29 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 29 of 43\nKey trade-off: simplicity at small scale versus centralized control and manageability as the environment grows.\nAspect\nPoint-to-Point\nHub-and-Spoke / ESB\nSetup\nFast for a few connections\nMore planning; centralized integration layer\nManagement\nEvery connection managed separately\nCentral routing and policies improve\nvisibility\nScalability\nConnection count grows quickly\nNew systems can often connect through the\nhub\nBest fit\nSmall/simple environments\nLarger, more complex enterprise\nenvironments\nAPI-based integration — APIs provide defined interfaces that allow applications to request data or functions without depending\non internal implementation details. Mobile/web app requests a service -> API layer authenticates, routes, and defines the\ninterface -> back-end provides data/functionality. Reusable (same API serves multiple clients), Controlled (auth at the interface),\nFlexible (back-ends evolve without exposing internals).\nEvent-driven integration — instead of one system directly calling every other, a system publishes an event that interested\nconsumers react to (Event Producer e.g., \"Order Created\" -> Event Broker publishes/routes -> Consumers: Inventory, Payment,\nNotification). Provides loose coupling, asynchronous flow, near-real-time response — useful when many services may need\nto react to the same business event.\nCloud & iPaaS integration — iPaaS (Integration Platform as a Service) provides cloud-hosted tools for connecting\napplications and data across cloud and on-premises environments (on-premises legacy apps/databases <-> iPaaS\nconnect/transform/monitor <-> cloud SaaS, cloud databases, analytics). Benefits: faster connection setup (reusable connectors),\nhybrid environments, centralized monitoring.\nChoosing the right integration strategy — no single strategy fits every organization; match to business needs, technical\nconstraints, and future growth:\n• Number of systems — few systems tolerate direct connections; many need stronger coordination.\n• Real-time needs — critical immediate updates favor APIs or event-driven flows.\n• Data volume — large scheduled transfers suit batch-oriented approaches.\n• Security — sensitive information needs strong authentication, authorization, monitoring.\n• Scalability — choose patterns that remain manageable as integrations increase.\n• Budget & skills — consider platform cost, maintenance effort, and available expertise.\n\n===== PAGE 30 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 30 of 43\nL8: ENTERPRISE INTEGRATION\nENTERPRISE INTEGRATION\nEnterprise Integration — the process of linking different IT systems, applications, and data sources so they can work\ntogether and share data seamlessly. Goal: make separate systems (e.g., HR, sales, inventory, finance) function as one unified\nsystem. Key idea: it's about connecting, not replacing, existing systems.\nWhy it matters: companies use many systems that don't \"talk\" to each other -> data silos, duplicate entries, inconsistent records.\nIntegration removes these gaps. Benefits: real-time data sharing, faster decisions, reduced manual work/errors, cost savings,\nbetter customer service.\nThe integration challenge: enterprises use systems built on different platforms, languages, and data formats (old legacy\nsystems vs modern cloud apps) — e.g., a sales system in one format, an accounting system in another. This mismatch is why\ncommon standards (like XML) and communication methods (like Web Services) were developed.\nApproaches to integration: Point-to-Point (direct connections; simple but hard to scale); Hub-and-Spoke (central hub manages\nconnections); Enterprise Service Bus (middleware that lets many systems communicate through one channel); API/Web\nServices-based (modern approach using standardized calls).\nKey technologies overview:\n• XML — a standard format for structuring and exchanging data between different systems, regardless of platform.\n• Web Services — standardized methods (e.g., SOAP, REST) that let applications communicate over a network.\n• SOA (Service-Oriented Architecture) — a design approach where business functions are built as reusable \"services\"\nthat can be combined and reused across the enterprise.\nXML INTEGRATION\nXML Integration — the practice of exchanging structured data between systems using XML as a common format — reliable,\nconsistent, secure, and fast.\nWhy best practices matter (failure modes — MEMORIZE):\n• Inconsistent data — same field, different tags across systems -> mapping breaks silently.\n• Invalid information — missing or malformed values corrupt downstream records.\n• Security risk — unvalidated input opens the door to injection and disclosure.\n• Slow processing — bloated payloads and deep nesting drag down throughput.\n• Hard troubleshooting — vague failures with no context waste hours per incident.\n• Compatibility issues — undocumented schema changes quietly break trading partners.\nClear structure & data consistency:\n• Structure: meaningful names (e.g., <shippingAddress>, not <sa>); logical hierarchy (order -> customer -> contact); flat &\nDRY (avoid unnecessary nesting; never duplicate a value).\n• Consistency: same tag every time (one name per concept, applied identically); consistent types & formats (quantity\nalways a number; dates always ISO 8601); define required fields (mark what must be present).\nValidate & secure the exchange:\n• Validation — layered checks: well-formed syntax; schema (XSD) check; matching tags & root; required fields & types.\n• Security — protect every hop: use HTTPS (encrypt XML in transit); never trust input (validate against schema and\nbusiness rules); guard sensitive data (mask/encrypt PII at rest and in tags); authenticate (tokens, certs, or API keys); least\nprivilege; block XML attacks (disable external entities; cap size/depth).\nOptimize performance & handle errors:\n• Performance: send only necessary data; keep XML reasonably sized (paginate/stream large collections; gzip when\nuseful); process data efficiently (prefer streaming/SAX over full DOM for large files).\n• Error handling: detect & identify (catch the failure where it occurs, pin down the root cause); give clear feedback (specific,\nactionable message — not a generic error code).\nBest practices, compounded — what you gain: reliable integrations, faster processing, accurate data exchange, easier \ntroubleshooting, better security, better compatibility. Design for the reader (structure, name, document XML as if a new engineer \nmust understand it cold); trust nothing by default (well-formed is not the same as safe); optimize the whole lifecycle (lean\n\n===== PAGE 31 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 31 of 43\npayloads, clear errors, living documentation).\nWEB SERVICES\nWhat are web services? — a standardized way for applications to communicate over a network, regardless of platform or\nlanguage. Platform-independent (works across Windows, Linux, mobile); protocols used: HTTP/HTTPS (sometimes SMTP/FTP);\ndata formats: XML or JSON; loosely coupled. Examples: payment gateways, courier tracking, social media login.\nAPIs: the gateway to web services — an API (Application Programming Interface) defines how software components should\ninteract.\n• REST APIs — most common today; used in web & mobile apps.\n• SOAP APIs — enterprise-grade; used in banking/finance.\n• GraphQL — flexible querying; request only what you need.\n• APIs act as a \"contract\" — defines available requests and expected responses; good APIs are well-documented (e.g.,\nSwagger/OpenAPI). Real-world examples: Google Maps API, Lalamove/J&T API, Stripe API.\nSOAP vs REST (COMPARISON TABLE — MEMORIZE)\nFeature\nSOAP\nREST\nType\nProtocol\nArchitectural style\nFormat\nXML only\nJSON, XML, plain text\nSecurity\nBuilt-in (WS-Security)\nDepends on implementation\nSpeed\nSlower (heavier messages)\nFaster (lightweight)\nBest for\nBanking, enterprise, government\nWeb & mobile apps\nExample\nInterbank transfer systems\nFacebook, Google Maps API\nSecurity & authentication:\n• Security: HTTPS/SSL/TLS encryption (protects data in transit); input validation (prevents SQL Injection, XSS);\nWS-Security for SOAP (message-level security); rate limiting & firewalls (prevents abuse/DDoS); data encryption at rest.\n• Authentication: API Keys, OAuth 2.0, JWT; Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA).\n• Remember: Authentication = \"who are you?\" vs Authorization = \"what can you do?\"\nReliability & performance:\n• Reliability: redundancy & failover systems; retry mechanisms; message queuing (RabbitMQ, Kafka); health monitoring &\nSLA (e.g., 99.9% uptime guarantee).\n• Performance: caching; load balancing; data compression (GZIP) & pagination; CDN (serves content closer to the user).\nWeb services best practices — checklist: use HTTPS always; choose the right protocol (SOAP vs REST); implement proper\nauthentication (OAuth, JWT, MFA); validate all inputs; design for failure (redundancy, retries, failover); optimize with caching, load\nbalancing, CDNs; document your API clearly (Swagger/OpenAPI); version your APIs (e.g., /api/v1/); monitor performance and\nuptime continuously.\nSERVICE-ORIENTED ENTERPRISE (SOE)\nService-Oriented Enterprise (SOE) — connects business systems through reusable services. It allows different applications\nand departments to communicate and share data. It focuses on integration, flexibility, and reuse of existing systems.\nExample: a school connects its student information system, payment system, library system, and learning platform through\nservices.\nService-Oriented Architecture (SOA) — an approach that organizes enterprise systems into independent, reusable services.\nServices communicate through standard interfaces and protocols. Key characteristics: loose coupling, reusability,\ninteroperability, and scalability.\nMain components of a Service-Oriented Enterprise (5 — MEMORIZE):\n1. Service Provider — creates and maintains a service.\n2. Service Consumer — application or user that uses the service.\n3. Service Registry / Broker — helps consumers find available services.\n\n===== PAGE 32 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 32 of 43\n4. Service Interface — defines how a service can be accessed.\n5. Enterprise Service Bus (ESB) — connects and manages communication between services.\nBuilding the Service-Oriented Enterprise (6 steps):\n1. Identify business processes that need integration.\n2. Identify existing systems and services that can be reused.\n3. Design services based on specific business functions.\n4. Create standard interfaces for communication.\n5. Integrate, test, and monitor the services.\n6. Maintain and improve services as business needs change.\nBenefits and Challenges:\n• Benefits: better system integration and flexibility; reuse of services reduces development time and costs; easier\nmaintenance and expansion.\n• Challenges: security and access control; complex integration between different systems; requires proper service\nmanagement and monitoring.\nSummary: a Service-Oriented Enterprise connects business systems through reusable services; SOA provides the structure for\ndesigning and integrating these services; key components include providers, consumers, interfaces, registries, and ESBs. The\ngoal is an enterprise that is integrated, flexible, reusable, and easier to manage.\n\n===== PAGE 33 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 33 of 43\nIDENTIFICATION BANK\nMatch the exact wording you may see in the exam.\nL2 Identification\nA detailed, step-by-step approach that describes how an Enterprise Architecture program is established, developed,\nmanaged, maintained, and used within an organization. -> EA Implementation Methodology\nA structured set of tools, models, and guidelines used to organize and design an enterprise's architecture. -> EA Framework\nDeveloped by John Zachman in 1987; not a methodology but a classification schema organized as a 6x6 matrix. -> Zachman\nFramework\nAn EA framework used to help organizations design, plan, implement, and manage their enterprise architecture. -> TOGAF\nTOGAF's method that helps organizations identify business requirements, analyze the current architecture, design the target\narchitecture, identify changes, plan implementation, and manage/review the architecture. -> ADM (Architecture\nDevelopment Method)\nThe ADM phase that establishes EA principles and governance and defines roles, responsibilities, and processes. ->\nPreliminary Phase — Preparation\nThe ADM phase that defines goals and scope, identifies stakeholders, and creates the overall architecture vision. -> Phase A\n— Architecture Vision\nThe ADM phase that defines infrastructure, networks, platforms, and technologies. -> Phase D — Technology Architecture\nThe ADM element that operates throughout all phases, tracking and managing changing requirements. -> Requirements\nManagement\nA framework developed by the U.S. Federal Government to organize and manage enterprise architecture. -> FEAF\nThe reference model that measures how well an organization and its IT systems achieve strategic goals and desired\noutcomes. -> PRM (Performance Reference Model)\nThe reference model that defines how data is categorized, described, shared, and managed across the enterprise. -> DRM\n(Data Reference Model)\nThe reference model that defines security-related capabilities, controls, and requirements for protecting enterprise systems\nand information. -> SRM (Security Reference Model)\nThe reference model that describes the organization's business functions, services, and activities. -> BRM (Business\nReference Model)\nThe reference model that organizes IT services and application components that support business functions. -> ARM\n(Application Reference Model)\nThe reference model that provides a common structure for technologies, platforms, and technical standards used by the\nenterprise. -> TRM (Technical Reference Model)\nAn EA methodology developed by Gartner that views Enterprise Architecture as a continuous process of change and\nimprovement, focused on business outcomes rather than technology alone. -> Gartner EA Methodology\nThe foundation of the EA framework; defines the organization's business strategy, governance, organization structure, and\nkey business processes. -> Business Architecture\nThe structure and management of an organization's logical and physical data assets and data management resources. ->\nData (Information) Architecture\nA blueprint for the individual applications deployment, their interactions, and their relationships to core business processes. ->\nApplication Architecture\nThe hardware, software, and network infrastructure that provides the hosting and runtime environment for applications. ->\nTechnology Architecture\nAn architectural work product that describes an aspect of the architecture — the deliverables, documents, diagrams, models,\nmatrices, and catalogs produced throughout the EA lifecycle. -> Architecture Artifact\nA hierarchical list or inventory of things that answers \"What inventory do we have?\" -> Catalog\nA grid that shows the relationship between two or more architectural elements that answers \"How do elements relate to each\nother?\" -> Matrix\nVisual representations of architecture components and their topology or dynamics that answer \"What does the structure or\nflow look like?\" -> Diagram / Model\n\n===== PAGE 34 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 34 of 43\nThe pitfall where architectural components are isolated and alignment is lost between strategic goals and technical execution.\n-> Siloed / disconnected artifacts\nThe four domains into which most standard EA frameworks (such as TOGAF) categorize the enterprise. -> Business, Data,\nApplication, Technology\nL3 Identification\nThe formal request companies use to update systems smoothly, archiving a history for developers to follow during\nimplementation. -> EA Change Request (EACR)\nA visual modeling technique that clearly shows the Inputs & Outputs, the \"Controls\" (rules), and the \"Mechanisms\" (tools) of a\nprocess. -> IDEF-O\nDiagrams that define how different parts of the business relate to each other (e.g., how a 'Buyer' connects to a 'Listing'). ->\nERD (Entity-Relationship Diagram)\nA scenario document that covers good and bad operating environments, drawn from the SWOT Analysis, to plan defensive\nresponses and offensive initiatives in advance. -> CONOPS Scenario (Concept of Operations)\nA short story told through the eyes of a central character living through future events, tied to planning assumptions in\nprocess, people, and technology. -> The Storytelling Technique (Dr. Robert Neilson)\nThe three areas of change in the CONOPS storytelling technique. -> Process (How), People (Who), Technology (What\ntools)\nThe technique of 'freezing' the current and future views at regular intervals (e.g., twice a year), publishing changes as a new\nrelease in month six. -> Freeze Periods\nThe current, \"as-is\" structure of the enterprise — the starting point for any transformation. -> Baseline Architecture\nOne or more intermediate states that bridge the gap between baseline and target on a realistic timeline. -> Transition\nArchitecture\nThe desired future state — capabilities, processes, and technologies aligned to strategic goals. -> Target Architecture\nThe plan that guides the transition from the current architecture to the desired future architecture; a living document that\ncoordinates IT projects, resources, standards, governance, and implementation. -> EA Management Plan\nThe four main parts of an EA Management Plan. -> EA Program Management, Current Architecture Summary, Future\nArchitecture Summary, Glossary and References\nThe person who manages the EA program, documentation, standards, and configuration management. -> Chief Architect\nImprovements in integration, user satisfaction, or IT investment decisions (a type of performance measure). -> Outcome\nmeasures\nNumber of projects completed, scope changes, or planning activities (a type of performance measure). -> Output measures\nThe future-view artifact used to track \"Grow e-government service adoption 30% in 3 years.\" -> Balanced Scorecard\nThe tool used to trace watertight reasoning from Citizen -> Apply -> Portal -> Submit -> Records Office. -> Swim Lane\n(Process) Diagram\nThe level of future artifacts that document the physical networks, hardware, and facilities — voice, data, and video. ->\nInfrastructure Level\nThe level of future artifacts that documents lifecycle management of a Permit Record with CRUD operations. -> Information\nLevel\nL4 Identification\nA structured approach to organizing an organization's business, data, applications, and technology. -> Enterprise\nArchitecture (EA)\nThe simple sequence that summarizes the three areas for using an Enterprise Architecture. -> Plan, Protect, and Manage\nThe EA planning discipline that sits between the architecture and the projects — \"defining the architectures needed for the\nbusiness use of information — and the plan to implement them\" (Spewak, 1992). -> Enterprise Architecture Planning\n(EAP)\nThe EAP guiding principle stating that applications manage data and technology supports applications. -> BUSINESS ->\nDATA -> APPLICATIONS -> TECHNOLOGY\nThe rule of thumb that prevents two projects from changing the same system at the same time. -> Never run two projects\nthat change the same system at the same time\n\n===== PAGE 35 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 35 of 43\nThe comparison of the As-Is against the To-Be to find what is missing, duplicated, and obsolete; every project must trace\nback to this. -> Gap Analysis\nThe board that holds checkpoints at initiation, at design, and before go-live, ending each review in approved, approved with\nconditions, or rejected. -> Architecture Review Board (ARB)\nA documented exception with an expiry date — otherwise the \"temporary fix\" becomes permanent technical debt. ->\nDeviation exception\nThe pitfall described as \"beautiful models nobody follows.\" -> Ivory-tower architecture\nThe four common EA pitfalls. -> Ivory-tower architecture, no executive sponsor, big-bang scope, stale artifacts\nThe four success factors of EA use. -> Active sponsor, small increments, reviews at project gates, living repository\nThe security model where people only get access to what they need for their job (e.g., HR staff can't touch finance data). ->\nRole-based access control (RBAC)\nThe four main areas of security. -> Data, Network/Technology, Application, Physical\nThe master storage facility for all architectural assets, models, principles, and guidelines; maintains single-source-of-truth\nvisibility. -> EA Repository\nThe repository component that defines the underlying schema, entity types, taxonomy, and relationship rules governing how\nartifacts connect. -> Architecture Metamodel\nThe repository component that catalogs real-time operational views, target blueprints, and active roadmap transitions. ->\nArchitecture Landscape\nThe EA support tool capability that generates dynamic visual models directly from repository data, keeping diagrams\nsynchronized. -> Automated Diagramming & Layout\nThe EA support tool capability that connects via APIs to cloud platforms, CMDBs, and source repositories to continuously\nrefresh landscape data. -> Automated Data Ingestion\nThe open-source EA tool known for ArchiMate modeling — free, lightweight graphical modeling. -> Archi (Open Source)\nThe EA tool known for comprehensive support for TOGAF, ArchiMate, UML, and SysML. -> Sparx Enterprise Architect\nThe EA tool focused on strategic IT governance & portfolio for large enterprises and financial institutions. -> Software AG\nAlfabet\nThe EA tool focused on Application Portfolio Management for agile, cloud-native enterprise IT. -> LeanIX\nThe tools used to keep developer compliance: Jira, Confluence, and CI/CD pipelines. -> SDLC & DevOps alignment tools\nThe databases like ServiceNow or AWS Config that reconcile planned architecture with actual runtime reality. -> CMDB\n(Configuration Management Database)\nL5 Identification\nAn approach in software development where we use 'services' to create our applications. -> SOA (Service-Oriented\nArchitecture)\nA software component we use that is assigned a task; used to make components reusable and modular. -> Service\nThe SOA principle that a service can be used in multiple parts of the system (e.g., Website -> Payment Service <- Mobile\nApp). -> Reusability\nThe SOA principle that changes in one service do not affect other components using the service; services should be\nstateless. -> Loose Coupling\nThe SOA principle that customers & clients should not be able to see important logic/code. -> Abstraction\nThe SOA principle of dividing a large system into smaller, manageable services, each with its own responsibility. ->\nModularity\nThe component that creates, maintains, and provides a service. -> Service Provider\nThe application or system that requests and uses a service. -> Service Consumer\nA directory of available services, containing information about what services are available and how consumers can access\nthem. -> Service Registry\nThe communication mode where the caller sends a request and waits for an immediate reply before continuing — common\nwith REST APIs. -> Synchronous communication\nThe communication mode where the caller sends a message and keeps working; a response arrives later via a queue or bus.\n-> Asynchronous / Message-Based communication\n\n===== PAGE 36 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 36 of 43\nThe runtime discovery mechanism where consumers locate providers through a registry (e.g., UDDI) instead of a hard-coded\naddress. -> Service Discovery\nAn architectural style (not a strict standard) that uses HTTP verbs GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, typically exchanges JSON,\nand is stateless. -> REST\nA strict, formal messaging standard where messages are always formatted as XML envelopes with built-in WS-Security. ->\nSOAP\nThe data format that is verbose, tag-based, common in SOAP messages and legacy enterprise systems. -> XML\nThe lightweight, key-value data format that maps directly to objects and is the default for REST APIs. -> JSON\nThe piece of middleware that sits between services, handling routing, translation, and message delivery. -> Enterprise\nService Bus (ESB)\nThe ESB function that converts data formats between systems, such as XML <-> JSON. -> Transformation\nThe ESB function that bridges services using different transport protocols, such as HTTP, JMS, or FTP. -> Protocol\nMediation\nThe ESB function that coordinates calls to multiple services in sequence to complete a single business process. ->\nOrchestration\nThe era of software architecture defined by one codebase, one development — simple to start, hard to scale. -> Monolithic\n(1990s)\nThe era of software architecture where business logic was split into reusable services integrated over the Enterprise Service\nBus. -> SOA (2000s)\nThe era of software architecture with small, independently deployable services communicating over lightweight APIs. ->\nMicroservices (2010s - now)\nThe modern successor to the ESB — routing, auth, and rate-limiting for cloud APIs. -> API gateway\nThe tools (like Istio) that manage service-to-service communication, adding retries, encryption, and observability. -> Service\nmesh\nDocker and Kubernetes as the modern implementation of SOA's \"independent service\" idea at cloud scale. -> Containers &\norchestration\nL6 Identification\nA standard for writing data as plain text, using tags that describe what the data means. -> XML (eXtensible Markup\nLanguage)\nThe XML property that means tags tell you what each value is. -> Self-describing\nThe part of an XML document that is always the very first line and declares version and encoding. -> Prolog\nThe part of an XML document that is the outermost element; exactly one per document. -> Root Element\nXML metadata written inside the opening tag in the form name=\"value\"; the name cannot repeat on the same element. ->\nAttribute\nThe property of an XML document with exactly one root, no overlapping tags, quoted attribute values, and case-sensitive\nnames. -> Well-formed (correct syntax)\nWell-formed XML that also follows a schema (only allowed elements, required elements present, values match data types).","lineStart":1401,"lineEnd":1900,"totalLines":2262,"truncated":true}},"title":"xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt","time":{"start":1789817817458,"end":1789817817523}},"id":"prt_0b9748866001BmTbE2i4Uhpk25","sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","messageID":"msg_0b9747ee5001MZlHnr4OGvGdsw"}}
