{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789817819232,"sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_0a96c52e2a194920a2897615","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt","offset":1901},"output":"<path>C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1901: -> Valid\n1902: The old schema language written in its own syntax (not XML), with no data types and no namespace support. -> DTD\n1903: (Document Type Definition)\n1904: The modern W3C-standard schema language written in XML itself, with real data types (integer, date, decimal, boolean) and\n1905: namespace support. -> XSD (XML Schema Definition)\n1906: The XML tool that FINDS/ locates one specific part of the document, written like a folder path. -> XPath\n1907: The XML tool that SEARCHES XML — what SQL does for tables. -> XQuery\n1908: The XML tool that CONVERTS an XML file into a different format (HTML, text, or XML). -> XSLT\n1909: The parser that loads the whole document into memory as a tree; best for small files to modify. -> DOM\n1910: The parser that streams through and fires events at your code; read once, forward-only, best for very large files. -> SAX\n1911: The parser where your code asks for the next piece when ready; same efficiency as SAX but you control the loop. -> StAX\n1912: The approach that converts XML directly into objects (e.g., <students> becomes a student object with getName()). -> Data\n1913: Binding (JAXB)\n1914: \n1915: ===== PAGE 37 =====\n1916: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1917: Page 37 of 43\n1918: The gap between the XML tree and a relational database's flat tables. -> Impedance mismatch\n1919: The kind of XML document with regular, predictable structure where order doesn't matter (invoices, config files). ->\n1920: Data-Centric\n1921: The kind of XML document with irregular, mixed content where order matters (manuals, contracts, articles). ->\n1922: Document-Centric\n1923: A normal relational database with XML features added (Oracle XML DB, SQL Server, Db2 pureXML). -> XML-enabled\n1924: database\n1925: An XML storage database where the document itself is the unit of storage (eXist-db, BaseX, MarkLogic). -> Native XML\n1926: database\n1927: The XML storage technique where the tree is broken into rows and columns — fast joins but the original document no longer\n1928: exists. -> Shredding\n1929: The standardized format for global interbank payments. -> ISO 20022\n1930: The standardized format family for healthcare & clinical data. -> HL7 / CDA\n1931: The standardized format family for B2B supply chains. -> RosettaNet / ebXML\n1932: A protocol for exchanging structured information over a network; traditionally uses XML and follows strict rules. -> SOAP\n1933: An architectural style to design web-based services and APIs; simpler and lighter than SOAP; uses HTTP methods. -> REST\n1934: The XML-based language that acts like a technical contract for a SOAP service. -> WSDL (Web Services Description\n1935: Language)\n1936: The directory service created to publish and discover web services; important early, less used today. -> UDDI (Universal\n1937: Description, Discovery, and Integration)\n1938: The family of specifications built on top of SOAP and XML that add security, reliability, and transactions. -> WS-*\n1939: technologies\n1940: The WS- spec that adds authentication, encryption, and message integrity to SOAP messages (often with SAML assertions).\n1941: -> WS-Security*\n1942: The WS- spec that ensures messages are still delivered correctly even over unstable networks. -> WS-ReliableMessaging*\n1943: The integration approach where a central process directs how services interact, coordinating from the top down. ->\n1944: Orchestration\n1945: The integration approach where services communicate directly peer-to-peer with no single controlling process. ->\n1946: Choreography\n1947: L7 Identification\n1948: The process of enabling independently designed applications to communicate, exchange data, and work together as a\n1949: coordinated system. -> Application Integration\n1950: The direct, custom-built connection between two systems that is generally not reusable and leads to \"spaghetti\" architecture\n1951: at scale. -> Point-to-point integration\n1952: A central hub that manages and routes communication between multiple systems, reducing the total number of connections.\n1953: -> Hub-and-spoke integration\n1954: The middleware layer that acts as a common \"communication backbone\" routing and transforming messages between\n1955: systems. -> Enterprise Service Bus (ESB)\n1956: A modern approach where applications expose specific functions through APIs that other systems call. -> API-based /\n1957: microservices integration\n1958: Integration where data is synced immediately as changes occur (e.g., stock trading updates balances right after a trade). ->\n1959: Real-time integration\n1960: Integration where data is collected and transferred at scheduled intervals (e.g., payroll syncs attendance overnight). -> Batch\n1961: integration\n1962: The process of connecting older, often outdated systems with newer systems or technologies. -> Legacy system\n1963: integration\n1964: An incremental modernization pattern that minimizes operational risk when migrating legacy systems. -> Strangler Fig\n1965: pattern\n1966: A pattern that protects a legacy system from a new system's data model changes. -> Anti-Corruption Layer (ACL)\n1967: \n1968: ===== PAGE 38 =====\n1969: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n1970: Page 38 of 43\n1971: The process of linking separate applications, databases, and hardware platforms across an entire organization to work as a\n1972: coordinated ecosystem. -> Enterprise Integration / EAI\n1973: The architecture where systems publish event messages to an enterprise broker and subscribing services react\n1974: asynchronously. -> Event-Driven Architecture (EDA)\n1975: The pattern that delivers messages from a single sender to multiple subscribed systems simultaneously. ->\n1976: Publish-Subscribe Channel\n1977: The pattern that inspects incoming message contents and routes data to the correct destination based on business rules. ->\n1978: Content-Based Router\n1979: The pattern that converts data payloads between incompatible structures (e.g., XML to JSON, EDI to SQL). -> Message\n1980: Translator\n1981: The cloud-hosted toolset for connecting applications and data across cloud and on-premises environments. -> iPaaS\n1982: (Integration Platform as a Service)\n1983: L8 Identification\n1984: The process of linking different IT systems, applications, and data sources so they can work together and share data\n1985: seamlessly. -> Enterprise Integration\n1986: The practice of exchanging structured data between systems using XML as a common format. -> XML Integration\n1987: A standardized way for applications to communicate over a network, regardless of platform or language. -> Web Services\n1988: An API type that is the most common today, used in web & mobile apps. -> REST API\n1989: An API type that is enterprise-grade, used in banking/finance. -> SOAP API\n1990: The API approach that offers flexible querying — request only what you need. -> GraphQL\n1991: The security standard family that provides message-level security for SOAP. -> WS-Security\n1992: The formula that distinguishes authentication from authorization. -> Authentication = \"who are you?\" vs Authorization =\n1993: \"what can you do?\"\n1994: \"Well-formed is not the same as ....\" -> safe\n1995: An enterprise that connects business systems through reusable services. -> Service-Oriented Enterprise (SOE)\n1996: The SOE component that defines how a service can be accessed. -> Service Interface\n1997: The SOE component that helps consumers find available services. -> Service Registry / Broker\n1998: The goal of a Service-Oriented Enterprise. -> An enterprise that is integrated, flexible, reusable, and easier to manage\n1999: \n2000: ===== PAGE 39 =====\n2001: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n2002: Page 39 of 43\n2003: COMPREHENSIVE ACRONYMS LIST\n2004: Acronym\n2005: Meaning\n2006: EA\n2007: Enterprise Architecture\n2008: ADM\n2009: Architecture Development Method\n2010: TOGAF\n2011: The Open Group Architecture Framework\n2012: FEAF\n2013: Federal Enterprise Architecture Framework\n2014: PRM\n2015: Performance Reference Model\n2016: DRM\n2017: Data Reference Model\n2018: SRM\n2019: Security Reference Model\n2020: BRM\n2021: Business Reference Model\n2022: ARM\n2023: Application Reference Model\n2024: TRM\n2025: Technical Reference Model\n2026: EAP\n2027: Enterprise Architecture Planning\n2028: PMO\n2029: Project Management Office\n2030: ARB\n2031: Architecture Review Board\n2032: RBAC\n2033: Role-Based Access Control\n2034: EACR\n2035: EA Change Request\n2036: CONOPS\n2037: Concept of Operations\n2038: ERD\n2039: Entity-Relationship Diagram\n2040: IDEF-O\n2041: Integration DEFinition / Process modeling (Inputs, Controls,\n2042: Outputs, Mechanisms)\n2043: SWOT\n2044: Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, Threats\n2045: MDM\n2046: Master Data Management\n2047: IaaS / PaaS\n2048: Infrastructure as a Service / Platform as a Service\n2049: BPMN\n2050: Business Process Model and Notation\n2051: DMZ\n2052: Demilitarized Zone\n2053: CRM\n2054: Customer Relationship Management\n2055: ERP\n2056: Enterprise Resource Planning\n2057: CMDB\n2058: Configuration Management Database\n2059: ITSM\n2060: IT Service Management\n2061: SDLC\n2062: Software Development Life Cycle\n2063: CI/CD\n2064: Continuous Integration / Continuous Delivery\n2065: SOA\n2066: Service-Oriented Architecture\n2067: SOE\n2068: Service-Oriented Enterprise\n2069: ESB\n2070: Enterprise Service Bus\n2071: WSDL\n2072: Web Services Description Language\n2073: UDDI\n2074: Universal Description, Discovery, and Integration\n2075: \n2076: ===== PAGE 40 =====\n2077: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n2078: Page 40 of 43\n2079: Acronym\n2080: Meaning\n2081: REST\n2082: Representational State Transfer\n2083: SOAP\n2084: Simple Object Access Protocol\n2085: HTTP / HTTPS\n2086: Hypertext Transfer Protocol / HTTP Secure\n2087: TLS\n2088: Transport Layer Security\n2089: XML\n2090: eXtensible Markup Language\n2091: JSON\n2092: JavaScript Object Notation\n2093: DTD\n2094: Document Type Definition\n2095: XSD\n2096: XML Schema Definition\n2097: XPath / XQuery / XSLT\n2098: XML locating / searching / transforming tools\n2099: DOM / SAX / StAX\n2100: XML parsing models (tree / event-streaming / pull-streaming)\n2101: JAXB\n2102: Java Architecture for XML Binding (data binding)\n2103: CRUD\n2104: Create, Read, Update, Delete\n2105: EAI\n2106: Enterprise Application Integration\n2107: iPaaS\n2108: Integration Platform as a Service\n2109: EDA\n2110: Event-Driven Architecture\n2111: ACL\n2112: Anti-Corruption Layer\n2113: ETL\n2114: Extract, Transform, Load\n2115: OAuth 2.0 / JWT\n2116: Open Authorization 2.0 / JSON Web Token\n2117: MFA\n2118: Multi-Factor Authentication\n2119: SLA\n2120: Service-Level Agreement\n2121: CDN\n2122: Content Delivery Network\n2123: API\n2124: Application Programming Interface\n2125: WS-*\n2126: Web Services specifications family (security, reliability, transactions)\n2127: SAML\n2128: Security Assertion Markup Language\n2129: B2B\n2130: Business-to-Business\n2131: ISO 20022\n2132: Global interbank payment messaging standard\n2133: HL7 / CDA\n2134: Healthcare data exchange standard / Clinical Document\n2135: Architecture\n2136: ebXML\n2137: Electronic Business using XML\n2138: gRPC\n2139: Google Remote Procedure Call\n2140: AWS EC2 / VPC\n2141: Amazon Web Services Elastic Compute Cloud / Virtual Private\n2142: Cloud\n2143: GZIP\n2144: File/directory compression tool\n2145: XSS\n2146: Cross-Site Scripting\n2147: DDos\n2148: Distributed Denial of Service\n2149: \n2150: ===== PAGE 41 =====\n2151: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n2152: Page 41 of 43\n2153: TOP THINGS TO MEMORIZE\n2154: L2 — Enterprise Architecture Components\n2155: 1. 4 Phases of EA Implementation — (I) EA Program Establishment, (II) Framework & Tool Selection, (III) Documentation\n2156: of the EA, (IV) Use & Maintain the EA.\n2157: 2. Zachman Framework — John Zachman, 1987; 6x6 matrix (36 cells); columns = What/How/Where/Who/When/Why; rows\n2158: = Planner, Owner, Designer, Builder, Sub-Contractor, Enterprise.\n2159: 3. Choosing the right framework — organize/classify -> Zachman; detailed development process -> TOGAF;\n2160: government/public sector -> FEAF; flexibility & business alignment -> Gartner.\n2161: 4. Four Core EA Components — Business (foundation), Data/Information, Application, Technology.\n2162: 5. Classification of Artifacts — Catalogs (inventory), Matrices (relationships), Diagrams/Models (visual representation).\n2163: 6. Traceability chain — Business -> Data -> Application -> Technology; used for Impact Analysis.\n2164: 7. FEAF Reference Models — PRM, DRM, SRM, BRM, ARM, TRM.\n2165: 8. TOGAF ADM Cycle — Preliminary, A (Vision) through H (Change Management) + Requirements Management (operates\n2166: throughout).\n2167: L3 — Developing an Enterprise Architecture\n2168: 9. Documentation before planning — plans built without an accurate baseline underestimate cost, timeline, and\n2169: complexity.\n2170: 10. CONOPS Scenario — good & bad environments from SWOT; Storytelling Technique (Dr. Robert Neilson):\n2171: Process/People/Technology.\n2172: 11. Freeze periods — freeze current & future views twice a year; changes accumulate 4-5 months; publish in month six.\n2173: 12. TOGAF architecture states — Baseline (as-is), Transition (intermediate), Target (desired future).\n2174: 13. EA Management Plan parts (4) — EA Program Management, Current Architecture Summary, Future Architecture\n2175: Summary, Glossary & References.\n2176: 14. Chief Architect — manages the EA program, documentation, standards, and configuration management.\n2177: 15. Outcome vs Output measures — outcomes = integration/user satisfaction/IT decisions; outputs = projects\n2178: completed/scope changes/planning activities.\n2179: L4 — Using an Enterprise Architecture 16. Plan, Protect, and Manage — Planning & Project Management, Security,\n2180: Repository & Support Tools. 17. EAP Guiding Principle — BUSINESS -> DATA -> APPLICATIONS -> TECHNOLOGY\n2181: (business-driven, data-driven, never technology-driven). 18. From Architecture to Projects — Gap Analysis -> Work Packages\n2182: -> Business Case -> Roadmap. 19. Common Pitfalls (4) — ivory-tower architecture, no executive sponsor, big-bang scope,\n2183: stale artifacts. 20. 4 Main Areas of Security — data, network/technology, application, physical. 21. EA Repository — central\n2184: knowledge base; single source of truth for As-Is, To-Be, Transition. 22. EA Support Tool Capabilities (4) — automated\n2185: diagramming, impact & risk analysis, collaborative governance, automated data ingestion.\n2186: L5 — All About SOA 23. SOA Core Principles (4) — Reusability, Loose Coupling, Abstraction, Modularity. 24. Main\n2187: Components of SOA (3) — Service Provider, Service Consumer, Service Registry. 25. ESB Core Functions (6) — Routing,\n2188: Transformation, Protocol Mediation, Orchestration, Monitoring & Logging, Security Enforcement. 26. REST vs SOAP — REST =\n2189: architectural style, JSON, GET/POST/PUT/DELETE, stateless; SOAP = strict standard, XML envelopes, WS-Security. 27.\n2190: Evolution — Monolithic (1990s) -> SOA (2000s) -> Microservices (2010s-now). 28. Monolithic vs SOA — single unit vs\n2191: independent deployment; one stack vs diverse; whole-app scaling vs targeted scaling; full-system failure vs isolated failure. 29.\n2192: SOA vs Microservices — ESB-mediated vs direct REST/gRPC; shared data vs per-service data; centralized vs decentralized\n2193: governance. 30. SOA's Legacy — API gateways, service mesh (Istio), containers & orchestration (Docker/Kubernetes), hybrid &\n2194: legacy integration.\n2195: L6 — Integrating Technology 31. XML properties — self-describing, extensible, platform neutral, data not design. 32. XML\n2196: document parts — prolog, root element, child element, attribute, element content, nesting. 33. Well-formed vs Valid —\n2197: well-formed = correct syntax; valid = well-formed + follows a DTD/XSD. 34. DTD vs XSD — DTD = old, own syntax, no data\n2198: types; XSD = W3C standard, written in XML, real data types, namespaces. 35. XPath finds, XQuery searches, XSLT converts.\n2199: 36. Reading XML — DOM (tree, edit), SAX (streaming, read-only), StAX (pull streaming), JAXB (data binding). 37. Storing XML\n2200: — XML-enabled DB, Native XML DB, Hybrid/XML column; shredding / CLOB / native tree. 38. XML integration standards —\n2201: ISO 20022 (interbank payments), HL7/CDA (healthcare), RosettaNet/ebXML (B2B supply chains). 39. Web service tech —\n2202: SOAP, REST, XML, JSON, HTTP/HTTPS, WSDL (the contract), UDDI (the directory).\n2203: \n2204: ===== PAGE 42 =====\n2205: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n2206: Page 42 of 43\n2207: L7 — Integrating Applications 40. Integration styles — Point-to-Point, Hub-and-Spoke, ESB, API-based/Microservices. 41.\n2208: Real-time vs Batch — real-time = immediate sync (stock trading); batch = scheduled intervals (overnight payroll). 42. Legacy\n2209: integration approaches (5) — API Wrapping, Middleware Connectivity, ESB, iPaaS, ETL & Data Synchronization. 43. Legacy\n2210: challenges (6) — architectural mismatch, data inconsistency, security gaps, skill shortages, missing documentation, downtime\n2211: risks. 44. Enterprise integration architectures — ESB, Hub-and-Spoke, SOA, Event-Driven (EDA).\n2212: L8 — Enterprise Integration 45. Enterprise Integration — connecting, not replacing, existing systems. 46. XML integration\n2213: best practices — validate everything (well-formed is not safe), use HTTPS, least privilege, block XML attacks. 47. SOAP vs\n2214: REST (L8) — SOAP = protocol/XML-only/built-in security/slower/enterprise; REST =\n2215: style/JSON/implementation-dependent/faster/web & mobile. 48. Auth vs Authorization — Authentication = \"who are you?\";\n2216: Authorization = \"what can you do?\" 49. SOE Main Components (5) — Provider, Consumer, Registry/Broker, Interface, ESB. 50.\n2217: WS-* specs — WSDL (describe), UDDI (discover), WS-Security (secure), WS-ReliableMessaging (reliable).\n2218: \n2219: ===== PAGE 43 =====\n2220: SAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\n2221: Page 43 of 43\n2222: QUICK CRAM SHEET (HIGHEST-YIELD FACTS)\n2223: EA Methodology vs Framework — Methodology = HOW the EA program is run; Framework = WHAT structure organizes the\n2224: EA.\n2225: Zachman — 1987, John Zachman, 6x6 = 36 cells, classification schema (not methodology), columns =\n2226: What/How/Where/Who/When/Why, rows = Planner/Owner/Designer/Builder/Sub-Contractor/Enterprise.\n2227: FEAF models mnemonic — Performance, Data, Security, Business, Application, Technical.\n2228: 4 EA layers — Business -> Data -> Application -> Technology (Business is the foundation).\n2229: Artifacts — Catalogs = inventory, Matrices = relationships, Diagrams/Models = visual/structure.\n2230: ADM cycle order — Preliminary -> A Vision -> B Business -> C Info Systems -> D Technology -> E Opportunities -> F Migration\n2231: -> G Governance -> H Change (+ Requirements Mgmt throughout).\n2232: 2 KEY EA numbers — Zachman 36 cells; Freeze periods twice a year (publish month 6).\n2233: EAP principle — Business -> Data -> Applications -> Technology; business-driven and data-driven, NEVER technology-driven.\n2234: 4 EAs to projects — Gap Analysis, Work Packages, Business Case, Roadmap.\n2235: Plan -> Protect -> Manage (the three EA usage areas).\n2236: SOA 4 principles — Reusability, Loose Coupling, Abstraction, Modularity.\n2237: SOA 3 components — Provider, Consumer, Registry.\n2238: ESB 6 functions — Routing, Transformation, Protocol Mediation, Orchestration, Monitoring & Logging, Security Enforcement.\n2239: REST vs SOAP — REST: style, JSON, fast, web/mobile; SOAP: protocol, XML, secure, banking/enterprise.\n2240: Architecture eras — Monolithic (90s) -> SOA (2000s) -> Microservices (2010s-now).\n2241: Microservices fixes over SOA — no central bus (ESB = single point of failure), per-service data, autonomous teams, isolated\n2242: failure.\n2243: SOA legacy — API gateways, service mesh (Istio), Docker/Kubernetes, hybrid integration.\n2244: XML 4 properties — self-describing, extensible, platform neutral, data not design.\n2245: XML parts — prolog, root, child, attribute, content, nesting.\n2246: Well-formed vs valid — well-formed = syntax correct; valid = well-formed + follows schema.\n2247: DTD vs XSD — DTD old/no types; XSD modern/W3C/real types/namespaces.\n2248: XML 3 tools — XPath finds, XQuery searches, XSLT converts.\n2249: XML parsing — DOM (tree), SAX (events), StAX (pull), JAXB (objects).\n2250: Impedance mismatch — XML tree vs database tables.\n2251: 3 ways to store XML — XML-enabled DB, native XML DB, hybrid/XML column.\n2252: Integration styles — Point-to-Point, Hub-and-Spoke, ESB, API-based, Event-driven.\n2253: Legacy vs New — legacy = cheaper, less disruption, less training, limited scalability; new = pricier, more downtime, more\n2254: training, highly scalable.\n2255: EAI objectives — data consistency, system interoperability, business process automation, real-time visibility.\n2256: Integration patterns — messaging channels, content-based router, message translator, publish-subscribe.\n2257: Web service stack — SOAP + WSDL + UDDI (+ WS-* for security/reliability).\n2258: Authentication vs Authorization — \"who are you?\" vs \"what can you do?\"\n2259: SOE components (5) — Provider, Consumer, Registry/Broker, Interface, ESB.\n2260: XML integration mindset — structure clearly, validate & secure every hop, optimize performance, handle errors with clear\n2261: feedback, design for the reader, trust nothing by default.\n2262: End of Reviewer — generated from SAI Reference (L2-L8). Study hard — you've got this!\n\n(End of file - total 2262 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"-> Valid\nThe old schema language written in its own syntax (not XML), with no data types and no namespace support. -> DTD\n(Document Type Definition)\nThe modern W3C-standard schema language written in XML itself, with real data types (integer, date, decimal, boolean) and\nnamespace support. -> XSD (XML Schema Definition)\nThe XML tool that FINDS/ locates one specific part of the document, written like a folder path. -> XPath\nThe XML tool that SEARCHES XML — what SQL does for tables. -> XQuery\nThe XML tool that CONVERTS an XML file into a different format (HTML, text, or XML). -> XSLT\nThe parser that loads the whole document into memory as a tree; best for small files to modify. -> DOM\nThe parser that streams through and fires events at your code; read once, forward-only, best for very large files. -> SAX\nThe parser where your code asks for the next piece when ready; same efficiency as SAX but you control the loop. -> StAX\nThe approach that converts XML directly into objects (e.g., <students> becomes a student object with getName()). -> Data\nBinding (JAXB)\n\n===== PAGE 37 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 37 of 43\nThe gap between the XML tree and a relational database's flat tables. -> Impedance mismatch\nThe kind of XML document with regular, predictable structure where order doesn't matter (invoices, config files). ->\nData-Centric","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt","text":"-> Valid\nThe old schema language written in its own syntax (not XML), with no data types and no namespace support. -> DTD\n(Document Type Definition)\nThe modern W3C-standard schema language written in XML itself, with real data types (integer, date, decimal, boolean) and\nnamespace support. -> XSD (XML Schema Definition)\nThe XML tool that FINDS/ locates one specific part of the document, written like a folder path. -> XPath\nThe XML tool that SEARCHES XML — what SQL does for tables. -> XQuery\nThe XML tool that CONVERTS an XML file into a different format (HTML, text, or XML). -> XSLT\nThe parser that loads the whole document into memory as a tree; best for small files to modify. -> DOM\nThe parser that streams through and fires events at your code; read once, forward-only, best for very large files. -> SAX\nThe parser where your code asks for the next piece when ready; same efficiency as SAX but you control the loop. -> StAX\nThe approach that converts XML directly into objects (e.g., <students> becomes a student object with getName()). -> Data\nBinding (JAXB)\n\n===== PAGE 37 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 37 of 43\nThe gap between the XML tree and a relational database's flat tables. -> Impedance mismatch\nThe kind of XML document with regular, predictable structure where order doesn't matter (invoices, config files). ->\nData-Centric\nThe kind of XML document with irregular, mixed content where order matters (manuals, contracts, articles). ->\nDocument-Centric\nA normal relational database with XML features added (Oracle XML DB, SQL Server, Db2 pureXML). -> XML-enabled\ndatabase\nAn XML storage database where the document itself is the unit of storage (eXist-db, BaseX, MarkLogic). -> Native XML\ndatabase\nThe XML storage technique where the tree is broken into rows and columns — fast joins but the original document no longer\nexists. -> Shredding\nThe standardized format for global interbank payments. -> ISO 20022\nThe standardized format family for healthcare & clinical data. -> HL7 / CDA\nThe standardized format family for B2B supply chains. -> RosettaNet / ebXML\nA protocol for exchanging structured information over a network; traditionally uses XML and follows strict rules. -> SOAP\nAn architectural style to design web-based services and APIs; simpler and lighter than SOAP; uses HTTP methods. -> REST\nThe XML-based language that acts like a technical contract for a SOAP service. -> WSDL (Web Services Description\nLanguage)\nThe directory service created to publish and discover web services; important early, less used today. -> UDDI (Universal\nDescription, Discovery, and Integration)\nThe family of specifications built on top of SOAP and XML that add security, reliability, and transactions. -> WS-*\ntechnologies\nThe WS- spec that adds authentication, encryption, and message integrity to SOAP messages (often with SAML assertions).\n-> WS-Security*\nThe WS- spec that ensures messages are still delivered correctly even over unstable networks. -> WS-ReliableMessaging*\nThe integration approach where a central process directs how services interact, coordinating from the top down. ->\nOrchestration\nThe integration approach where services communicate directly peer-to-peer with no single controlling process. ->\nChoreography\nL7 Identification\nThe process of enabling independently designed applications to communicate, exchange data, and work together as a\ncoordinated system. -> Application Integration\nThe direct, custom-built connection between two systems that is generally not reusable and leads to \"spaghetti\" architecture\nat scale. -> Point-to-point integration\nA central hub that manages and routes communication between multiple systems, reducing the total number of connections.\n-> Hub-and-spoke integration\nThe middleware layer that acts as a common \"communication backbone\" routing and transforming messages between\nsystems. -> Enterprise Service Bus (ESB)\nA modern approach where applications expose specific functions through APIs that other systems call. -> API-based /\nmicroservices integration\nIntegration where data is synced immediately as changes occur (e.g., stock trading updates balances right after a trade). ->\nReal-time integration\nIntegration where data is collected and transferred at scheduled intervals (e.g., payroll syncs attendance overnight). -> Batch\nintegration\nThe process of connecting older, often outdated systems with newer systems or technologies. -> Legacy system\nintegration\nAn incremental modernization pattern that minimizes operational risk when migrating legacy systems. -> Strangler Fig\npattern\nA pattern that protects a legacy system from a new system's data model changes. -> Anti-Corruption Layer (ACL)\n\n===== PAGE 38 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 38 of 43\nThe process of linking separate applications, databases, and hardware platforms across an entire organization to work as a\ncoordinated ecosystem. -> Enterprise Integration / EAI\nThe architecture where systems publish event messages to an enterprise broker and subscribing services react\nasynchronously. -> Event-Driven Architecture (EDA)\nThe pattern that delivers messages from a single sender to multiple subscribed systems simultaneously. ->\nPublish-Subscribe Channel\nThe pattern that inspects incoming message contents and routes data to the correct destination based on business rules. ->\nContent-Based Router\nThe pattern that converts data payloads between incompatible structures (e.g., XML to JSON, EDI to SQL). -> Message\nTranslator\nThe cloud-hosted toolset for connecting applications and data across cloud and on-premises environments. -> iPaaS\n(Integration Platform as a Service)\nL8 Identification\nThe process of linking different IT systems, applications, and data sources so they can work together and share data\nseamlessly. -> Enterprise Integration\nThe practice of exchanging structured data between systems using XML as a common format. -> XML Integration\nA standardized way for applications to communicate over a network, regardless of platform or language. -> Web Services\nAn API type that is the most common today, used in web & mobile apps. -> REST API\nAn API type that is enterprise-grade, used in banking/finance. -> SOAP API\nThe API approach that offers flexible querying — request only what you need. -> GraphQL\nThe security standard family that provides message-level security for SOAP. -> WS-Security\nThe formula that distinguishes authentication from authorization. -> Authentication = \"who are you?\" vs Authorization =\n\"what can you do?\"\n\"Well-formed is not the same as ....\" -> safe\nAn enterprise that connects business systems through reusable services. -> Service-Oriented Enterprise (SOE)\nThe SOE component that defines how a service can be accessed. -> Service Interface\nThe SOE component that helps consumers find available services. -> Service Registry / Broker\nThe goal of a Service-Oriented Enterprise. -> An enterprise that is integrated, flexible, reusable, and easier to manage\n\n===== PAGE 39 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 39 of 43\nCOMPREHENSIVE ACRONYMS LIST\nAcronym\nMeaning\nEA\nEnterprise Architecture\nADM\nArchitecture Development Method\nTOGAF\nThe Open Group Architecture Framework\nFEAF\nFederal Enterprise Architecture Framework\nPRM\nPerformance Reference Model\nDRM\nData Reference Model\nSRM\nSecurity Reference Model\nBRM\nBusiness Reference Model\nARM\nApplication Reference Model\nTRM\nTechnical Reference Model\nEAP\nEnterprise Architecture Planning\nPMO\nProject Management Office\nARB\nArchitecture Review Board\nRBAC\nRole-Based Access Control\nEACR\nEA Change Request\nCONOPS\nConcept of Operations\nERD\nEntity-Relationship Diagram\nIDEF-O\nIntegration DEFinition / Process modeling (Inputs, Controls,\nOutputs, Mechanisms)\nSWOT\nStrengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, Threats\nMDM\nMaster Data Management\nIaaS / PaaS\nInfrastructure as a Service / Platform as a Service\nBPMN\nBusiness Process Model and Notation\nDMZ\nDemilitarized Zone\nCRM\nCustomer Relationship Management\nERP\nEnterprise Resource Planning\nCMDB\nConfiguration Management Database\nITSM\nIT Service Management\nSDLC\nSoftware Development Life Cycle\nCI/CD\nContinuous Integration / Continuous Delivery\nSOA\nService-Oriented Architecture\nSOE\nService-Oriented Enterprise\nESB\nEnterprise Service Bus\nWSDL\nWeb Services Description Language\nUDDI\nUniversal Description, Discovery, and Integration\n\n===== PAGE 40 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 40 of 43\nAcronym\nMeaning\nREST\nRepresentational State Transfer\nSOAP\nSimple Object Access Protocol\nHTTP / HTTPS\nHypertext Transfer Protocol / HTTP Secure\nTLS\nTransport Layer Security\nXML\neXtensible Markup Language\nJSON\nJavaScript Object Notation\nDTD\nDocument Type Definition\nXSD\nXML Schema Definition\nXPath / XQuery / XSLT\nXML locating / searching / transforming tools\nDOM / SAX / StAX\nXML parsing models (tree / event-streaming / pull-streaming)\nJAXB\nJava Architecture for XML Binding (data binding)\nCRUD\nCreate, Read, Update, Delete\nEAI\nEnterprise Application Integration\niPaaS\nIntegration Platform as a Service\nEDA\nEvent-Driven Architecture\nACL\nAnti-Corruption Layer\nETL\nExtract, Transform, Load\nOAuth 2.0 / JWT\nOpen Authorization 2.0 / JSON Web Token\nMFA\nMulti-Factor Authentication\nSLA\nService-Level Agreement\nCDN\nContent Delivery Network\nAPI\nApplication Programming Interface\nWS-*\nWeb Services specifications family (security, reliability, transactions)\nSAML\nSecurity Assertion Markup Language\nB2B\nBusiness-to-Business\nISO 20022\nGlobal interbank payment messaging standard\nHL7 / CDA\nHealthcare data exchange standard / Clinical Document\nArchitecture\nebXML\nElectronic Business using XML\ngRPC\nGoogle Remote Procedure Call\nAWS EC2 / VPC\nAmazon Web Services Elastic Compute Cloud / Virtual Private\nCloud\nGZIP\nFile/directory compression tool\nXSS\nCross-Site Scripting\nDDos\nDistributed Denial of Service\n\n===== PAGE 41 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 41 of 43\nTOP THINGS TO MEMORIZE\nL2 — Enterprise Architecture Components\n1. 4 Phases of EA Implementation — (I) EA Program Establishment, (II) Framework & Tool Selection, (III) Documentation\nof the EA, (IV) Use & Maintain the EA.\n2. Zachman Framework — John Zachman, 1987; 6x6 matrix (36 cells); columns = What/How/Where/Who/When/Why; rows\n= Planner, Owner, Designer, Builder, Sub-Contractor, Enterprise.\n3. Choosing the right framework — organize/classify -> Zachman; detailed development process -> TOGAF;\ngovernment/public sector -> FEAF; flexibility & business alignment -> Gartner.\n4. Four Core EA Components — Business (foundation), Data/Information, Application, Technology.\n5. Classification of Artifacts — Catalogs (inventory), Matrices (relationships), Diagrams/Models (visual representation).\n6. Traceability chain — Business -> Data -> Application -> Technology; used for Impact Analysis.\n7. FEAF Reference Models — PRM, DRM, SRM, BRM, ARM, TRM.\n8. TOGAF ADM Cycle — Preliminary, A (Vision) through H (Change Management) + Requirements Management (operates\nthroughout).\nL3 — Developing an Enterprise Architecture\n9. Documentation before planning — plans built without an accurate baseline underestimate cost, timeline, and\ncomplexity.\n10. CONOPS Scenario — good & bad environments from SWOT; Storytelling Technique (Dr. Robert Neilson):\nProcess/People/Technology.\n11. Freeze periods — freeze current & future views twice a year; changes accumulate 4-5 months; publish in month six.\n12. TOGAF architecture states — Baseline (as-is), Transition (intermediate), Target (desired future).\n13. EA Management Plan parts (4) — EA Program Management, Current Architecture Summary, Future Architecture\nSummary, Glossary & References.\n14. Chief Architect — manages the EA program, documentation, standards, and configuration management.\n15. Outcome vs Output measures — outcomes = integration/user satisfaction/IT decisions; outputs = projects\ncompleted/scope changes/planning activities.\nL4 — Using an Enterprise Architecture 16. Plan, Protect, and Manage — Planning & Project Management, Security,\nRepository & Support Tools. 17. EAP Guiding Principle — BUSINESS -> DATA -> APPLICATIONS -> TECHNOLOGY\n(business-driven, data-driven, never technology-driven). 18. From Architecture to Projects — Gap Analysis -> Work Packages\n-> Business Case -> Roadmap. 19. Common Pitfalls (4) — ivory-tower architecture, no executive sponsor, big-bang scope,\nstale artifacts. 20. 4 Main Areas of Security — data, network/technology, application, physical. 21. EA Repository — central\nknowledge base; single source of truth for As-Is, To-Be, Transition. 22. EA Support Tool Capabilities (4) — automated\ndiagramming, impact & risk analysis, collaborative governance, automated data ingestion.\nL5 — All About SOA 23. SOA Core Principles (4) — Reusability, Loose Coupling, Abstraction, Modularity. 24. Main\nComponents of SOA (3) — Service Provider, Service Consumer, Service Registry. 25. ESB Core Functions (6) — Routing,\nTransformation, Protocol Mediation, Orchestration, Monitoring & Logging, Security Enforcement. 26. REST vs SOAP — REST =\narchitectural style, JSON, GET/POST/PUT/DELETE, stateless; SOAP = strict standard, XML envelopes, WS-Security. 27.\nEvolution — Monolithic (1990s) -> SOA (2000s) -> Microservices (2010s-now). 28. Monolithic vs SOA — single unit vs\nindependent deployment; one stack vs diverse; whole-app scaling vs targeted scaling; full-system failure vs isolated failure. 29.\nSOA vs Microservices — ESB-mediated vs direct REST/gRPC; shared data vs per-service data; centralized vs decentralized\ngovernance. 30. SOA's Legacy — API gateways, service mesh (Istio), containers & orchestration (Docker/Kubernetes), hybrid &\nlegacy integration.\nL6 — Integrating Technology 31. XML properties — self-describing, extensible, platform neutral, data not design. 32. XML\ndocument parts — prolog, root element, child element, attribute, element content, nesting. 33. Well-formed vs Valid —\nwell-formed = correct syntax; valid = well-formed + follows a DTD/XSD. 34. DTD vs XSD — DTD = old, own syntax, no data\ntypes; XSD = W3C standard, written in XML, real data types, namespaces. 35. XPath finds, XQuery searches, XSLT converts.\n36. Reading XML — DOM (tree, edit), SAX (streaming, read-only), StAX (pull streaming), JAXB (data binding). 37. Storing XML\n— XML-enabled DB, Native XML DB, Hybrid/XML column; shredding / CLOB / native tree. 38. XML integration standards —\nISO 20022 (interbank payments), HL7/CDA (healthcare), RosettaNet/ebXML (B2B supply chains). 39. Web service tech —\nSOAP, REST, XML, JSON, HTTP/HTTPS, WSDL (the contract), UDDI (the directory).\n\n===== PAGE 42 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 42 of 43\nL7 — Integrating Applications 40. Integration styles — Point-to-Point, Hub-and-Spoke, ESB, API-based/Microservices. 41.\nReal-time vs Batch — real-time = immediate sync (stock trading); batch = scheduled intervals (overnight payroll). 42. Legacy\nintegration approaches (5) — API Wrapping, Middleware Connectivity, ESB, iPaaS, ETL & Data Synchronization. 43. Legacy\nchallenges (6) — architectural mismatch, data inconsistency, security gaps, skill shortages, missing documentation, downtime\nrisks. 44. Enterprise integration architectures — ESB, Hub-and-Spoke, SOA, Event-Driven (EDA).\nL8 — Enterprise Integration 45. Enterprise Integration — connecting, not replacing, existing systems. 46. XML integration\nbest practices — validate everything (well-formed is not safe), use HTTPS, least privilege, block XML attacks. 47. SOAP vs\nREST (L8) — SOAP = protocol/XML-only/built-in security/slower/enterprise; REST =\nstyle/JSON/implementation-dependent/faster/web & mobile. 48. Auth vs Authorization — Authentication = \"who are you?\";\nAuthorization = \"what can you do?\" 49. SOE Main Components (5) — Provider, Consumer, Registry/Broker, Interface, ESB. 50.\nWS-* specs — WSDL (describe), UDDI (discover), WS-Security (secure), WS-ReliableMessaging (reliable).\n\n===== PAGE 43 =====\nSAI 1 Reviewer | System Architecture and Integration 1 (ITNET312-T)\nPage 43 of 43\nQUICK CRAM SHEET (HIGHEST-YIELD FACTS)\nEA Methodology vs Framework — Methodology = HOW the EA program is run; Framework = WHAT structure organizes the\nEA.\nZachman — 1987, John Zachman, 6x6 = 36 cells, classification schema (not methodology), columns =\nWhat/How/Where/Who/When/Why, rows = Planner/Owner/Designer/Builder/Sub-Contractor/Enterprise.\nFEAF models mnemonic — Performance, Data, Security, Business, Application, Technical.\n4 EA layers — Business -> Data -> Application -> Technology (Business is the foundation).\nArtifacts — Catalogs = inventory, Matrices = relationships, Diagrams/Models = visual/structure.\nADM cycle order — Preliminary -> A Vision -> B Business -> C Info Systems -> D Technology -> E Opportunities -> F Migration\n-> G Governance -> H Change (+ Requirements Mgmt throughout).\n2 KEY EA numbers — Zachman 36 cells; Freeze periods twice a year (publish month 6).\nEAP principle — Business -> Data -> Applications -> Technology; business-driven and data-driven, NEVER technology-driven.\n4 EAs to projects — Gap Analysis, Work Packages, Business Case, Roadmap.\nPlan -> Protect -> Manage (the three EA usage areas).\nSOA 4 principles — Reusability, Loose Coupling, Abstraction, Modularity.\nSOA 3 components — Provider, Consumer, Registry.\nESB 6 functions — Routing, Transformation, Protocol Mediation, Orchestration, Monitoring & Logging, Security Enforcement.\nREST vs SOAP — REST: style, JSON, fast, web/mobile; SOAP: protocol, XML, secure, banking/enterprise.\nArchitecture eras — Monolithic (90s) -> SOA (2000s) -> Microservices (2010s-now).\nMicroservices fixes over SOA — no central bus (ESB = single point of failure), per-service data, autonomous teams, isolated\nfailure.\nSOA legacy — API gateways, service mesh (Istio), Docker/Kubernetes, hybrid integration.\nXML 4 properties — self-describing, extensible, platform neutral, data not design.\nXML parts — prolog, root, child, attribute, content, nesting.\nWell-formed vs valid — well-formed = syntax correct; valid = well-formed + follows schema.\nDTD vs XSD — DTD old/no types; XSD modern/W3C/real types/namespaces.\nXML 3 tools — XPath finds, XQuery searches, XSLT converts.\nXML parsing — DOM (tree), SAX (events), StAX (pull), JAXB (objects).\nImpedance mismatch — XML tree vs database tables.\n3 ways to store XML — XML-enabled DB, native XML DB, hybrid/XML column.\nIntegration styles — Point-to-Point, Hub-and-Spoke, ESB, API-based, Event-driven.\nLegacy vs New — legacy = cheaper, less disruption, less training, limited scalability; new = pricier, more downtime, more\ntraining, highly scalable.\nEAI objectives — data consistency, system interoperability, business process automation, real-time visibility.\nIntegration patterns — messaging channels, content-based router, message translator, publish-subscribe.\nWeb service stack — SOAP + WSDL + UDDI (+ WS-* for security/reliability).\nAuthentication vs Authorization — \"who are you?\" vs \"what can you do?\"\nSOE components (5) — Provider, Consumer, Registry/Broker, Interface, ESB.\nXML integration mindset — structure clearly, validate & secure every hop, optimize performance, handle errors with clear\nfeedback, design for the reader, trust nothing by default.\nEnd of Reviewer — generated from SAI Reference (L2-L8). Study hard — you've got this!","lineStart":1901,"lineEnd":2262,"totalLines":2262,"truncated":false}},"title":"xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\test001\\source.txt","time":{"start":1789817819197,"end":1789817819226}},"id":"prt_0b9748f46001s1iuLAunYKEkJf","sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","messageID":"msg_0b97489cc001BvnMunTSgXhkxK"}}
