{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789818153725,"sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_dead4cdcc5ea49b18116cb92","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"[\n  {\n      \"id\": \"ch1-q16\",\n      \"question\": \"Which ADM phase monitors implementation, checks architecture compliance, and manages implementation issues?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Phase H — Architecture Change Management\",\n        \"Phase G — Implementation Governance\",\n        \"Phase D — Technology Architecture\",\n        \"Phase C — Information Systems Architecture\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"Phase G, Implementation Governance, monitors implementation, checks architecture compliance, and manages implementation issues.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch1-q17\",\n      \"question\": \"Which architectural domain is described as the foundation of the EA framework?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Technology Architecture\",\n        \"Application Architecture\",\n        \"Data Architecture\",\n        \"Business Architecture\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n      \"explanation\": \"Business Architecture is the foundation of the EA framework and defines strategy, governance, structure, and key business processes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch1-q18\",\n      \"question\": \"Which of the following artifacts would best display the relationships and cardinality among data entities?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"An Application Portfolio Catalog\",\n        \"An Application-to-Function Matrix\",\n        \"An Entity-Relationship Diagram\",\n        \"A Technology Standards Catalog\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n      \"explanation\": \"An Entity-Relationship Diagram is a Data-layer Diagram that visually models data entities and their relationships and cardinality.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch1-q19\",\n      \"question\": \"Which FEAF reference model measures how well an organization and its IT systems achieve strategic goals and desired outcomes?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"PRM\",\n        \"DRM\",\n        \"SRM\",\n        \"TRM\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"PRM, the Performance Reference Model, measures how well the organization and its IT systems achieve strategic goals and outcomes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch1-q20\",\n      \"question\": \"In the Zachman Framework, which row represents the perspective of the 'Enterprise (Actual System)'?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Builder\",\n        \"Sub-Contractor\",\n        \"Designer\",\n        \"Enterprise\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n      \"explanation\": \"The six rows are Planner, Owner, Designer, Builder, Sub-Contractor, and Enterprise (Actual System).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch1-q21\",\n      \"question\": \"Which statement best describes the role of Requirements Management within the TOGAF ADM cycle?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"It operates only during Phase A, Architecture Vision\",\n        \"It operates throughout all ADM phases and tracks changing requirements\",\n        \"It replaces the Preliminary Phase entirely\",\n        \"It is applied only once after Phase H ends\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"Requirements Management operates throughout all ADM phases, tracking, evaluating, and managing changing requirements.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch1-q22\",\n      \"question\": \"The technology layer of the EA framework answers which question?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"What software applications are needed to automate processes?\",\n        \"What information does the enterprise need to operate?\",\n        \"What infrastructure is required to run applications securely?\",\n        \"What does the business do to achieve its goals?\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n      \"explanation\": \"Technology Architecture answers, 'What physical and virtual infrastructure is required to run the application architecture securely and scalably?'\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch1-q23\",\n      \"question\": \"Which phase of the EA implementation develops future operating scenarios and defines future planning assumptions?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Phase I — EA Program Establishment\",\n        \"Phase II — Framework and Tool Selection\",\n        \"Phase IV — Use and Maintain the EA\",\n        \"Phase III — Documentation of the EA\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n      \"explanation\": \"Phase III, Documentation of the EA, includes developing future operating scenarios, defining future planning assumptions, and documenting the future state.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch1-q24\",\n      \"question\": \"Which FEAF reference model defines security-related capabilities, controls, and requirements for protecting enterprise information?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"BRM\",\n        \"DRM\",\n        \"SRM\",\n        \"TRM\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n      \"explanation\": \"SRM, the Security Reference Model, defines security-related capabilities, controls, and requirements for protecting enterprise systems and information.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch1-q25\",\n      \"question\": \"Which ADM phase identifies possible solutions, groups changes into projects, and defines the implementation approach?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Phase F — Migration Planning\",\n        \"Phase E — Opportunities & Solutions\",\n        \"Phase C — Information Systems Architecture\",\n        \"Phase B — Business Architecture\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"Phase E, Opportunities & Solutions, identifies possible solutions, groups changes into projects, and defines the implementation approach.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch1-q26\",\n      \"question\": \"Which chain represents the correct cross-layer traceability order that enables impact analysis?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Application -> Technology -> Data -> Business\",\n        \"Business -> Data -> Application -> Technology\",\n        \"Technology -> Application -> Data -> Business\",\n        \"Data -> Business -> Application -> Technology\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"Traceability runs Business -> Data -> Application -> Technology so every technical asset maps back to a business need.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch1-q27\",\n      \"question\": \"Which statement is TRUE about the Gartner EA methodology?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"It views EA as a one-time project completed at implementation\",\n        \"It focuses purely on technology rather than business outcomes\",\n        \"It is mandatory for U.S. government agencies\",\n        \"It views EA as a continuous process of change and improvement\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n      \"explanation\": \"Gartner views Enterprise Architecture as a continuous process of change and improvement, not a one-time project.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch1-q28\",\n      \"question\": \"Which pair of Zachman Framework columns is correctly identified?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Where — captures motivation and strategy\",\n        \"Who — addresses how the work is performed\",\n        \"When — identifies the people and actors\",\n        \"What — answers what data or things exist\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n      \"explanation\": \"The columns are the interrogatives What, How, Where, Who, When, Why. 'What' focuses on the data or physical things.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch1-q29\",\n      \"question\": \"Which artifact category is an Application-to-Function Matrix an example of?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"A Catalog\",\n        \"A Matrix\",\n        \"A Diagram\",\n        \"A Model\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"Matrices are grids that show the relationship between two or more architectural elements; the Application-to-Function Matrix is a listed example.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch1-q30\",\n      \"question\": \"Why does the reference describe siloed or disconnected artifacts as a critical pitfall in EA?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"They are cheaper to produce than integrated models\",\n        \"They reduce the number of approved tools\",\n        \"They lose alignment between strategic goals and technical execution\",\n        \"They prevent stakeholders from joining review meetings\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n      \"explanation\": \"When architectural components are isolated, organizations lose alignment between strategic goals and technical execution.\"\n    }\n]","filePath":"C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\sai_build\\ch1b.json"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\sai_build\\ch1b.json","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\sai_build\\ch1b.json","time":{"start":1789818153714,"end":1789818153721}},"id":"prt_0b9798380001x83ULa1SXBsnU0","sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","messageID":"msg_0b9797b01001IyGFJGsf3bYz7R"}}
