{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789818199368,"sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_7ee1e3824b844467956495ef","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"[\n  {\n      \"id\": \"ch2-q16\",\n      \"question\": \"A Network Connectivity Diagram showing a new fiber link between City Hall and the Data Center belongs to which future artifact level?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Infrastructure Level\",\n        \"Systems / Services Level\",\n        \"Business Level\",\n        \"Strategic Level\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"The Infrastructure Level documents physical networks, hardware, and facilities, including Network Connectivity Diagrams.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch2-q17\",\n      \"question\": \"What is the purpose of freeze periods in maintaining trustworthy future views?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"To keep current and future views frozen at regular intervals so changes are published in releases\",\n        \"To prevent any new changes from ever being approved\",\n        \"To permanently lock the EA repository at go-live\",\n        \"To freeze budgets for the fiscal year\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"Freeze periods freeze current and future views at regular intervals (e.g., twice a year); changes accumulate for 4-5 months, then publish as a new release in month six.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch2-q18\",\n      \"question\": \"In TOGAF's view, an intermediate state that bridges the gap between baseline and target on a realistic timeline is called:\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Target Architecture\",\n        \"Transition Architecture\",\n        \"Baseline Architecture\",\n        \"Future Operating Scenario\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"Transition Architecture is one or more intermediate states that bridge the gap between baseline and target on a realistic timeline.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch2-q19\",\n      \"question\": \"Which of these is one of the four main parts of an EA Management Plan?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Enterprise Architecture Repository\",\n        \"Current Architecture Summary\",\n        \"Architecture Review Calendar\",\n        \"TOGAF Requirements Log\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"The four parts are EA Program Management, Current Architecture Summary, Future Architecture Summary, and Glossary and References.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch2-q20\",\n      \"question\": \"Which role is responsible for managing the EA program, documentation, standards, and configuration management?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Chief Architect\",\n        \"Project Manager\",\n        \"Service Owner\",\n        \"Business Analyst\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"The Chief Architect manages the EA program, documentation, standards, and configuration management.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch2-q21\",\n      \"question\": \"Improvements in integration, user satisfaction, or IT investment decisions are examples of which type of performance measure?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Output measures\",\n        \"Outcome measures\",\n        \"Cost measures\",\n        \"Sprint measures\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"Outcome measures are improvements in integration, user satisfaction, or IT investment decisions; Output measures include projects completed and scope changes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch2-q22\",\n      \"question\": \"The number of projects completed, scope changes, or planning activities are examples of which type of measure?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Outcome measures\",\n        \"Benefit measures\",\n        \"Output measures\",\n        \"Governance measures\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n      \"explanation\": \"Output measures count things like projects completed, scope changes, or planning activities.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch2-q23\",\n      \"question\": \"What should be documented in a separate IT Security Plan instead of the main architecture summary?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Sensitive security details\",\n        \"Business capabilities\",\n        \"Technology standards\",\n        \"Workforce skills\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"Sensitive security details should be documented in a separate IT Security Plan to avoid exposing vulnerabilities.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch2-q24\",\n      \"question\": \"The EA Sequencing Plan primarily ensures that:\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Projects occur in the correct order and dependent systems are not implemented too early\",\n        \"Every project receives equal funding\",\n        \"Only one technology vendor is used\",\n        \"Security controls replace testing\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"The EA Sequencing Plan lists projects, tasks, milestones, dependencies, and timeframes to ensure projects occur in the correct order.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch2-q25\",\n      \"question\": \"Which practice reviews and controls proposed EA changes, applies technical standards, and manages waivers?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Configuration Management\",\n        \"Release Management\",\n        \"Capacity Planning\",\n        \"Service Desk\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"Configuration Management reviews and controls proposed changes, applies technical standards, manages waivers, and maintains version control.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch2-q26\",\n      \"question\": \"Which statement reflects the reference's analogy for EA future views?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Having a full set of blueprints for modifying an existing home\",\n        \"Playing chess against an unknown opponent\",\n        \"Writing a standardized college paper\",\n        \"Keeping a giant checklist of all hardware\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"The reference analogizes future EA views to having a full set of blueprints for the modification of an existing home.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch2-q27\",\n      \"question\": \"What does the Current Architecture Summary primarily describe?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"The desired future direction of the enterprise\",\n        \"How existing resources support the organization\",\n        \"The approved technology standards list\",\n        \"The enterprise's annual budget allocation\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"The Current Architecture Summary describes how existing resources support the organization, including roles, strengths, weaknesses, and performance gaps.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch2-q28\",\n      \"question\": \"Which of the following is a future operating scenario mentioned in the reference?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Aggressive growth\",\n        \"Quarterly audits\",\n        \"Vendor consolidation\",\n        \"Data center shutdown\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"Future operating scenarios include the status quo, aggressive growth, or responding defensively to a market downturn.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch2-q29\",\n      \"question\": \"The EA Management Plan is best described as:\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"A one-time document delivered when the EA is built\",\n        \"A living document that guides the transition from current to future architecture\",\n        \"A compliance report for auditors\",\n        \"A list of all IT project budgets\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"The EA Management Plan directs the transition from current to future architecture and is a living document that is regularly updated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch2-q30\",\n      \"question\": \"Why does the reference say future artifacts must use the same methods and levels as current artifacts?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"So change is always traceable from current to future state\",\n        \"So auditors can compare payroll records\",\n        \"So vendors can reuse existing licenses\",\n        \"So future plans fit one standardized format\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"Future artifacts are built with the same planning assumptions, documentation, and modeling techniques as current views, at every level, so change is always traceable.\"\n    }\n]","filePath":"C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\sai_build\\ch2b.json"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\sai_build\\ch2b.json","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\sai_build\\ch2b.json","time":{"start":1789818199358,"end":1789818199365}},"id":"prt_0b97a3882001ySUZvoSw3QWvg9","sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","messageID":"msg_0b97a2f9f001KvwpP5ajeY0Gsf"}}
