{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789818214117,"sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_7b47b1c5a7144463ac5ac139","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"{\n  \"chapterId\": \"ch3\",\n  \"title\": \"L4 — Using an Enterprise Architecture\",\n  \"description\": \"Planning and project management, EAP, sequencing and prioritization, security areas, the EA repository, support tools, and integrating EA with operational IT.\",\n  \"questions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch3-q01\",\n      \"question\": \"Which statement best describes what Enterprise Architecture provides to an organization?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"A blueprint showing how parts of the organization work together\",\n        \"A replacement for all legacy applications\",\n        \"A mandatory federal compliance framework\",\n        \"A pricing model for cloud services\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"EA provides a blueprint showing how different parts of an organization work together and how technology supports business goals.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch3-q02\",\n      \"question\": \"The three main areas of using an EA are commonly remembered as the sequence:\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Design, Build, Test\",\n        \"Plan, Protect, and Manage\",\n        \"Analyze, Document, Govern\",\n        \"Budget, Deploy, Monitor\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"The three areas are Planning & Project Management, Security, and Repository & Support Tools — remembered as Plan, Protect, and Manage.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch3-q03\",\n      \"question\": \"In the Amazon order-fulfillment example, which system processes payment after the e-commerce system receives an order?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Inventory System\",\n        \"Logistics System\",\n        \"Payment System\",\n        \"Fulfillment System\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n      \"explanation\": \"The flow is Customer Places Order -> E-Commerce System -> Payment System -> Inventory System -> Fulfillment System -> Logistics System.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch3-q04\",\n      \"question\": \"Which statement best describes the Planning step in using an EA?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"It converts the gap between As-Is and To-Be into a prioritized list of initiatives\",\n        \"It controls project scope, schedule, cost, and risk\",\n        \"It protects systems from unauthorized access\",\n        \"It stores all EA diagrams and models\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"Planning (What & Why) converts the gap between the As-Is and To-Be architecture into a list of initiatives and decides which goes first.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch3-q05\",\n      \"question\": \"EAP is defined by Spewak (1992) as:\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Defining the architectures needed for the business use of information and the plan to implement them\",\n        \"Documenting every hardware asset in the enterprise\",\n        \"Standardizing all communication protocols\",\n        \"Designing databases for transactional systems\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"Spewak (1992) defines EAP as defining the architectures needed for the business use of information — and the plan to implement them.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch3-q06\",\n      \"question\": \"What is the guiding principle of EAP?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Technology -> Application -> Data -> Business\",\n        \"Business -> Data -> Applications -> Technology\",\n        \"Data -> Technology -> Business -> Application\",\n        \"Application -> Business -> Technology -> Data\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"The guiding principle is BUSINESS -> DATA -> APPLICATIONS -> TECHNOLOGY: applications manage data, technology supports applications, and everything serves a business process.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch3-q07\",\n      \"question\": \"Which choice best states the consequence of reversing the EAP order?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"You get a solution hunting for a problem\",\n        \"You get better data governance\",\n        \"You eliminate technical debt\",\n        \"You accelerate time-to-market\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"Reverse the order and you get a solution hunting for a problem; EAP must be business-driven and data-driven, never technology-driven.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch3-q08\",\n      \"question\": \"In the 'Where we want to be' layer of EAP, which architecture is defined first?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Data architecture\",\n        \"Application architecture\",\n        \"Technology architecture\",\n        \"Network architecture\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"In 'Where we want to be,' data architecture comes first, then applications, then technology; these describe WHAT is needed, not how it will be coded.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch3-q09\",\n      \"question\": \"An enterprise must enforce a compliance deadline from the Data Privacy Act. Based on sequencing tests, how should this work be treated?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"As a pilot first, then a rollout\",\n        \"As non-negotiable work that must be scheduled\",\n        \"As a dependency for every dashboard\",\n        \"As an optional enhancement\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"Compliance deadlines are a sequencing test: non-negotiable work, such as the Data Privacy Act, must be scheduled.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch3-q10\",\n      \"question\": \"What is the rule of thumb the reference gives about projects that change the same system?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Always run two parallel projects to save time\",\n        \"Never run two projects that change the same system at the same time\",\n        \"Only change the system when it is down\",\n        \"Assign both projects to the same owner\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"The rule of thumb is to never run two projects that change the same system at the same time.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch3-q11\",\n      \"question\": \"In the 'From Architecture to Projects' process, what does Gap Analysis do?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Compares As-Is against To-Be to find what is missing, duplicated, and obsolete\",\n        \"Assigns a single owner to each deliverable\",\n        \"Attaches cost, benefit, and risk to each package\",\n        \"Places packages on a timeline with transition architectures\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"Gap Analysis compares As-Is against To-Be to find what is missing, duplicated, and obsolete; every project must trace back to a proven gap.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch3-q12\",\n      \"question\": \"What does a Work Package in EA planning consist of?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"A comparison of current and target states\",\n        \"A carved-out project scope with an assigned owner and aligned business outcome\",\n        \"A business case attached to cost and benefit\",\n        \"Two projects changing the same system\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"Work Packages group related gaps into small deliverables, each with clear scope, a single owner, and one concrete outcome.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch3-q13\",\n      \"question\": \"Why does leadership fund a Business Case rather than diagrams?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Because a business case only reports status\",\n        \"Because business cases are shorter to review\",\n        \"Because leadership funds outcomes, not diagrams\",\n        \"Because diagrams lack legal sign-off\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n      \"explanation\": \"The Business Case attaches cost, benefit, and risk to each package; leadership funds outcomes, not diagrams.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch3-q14\",\n      \"question\": \"Which role's question is 'Are we on scope, schedule, and budget?'\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Enterprise Architecture Team\",\n        \"Project Manager / PMO\",\n        \"Architecture Review Board\",\n        \"Service Registry\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"The Project Manager / PMO cares about delivering this one project and asks whether the team is on scope, schedule, and budget.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch3-q15\",\n      \"question\": \"What is required to document an ARB deviation so a 'temporary fix' does not become permanent technical debt?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"A written executive memo\",\n        \"A documented exception with an expiry date\",\n        \"A quarterly stakeholder meeting\",\n        \"A new business case\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"Any deviation needs a documented exception with an expiry date; otherwise the temporary fix becomes permanent technical debt.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}","filePath":"C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\sai_build\\ch3a.json"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\sai_build\\ch3a.json","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\sai_build\\ch3a.json","time":{"start":1789818214098,"end":1789818214115}},"id":"prt_0b97a61b0001MUbpmQy8FcNy9b","sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","messageID":"msg_0b97a5d69001Yi9sQI47rzmCFy"}}
