{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789818260961,"sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_a2bf6c82454a49fda4392e89","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"[\n  {\n      \"id\": \"ch4-q16\",\n      \"question\": \"Which benefit of SOA refers to teams developing, updating, and deploying individual services independently?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Reusability\",\n        \"Scalability\",\n        \"Agility\",\n        \"Modularity\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n      \"explanation\": \"Agility lets teams develop, update, and deploy individual services independently, drastically reducing time to bring features to market.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch4-q17\",\n      \"question\": \"Which technical advantage of SOA allows services to be written in entirely different programming languages?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Fault Isolation\",\n        \"Technology Diversity\",\n        \"Independent Deployment\",\n        \"Streamlined Maintenance\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"Technology Diversity (polyglot programming) allows services in different languages because they communicate over standard protocols like HTTP.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch4-q18\",\n      \"question\": \"If one service crashes, the rest of the application can often continue functioning. Which SOA advantage does this describe?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Fault Isolation\",\n        \"Technology Diversity\",\n        \"Reusability\",\n        \"Agility\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"Fault Isolation means a crash in one service often leaves the rest of the application functioning.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch4-q19\",\n      \"question\": \"Which of the following is an inherent drawback of SOA?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"All services must share one database\",\n        \"Network overhead introduces unavoidable latency\",\n        \"Every service must be written in the same language\",\n        \"Services cannot be reused across applications\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"SOA's heavy reliance on network communication introduces unavoidable latency and complex security challenges.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch4-q20\",\n      \"question\": \"In a monolithic architecture, how does a bug in one module affect the system?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"It is isolated to that module only\",\n        \"It can crash the entire system\",\n        \"It slows only the payment function\",\n        \"It is automatically contained by the ESB\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"In a monolithic architecture, failures are not isolated; a bug in one module can crash the entire system.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch4-q21\",\n      \"question\": \"Which statement best contrasts monolithic scaling with SOA scaling?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Monoliths scale specific services; SOA duplicates the full app\",\n        \"Both must duplicate the entire application to scale\",\n        \"Monoliths duplicate the entire app; SOA scales only the services under load\",\n        \"Both scale only the specific service under load\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n      \"explanation\": \"Monolithic architecture must duplicate the entire application to scale, while SOA scales only the specific services under heavy load.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch4-q22\",\n      \"question\": \"Which era of software architecture is described as 'one codebase, one development, simple to start, hard to scale'?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Microservices (2010s)\",\n        \"SOA (2000s)\",\n        \"Monolithic (1990s)\",\n        \"Cloud Native (2020s)\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n      \"explanation\": \"The monolithic era (1990s) had one codebase and one development line: simple to start, hard to scale or change safely.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch4-q23\",\n      \"question\": \"What problem of monolithic systems forced SOA's invention?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Too many independent services to manage\",\n        \"The entire application had to scale as one unit even if only one function was under load\",\n        \"Every service needed its own database\",\n        \"The ESB became a performance bottleneck\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"Scalability bottlenecks occurred because the entire application had to scale as one unit, and a small change could break unrelated functionality.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch4-q24\",\n      \"question\": \"In SOA, how was interoperability between a Java service and a .NET or mainframe service achieved?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"By copying each other's source code\",\n        \"By using standardized contracts described with WSDL/SOAP\",\n        \"By storing all data in one shared database\",\n        \"By requiring identical hardware\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"Standardized contracts described with WSDL/SOAP let services on different platforms interoperate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch4-q25\",\n      \"question\": \"Which of the following is cited as a reason SOA evolved into microservices?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"The ESB became a single point of failure and a performance bottleneck\",\n        \"Services were too small and too numerous\",\n        \"Developers preferred one large codebase\",\n        \"REST APIs were not yet invented\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"SOA growing pains included the ESB becoming a single point of failure and performance bottleneck as message volume grew.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch4-q26\",\n      \"question\": \"Which statement correctly contrasts SOA and microservices communication?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Both use an ESB to mediate every message\",\n        \"Microservices use direct REST/gRPC calls between services\",\n        \"SOA uses direct calls with no middleware\",\n        \"Microservices require centralized governance\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"SOA uses an ESB to mediate messages, while microservices make direct REST/gRPC calls straight between services.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch4-q27\",\n      \"question\": \"How does data ownership typically differ between SOA and microservices?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"SOA services each own private data; microservices share one database\",\n        \"Both share a single enterprise database\",\n        \"SOA often shares databases; each microservice owns its own data\",\n        \"Both require one table per service\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n      \"explanation\": \"SOA often uses shared databases across services, while each microservice owns and manages its own data.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch4-q28\",\n      \"question\": \"Which is the modern successor to the ESB for cloud APIs?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"API Gateway\",\n        \"Service Registry\",\n        \"WSDL contract\",\n        \"Message queue\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"API gateways are the modern successor to the ESB, providing routing, auth, and rate-limiting for cloud APIs.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch4-q29\",\n      \"question\": \"Tools like Istio that manage service-to-service communication with retries, encryption, and observability are examples of:\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Service Mesh\",\n        \"Enterprise Service Bus\",\n        \"Integration Platform as a Service\",\n        \"Content Delivery Network\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"A service mesh (like Istio) manages service-to-service communication, echoing SOA's mediation layer with added observability.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch4-q30\",\n      \"question\": \"Which statement best summarizes SOA's key takeaway about how it solved enterprise problems?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"SOA removed the need for any middleware\",\n        \"SOA made all enterprises use one coding language\",\n        \"SOA rebuilt every application from scratch\",\n        \"SOA split logic into reusable services connected through a central ESB\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n      \"explanation\": \"SOA split business logic into reusable services integrated over a central ESB, letting large organizations reuse capabilities instead of rebuilding logic.\"\n    }\n]","filePath":"C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\sai_build\\ch4b.json"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\sai_build\\ch4b.json","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\sai_build\\ch4b.json","time":{"start":1789818260944,"end":1789818260959}},"id":"prt_0b97b238d001I7D1ulBBvApYhQ","sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","messageID":"msg_0b97b1e91001FgqA6ZXclpnklX"}}
