{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789818272515,"sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_c71a1d4883a747399f5b3a40","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"{\n  \"chapterId\": \"ch5\",\n  \"title\": \"L6 — Integrating Technology\",\n  \"description\": \"XML foundations, document structure, DTD vs XSD, XPath/XQuery/XSLT, XML parsing approaches, storage techniques, and web service technologies.\",\n  \"questions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch5-q01\",\n      \"question\": \"Which phrase best describes XML according to the reference?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"A standard for writing data as plain text using tags that describe meaning\",\n        \"A compiled programming language for web servers\",\n        \"A relational database management system\",\n        \"A file compression format for images\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"XML is a standard for writing data as plain text, using tags that describe what the data means.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch5-q02\",\n      \"question\": \"Which property means XML tags tell you what each value is?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Extensible\",\n        \"Self-describing\",\n        \"Platform neutral\",\n        \"Data, not design\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"Self-describing means tags tell you what each value is, e.g., <name> clearly labels a name value.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch5-q03\",\n      \"question\": \"Which of the following is NOT a listed property of XML?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Extensible\",\n        \"Platform neutral\",\n        \"Data, not design\",\n        \"Binary encoding\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n      \"explanation\": \"XML is plain text, not binary. Its four properties are self-describing, extensible, platform neutral, and data not design.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch5-q04\",\n      \"question\": \"In the sample document <?xml version=\\\"1.0\\\" encoding=\\\"UTF-8\\\"?>, what is this first line called?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Root element\",\n        \"Prolog\",\n        \"Attribute\",\n        \"Element content\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"The prolog is always the very first line and declares the XML version and encoding.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch5-q05\",\n      \"question\": \"Having exactly one outermost element with everything else inside it is a requirement for:\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"The attribute\",\n        \"The root element\",\n        \"The prolog\",\n        \"The namespace\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"The root element is the outermost element; there is exactly one per document and everything else sits inside it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch5-q06\",\n      \"question\": \"In <book isbn=\\\"978-0-13-235088\\\">, what is isbn?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"A child element\",\n        \"An attribute\",\n        \"The root element\",\n        \"A namespace\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"An attribute is written inside the opening tag in the format name=\\\"value\\\", and its name cannot repeat on the same element.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch5-q07\",\n      \"question\": \"Which document is well-formed but NOT valid?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"<student><name>Ana<age>19</name></age></student>\",\n        \"<student><name>Ana</name><age>banana</age></student>\",\n        \"<student><name>Ana</name><age>19</age></student>\",\n        \"<student><name></student>\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"It is well-formed (syntax correct) but not valid because <age> contains 'banana', which does not match its declared integer data type.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch5-q08\",\n      \"question\": \"Which statement correctly compares DTD and XSD?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"DTD is the modern standard and XSD is the old way\",\n        \"XSD supports namespaces and real data types; DTD has neither\",\n        \"DTD is written in XML and XSD uses its own syntax\",\n        \"Both support data types like integer and date\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"XSD is a W3C standard written in XML itself with real data types and namespace support; DTD is older, uses its own syntax, and has no data types.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch5-q09\",\n      \"question\": \"Which XML tool FINDS or locates one specific part of the document, written like a folder path?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"XQuery\",\n        \"XSLT\",\n        \"XPath\",\n        \"XSD\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n      \"explanation\": \"XPath finds and locates one specific part of the document, written like a folder path, without changing anything.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch5-q10\",\n      \"question\": \"Which XML tool SEARCHES XML and returns only the parts you ask for, similar to what SQL does for tables?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"XQuery\",\n        \"XPath\",\n        \"XSLT\",\n        \"StAX\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"XQuery searches XML and returns only the parts requested, like SQL for tables, e.g., querying books published after 2005.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch5-q11\",\n      \"question\": \"Which XML tool CONVERTS an XML file into a different format such as HTML, plain text, or other XML?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"XPath\",\n        \"XSLT\",\n        \"XQuery\",\n        \"SAX\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"XSLT converts XML into a different format like HTML, plain text, or other XML; the original file is never changed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch5-q12\",\n      \"question\": \"Which parsing approach loads the whole document into memory as a tree, using memory equal to the file size?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"SAX\",\n        \"StAX\",\n        \"DOM\",\n        \"JAXB\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n      \"explanation\": \"DOM loads the whole document into memory as a tree, best for small files you need to modify.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch5-q13\",\n      \"question\": \"A developer needs to read a very large XML file once, forward only, with minimal memory. Which approach fits best?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"DOM\",\n        \"JAXB\",\n        \"SAX\",\n        \"Hybrid column\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n      \"explanation\": \"SAX streams through and fires events with very little memory; it is read once, forward only, and best for very large files.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch5-q14\",\n      \"question\": \"Which approach converts XML directly into objects, best for when the schema is stable?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Data Binding (JAXB)\",\n        \"StAX\",\n        \"SAX\",\n        \"CLOB storage\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"Data Binding (JAXB) converts XML directly into objects and is best for when the schema is stable.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch5-q15\",\n      \"question\": \"The gap between the XML tree and a relational database's flat tables is called:\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"The schema drift\",\n        \"The impedance mismatch\",\n        \"The normalization gap\",\n        \"The cardinality error\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"The impedance mismatch is the gap between the XML tree structure and relational database tables.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}","filePath":"C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\sai_build\\ch5a.json"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\sai_build\\ch5a.json","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\sai_build\\ch5a.json","time":{"start":1789818272469,"end":1789818272485}},"id":"prt_0b97b53900018MTfAT019X9DII","sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","messageID":"msg_0b97b4df9001iOJH0C2beu0lxd"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1789818272517,"sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","part":{"id":"prt_0b97b532f001syiGMdN6SXjIQi","messageID":"msg_0b97b4df9001iOJH0C2beu0lxd","sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","type":"text","text":"Now Chapter 5 (L6 - Integrating Technology), which covers XML.","time":{"start":1789818262320,"end":1789818272489}}}
