{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789818299821,"sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_2f41ddbe94eb425c895f2470","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"{\n  \"chapterId\": \"ch6\",\n  \"title\": \"L7 — Integrating Applications\",\n  \"description\": \"Application integration styles, real-time vs batch integration, legacy system integration, enterprise integration architectures, and integration patterns.\",\n  \"questions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch6-q01\",\n      \"question\": \"What is the goal of Application Integration according to the reference?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"To replace every independent application with a single system\",\n        \"To create a seamless flow of information and processes across systems\",\n        \"To store all data in one shared spreadsheet\",\n        \"To make all applications use the same programming language\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"The goal is a seamless flow of information and processes across systems, reducing or eliminating manual data transfer.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch6-q02\",\n      \"question\": \"Which of the following is a reason application integration matters?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"It reduces manual data entry and human error\",\n        \"It keeps data in isolated departmental silos\",\n        \"It requires staff to copy data between systems\",\n        \"It slows down organizational decision-making\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"Integration eliminates data silos, reduces manual entry and human error, improves accuracy, and speeds up decision-making.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch6-q03\",\n      \"question\": \"A website connects directly to a payment gateway without an intermediary. Which integration style does this represent?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Hub-and-spoke integration\",\n        \"ESB integration\",\n        \"Point-to-point integration\",\n        \"API-based integration\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n      \"explanation\": \"This is point-to-point integration: a direct, custom-built connection between two systems, generally not reusable.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch6-q04\",\n      \"question\": \"What is the main limitation of point-to-point integration as more systems are added?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"It becomes difficult to manage and scale, leading to 'spaghetti' architecture\",\n        \"It stops working entirely after five systems\",\n        \"It eliminates the need for APIs\",\n        \"It requires a central integration engine\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"Point-to-point integration leads to a tangled 'spaghetti' architecture that is difficult to manage and scale as systems grow.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch6-q05\",\n      \"question\": \"In hub-and-spoke integration, how do the systems connect to each other?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Each system connects directly to every other system\",\n        \"Each system connects to a central hub that routes communication\",\n        \"Systems connect through a peer-to-peer mesh\",\n        \"Systems exchange flat files on a shared drive\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"In hub-and-spoke, each system connects to the central hub, reducing the total number of connections.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch6-q06\",\n      \"question\": \"A bank uses middleware to connect core banking, a mobile app, and an ATM network using XML and JSON. Which layer does this example illustrate?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Point-to-point integration\",\n        \"Enterprise Service Bus\",\n        \"API gateway\",\n        \"Direct database links\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"An ESB acts as a common communication backbone routing and transforming messages between systems with different formats and protocols.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch6-q07\",\n      \"question\": \"A ride-sharing app uses Google Maps, Stripe, and Twilio through well-defined interfaces. Which integration approach does this use?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"API-based / microservices integration\",\n        \"Hub-and-spoke integration\",\n        \"Batch data sync\",\n        \"Point-to-point wire transfer\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"API-based integration lets independent components communicate through well-defined APIs, as with the ride-sharing example.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch6-q08\",\n      \"question\": \"Which integration timing best suits a stock trading platform that updates balances right after every trade?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Batch integration\",\n        \"Real-time integration\",\n        \"Scheduled night sync\",\n        \"Quarterly reconciliation\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"Real-time integration syncs data immediately; stock trading updates balances right after every trade.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch6-q09\",\n      \"question\": \"A payroll system syncs attendance data overnight in large volumes. Which timing approach does this use?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Real-time integration\",\n        \"Batch integration\",\n        \"Streaming integration\",\n        \"Event-driven integration\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"Batch integration collects and transfers data at scheduled intervals, reducing processing overhead and system load.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch6-q10\",\n      \"question\": \"Which of the following is a common challenge in application integration?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Fewer points of failure as systems grow\",\n        \"Data format mismatches that require transformation\",\n        \"Decreased security risk from fewer entry points\",\n        \"Simpler monitoring across fewer systems\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"Common challenges include data format mismatches, security risks from more entry points, system downtime/dependency, and growing complexity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch6-q11\",\n      \"question\": \"Which of the following is one of the core approaches to legacy system integration?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"API Wrapping\",\n        \"Full database replacement\",\n        \"Manual double entry\",\n        \"Deployment retirement\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"The five core approaches are API Wrapping, Middleware Connectivity, ESB, iPaaS, and ETL & Data Synchronization.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch6-q12\",\n      \"question\": \"Why do businesses still keep and integrate legacy systems?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"To avoid the massive expense and risk of rebuilding core databases\",\n        \"Because legacy systems are the most scalable\",\n        \"To eliminate the need for staff training\",\n        \"Because they already run on modern APIs\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"Businesses keep legacy systems for cost savings, avoiding the huge expense and risk of rebuilding core databases.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch6-q13\",\n      \"question\": \"Which statement best contrasts legacy integration with implementing a new system?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"New systems offer lower upfront cost than integration\",\n        \"Legacy integration minimizes disruption; new systems risk downtime during transition\",\n        \"Legacy systems are more easily customizable than new ones\",\n        \"New systems require less training than legacy ones\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"Legacy integration causes minimal disruption, while new system implementation carries high risk of downtime and requires extensive training.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch6-q14\",\n      \"question\": \"Which of the following is a challenge of integrating legacy systems?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Data inconsistency due to proprietary formats\",\n        \"Fully documented modern interfaces\",\n        \"Lightweight authentication protocols\",\n        \"Consistent, well-governed schemas\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n      \"explanation\": \"Legacy databases use proprietary formats requiring complex translation logic, causing data inconsistency challenges.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ch6-q15\",\n      \"question\": \"Which incremental modernization pattern minimizes operational risk when migrating legacy systems?\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        \"Big-bang replacement\",\n        \"Strangler Fig pattern\",\n        \"Full mirror cutover\",\n        \"Parallel code forks\"\n      ],\n      \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n      \"explanation\": \"The Strangler Fig pattern is an incremental approach that minimizes operational risk during legacy migration.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}","filePath":"C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\sai_build\\ch6a.json"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\sai_build\\ch6a.json","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\sai_build\\ch6a.json","time":{"start":1789818299802,"end":1789818299818}},"id":"prt_0b97bb225001WGRezqJBrHt146","sessionID":"ses_f468c1d68ffe9hPjm84tFHrrnQ","messageID":"msg_0b97bad6d001vUyZEKKXWxAkuI"}}
